hadiths[600]{hadithnumber,text}:
"4801","4024 - And Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn Bashshar narrated to us, Abd al-Wahhab ibn Abd al-Majid al-Thaqafi narrated to us, dictating to us from his book, Muhammad ibn Amr narrated to us, from Abu Salamah and Yahya ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Hatib, from Usamah ibn Zayd, from Zayd ibn Harithah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: I went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a hot day of the days of Makkah, with him mounting me behind him, toward a stone altar of the altars, and we had slaughtered a sheep for him and cooked it. He said: Then Zayd ibn Amr ibn Nufayl met him, and each of them greeted the other with the greeting of Jahiliyyah. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Zayd, why do I see your people have come to hate you? He said: By Allah, O Muhammad, that is not for any fault of mine toward them, but I went out seeking this religion until I came to the rabbis of Fadak, and I found them worshipping Allah yet associating with Him, so I said: This is not what I seek. Then an elder of them said: You ask about a religion; we know no one who worships Allah Almighty by it except an elder in al-Hirah. So I went out until I came to him, and when he saw me he said: From where are you? I said: From the people of the House of Allah, and from the people of thorn trees and qaraz (acacia used for tanning). He said: What you seek has appeared in your land; a prophet has been sent whose star has risen, and all those you have seen are in error, but I have not yet sensed anything, O Muhammad. He said: Then he brought near to him the food mat, and he said: What is this? He said: A sheep we slaughtered for one of the altars. So he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I would never eat of what Allah's name has not been mentioned over. Zayd ibn Harithah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to the House and circumambulated it, and I was with him, and to al-Safa and al-Marwah, and at al-Safa and al-Marwah there were two idols of brass, one called Yasaf and the other Na'ilah, and when the polytheists circumambulated they would wipe over them. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not wipe over them, for they are filth. So I said to myself: I shall surely wipe over them to see what the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) will say, so I wiped over them, and he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Zayd, were you not forbidden? And Zayd ibn Amr died, and revelation was sent down to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Zayd: He will be raised alone as a nation."
"4802","Chapter 49: The merit of Abu Talhah (may Allah be pleased with him). 4025 - al-Harith said: Qabisah narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Jabir ibn Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with them both) - or Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The voice of Abu Talhah (may Allah be pleased with him) in the army is better than a thousand men."
"4803","4026/1 - Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Thabit, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: Abu Talhah (may Allah be pleased with him) recited Surah Bara'ah (Repentance), and came to this verse: {March forth, light and heavy}, and said: Do I not see my Lord mobilizing me, young and old? Equip me. So his sons said to him: You raided with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until he passed away, and with Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) until he died, and with Umar (may Allah be pleased with him), so we will raid on your behalf. He said: Equip me. So they equipped him, and he rode the sea until he died, and they found no island on which to bury him except after seven days, and he had not changed (decayed)."
"4804","4026/2 - And Abu Ya'la said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Sallam narrated to us, Hammad narrated to us with it. Ibn Hibban authenticated it."
"4805","Chapter 50: The merit of Sa'd ibn Mu'adh (may Allah be pleased with him). A hadith for him has preceded in Funerals. And a hadith in the military expeditions concerning his judgment on Banu Qurayzah. 4027/1 - And Abu Bakr said: Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to us, from Ata' ibn al-Sa'ib, from Mujahid, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: The Throne shook out of Allah Almighty's love for meeting Sa'd (may Allah be pleased with him). Then he said: He means only the bier; Allah Almighty said: {And he raised his parents upon the throne}, he said: its wood planks split apart. He said: And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered his grave and was detained, and when he came out it was said: O Messenger of Allah, what detained you? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Sa'd (may Allah be pleased with him) was squeezed in the grave with a squeezing, so I supplicated to Allah Almighty to relieve it from him."
"4806","4027/2 - And al-Bazzar said: Ismail ibn Hafs narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated to us with it, and said: This hadith with this explanation we know only from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both)."
"4807","Chapter 52: The merit of Amir ibn al-Akwa' (may Allah be pleased with him). 4028 - Musaddad said: Abdullah ibn Dawud narrated to us, from al-Rabi', from Majza'ah ibn Zahir, he said: Amir ibn al-Akwa' (may Allah be pleased with him) dueled a man and killed him, and wounded himself. He said: That was mentioned to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: For him are two rewards."
"4808","4029 - And Abu Bakr said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Asadi narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Bishr al-Aslami narrated to us, Iyas ibn Salamah ibn al-Akwa' narrated to us, he said: His uncle was wounded on the day of Khaybar, and killed a man, so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: For you are two rewards."
"4809","Chapter 53: The merit of Suhayb (may Allah be pleased with him). 4030 - Ishaq said: al-Nadr ibn Shumayl, and Rawh ibn Ubadah, and Abu Salamah informed us, they said: Awf ibn Abi Jamilah al-A'rabi narrated to us, from Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, he said: When Suhayb wanted to emigrate to Madinah, the disbelievers of Quraysh said to him: You came to us poor, then your wealth grew with us and you reached what you reached, then you want to leave with yourself and your wealth! By Allah, that shall not be. So he said to them: What if I give you my wealth, will you let me go my way? They said: Yes. So he said: I call you to witness that I have made my wealth for you. That reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Suhayb has profited, Suhayb has profited. This is a sound hadith, if Abu Uthman heard it from Suhayb; and Ja'far ibn Sulayman al-Duba'i narrated it from Awf, from Abu Uthman, from Suhayb (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: When I wanted... and he mentioned the like of it. So its continuity became sound, and praise be to Allah. Ibn Mardawayh narrated it in the Tafsir as a musnad hadith from Ja'far. And it has a witness from the hadith of Ali ibn Zayd ibn Jud'an, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from Suhayb (may Allah be pleased with him) - which has preceded in the Tafsir of al-Baqarah."
"4810","4031 - And Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr al-Muqaddami narrated to us, Daffa' ibn Daghfal narrated to us, al-Nu'man ibn Abdullah ibn Jabir ibn Abdullah al-Ansari narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said to Suhayb: O Suhayb, in you are three traits I dislike for you. He (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Your feeding food while you have no wealth, your taking a kunyah while you have no child, and your claiming descent to the Arabs while in your tongue is an impediment (luknah). He (may Allah be pleased with him) said: As for what you mentioned of food, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best of you is he who feeds food, and by Allah I shall never abandon feeding food. And he mentioned the kunyah, he said: Upon it I live and upon it I die. And he mentioned the claim of descent, he said: I am Suhayb ibn Sinan, and he traced his lineage to al-Namir ibn Qasit; I used to graze for my family, and the Romans raided and enslaved me, and taught me their language, so that is what you see of my impediment. I say: This is a strange chain, and Ahmad narrated from the path of Hamzah ibn Suhayb, he said: Suhayb... and he mentioned the like of it. And this wording is fuller. And in al-Bukhari is part of it. And in Ibn Majah is another part. And I only narrated it for the strangeness of its chain and the completeness of its wording."
"4811","Chapter 54: The merit of al-Nabighah al-Ja'di (may Allah be pleased with him). 4032 - al-Harith said: al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abdullah narrated to us, al-Hasan ibn Ubaydullah narrated to me, one who heard al-Nabighah narrated to me, saying: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and recited to him my verse... He said: Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: To where? I said: To Paradise. He said: Yes, if Allah Almighty wills. He (may Allah be pleased with him) said: When I recited to him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)... the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: May Allah never break your mouth. He said: So he (may Allah be pleased with him) was of the people with the finest teeth, and whenever a tooth of his fell, another grew."
"4812","Chapter 55: The merit of the paralyzed man who died during his lifetime (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 4033/1 - Abd said: Abu Jabir narrated to us. 4033/2 - And al-Harith said: Abd al-Wahhab narrated to us, both from Fa'id, from Abdullah ibn Abi Awfa (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: There was in Madinah a paralyzed man, and he said to his family: Place me on the road of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to his mosque. He said: So the paralyzed man was placed on the road of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and whenever he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went to the mosque he would greet the paralyzed man. Then the family of the paralyzed man came to take him back, and he said: No, by Allah, I shall not move from this place as long as the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) lives; so build for me a hut of palm branches (khuss). So they built for him a hut, and he stayed in it, and whenever the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed to the mosque he would enter the hut and greet the paralyzed man, and whenever he received a bit of food, he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would send some to the paralyzed man. He said: While we were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), a comer came and announced to him the death of the paralyzed man. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rose, and we rose with him, until when he drew near the hut, he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to his Companions: Let no one approach the hut but me. So he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) drew near the hut, and behold Jibril (peace and blessings be upon him) was sitting at the paralyzed man. He said: O Messenger of Allah, had you not come to us we would have sufficed you his affair, but since you have come, you are most entitled to him. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood to him and washed him with his hand, shrouded him, prayed over him, and placed him in the grave. Fa'id Abu al-Warqa' is alone in it, and he is weak."
"4813","Chapter 56: The merit of Ibn Umm Maktum (may Allah be pleased with him). 4034 - al-Harith said: Yunus ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Shayban narrated to us, from Qatadah, from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: I saw Ibn Umm Maktum on the day of al-Qadisiyyah, wearing armor and holding a banner in his hand."
"4814","Chapter 58: The merit of Ja'far ibn Abi Talib, and Zayd ibn Harithah, may Allah be pleased with them. The hadith of Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) concerning the daughter of Hamzah (may Allah be pleased with them both) has preceded in Custody. 4035 - And Abu Ya'la said: Wahb, and he is Ibn Baqiyyah, narrated to us, Khalid, and he is Ibn Abdullah, narrated to us, from Husayn, meaning Ibn Qays, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) performed Umrah, and there was between him and the people of Makkah an agreement that he would not take out anyone of its people. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) completed his Umrah he left Makkah, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed by the daughter of Hamzah ibn Abd al-Muttalib (may Allah be pleased with them both), and she said: O Messenger of Allah, to whom do you leave me? But he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not turn to her because of the covenant between him and the people of Makkah. And Zayd ibn Harithah (may Allah be pleased with him) passed by her, and she said: To whom do you leave me? But he did not turn to her. And Ja'far (may Allah be pleased with him) passed by her and she pleaded with him, but he did not turn to her. Then Ali ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him) passed by her, and she said: O Abu al-Hasan, to whom do you leave me? So Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) took her and placed her behind Fatimah (may Allah be pleased with her). When they stopped at the nearest stopping place, Zayd came to Ali (may Allah be pleased with him) and said: We are more entitled to her than you... and he mentioned the rest of the hadith. And it is with Ahmad from the path of Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both)."
"4815","4036 - And Musaddad said: Hammad narrated to us, from Asim ibn Bahdalah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Ja'far has been afflicted (martyred), and I used to love Ja'far (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"4816","4037 - Abdullah narrated to us, from Abu Ma'shar, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: What was counted, as was said, upon Ja'far (may Allah be pleased with him) was ninety wounds, between a strike with a sword and a stab with a spear. Its origin is in the Sahih."
"4817","4038 - And Abu Ya'la said: Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Ismail ibn Mujalid narrated to us, from his father, from Amir, from Jabir (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: When Ja'far (may Allah be pleased with him) came from Abyssinia, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) embraced him."
"4818","4039 - And al-Humaydi said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from al-Sha'bi, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) never sent a detachment in which Zayd ibn Harithah (may Allah be pleased with him) was present except that he put him in command over them. Abridged."
"4819","4040 - And Abu Ya'la said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Salih al-Azdi narrated to us, Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Ishaq, from his father, from al-Bara', from Zayd ibn Harithah (may Allah be pleased with them), that he said: O Messenger of Allah, you established brotherhood between me and Hamzah ibn Abd al-Muttalib (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"4820","Chapter 59: The merit of Abu Umamah (may Allah be pleased with him). 4041 - Abu Ya'la said: Abdullah ibn Salamah al-Basri narrated to us, Sadaqah ibn Hurmuz al-Qasmali narrated to us, from Abu Ghalib, from Abu Umamah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me to my people, and I reached them while I was hungry, and I reached them while they were eating blood. So they said: Come. I said: I have come to forbid you from this. Then I slept while overcome, and a comer came to me in my dream with a vessel in which was drink, and said: Take. So I took it and drank, and I was satisfied and quenched. Then a man of the people said: A man of the nobles of your people has come to you and you did not present him a little milk mixed with water. He said: So they brought me their mixed milk, and I said: I have no need of it. They said: We saw you in hardship. So I showed them my belly, and they accepted Islam, all of them, may Allah be pleased with them."
"4821","4042 - And Ahmad ibn Mani' said: al-Haytham narrated to us, al-Walid narrated to us, from Ibn Jabir, he said: Salim ibn Amir narrated to me, he said: I said to Abu Umamah (may Allah be pleased with him): How old were you at the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: None asked me about it before you; I was thirty-three years old, and you would have seen me attending a sermon, and he would incline with the chest of his mount so that it almost removed me from hearing, so I would place my shoulder against the chest of his mount and push it away."
"4822","Chapter 60: The merit of Abdullah ibn Qays al-Ansari (may Allah be pleased with him). 4043 - Abd said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Salim ibn Ubayd informed us, from Abu Abdullah, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, he said: He heard Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There is no man on earth who dies while in his heart is pride equal to the weight of a mustard seed but that Allah Almighty will place him in the Fire. When Abdullah ibn Qays al-Ansari (may Allah be pleased with him) heard that, he wept. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Abdullah ibn Qays, why do you weep? He said: Because of your words. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Rejoice, for you are in Paradise. He said: Then the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent an expedition and he raided, and he was killed among them as a martyr... the hadith. And the rest of it has preceded in Manners (Adab), in the censure of pride."
"4823","Chapter 61: The merit of Abdullah ibn Ja'far ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with them both). 4044/1 - Abu Bakr said: Waki' narrated to us, from Mis'ar, from al-Walid ibn Sari', from Fitr ibn Khalifah, from his father, from Amr ibn Hurayth, he said: Then he passed - meaning the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - by Abdullah ibn Ja'far (may Allah be pleased with them both) while he was playing with something he was selling, and he was a boy, so he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, bless for him in his trade."
"4824","4044/2 - Ibn Numayr narrated to us, from Fitr, the like of it. Its chain is hasan, upon the condition of Abu Dawud. With this chain he narrated the beginning of this hadith, and did not mention what I have cited."
"4825","4044/3 - And Abu Ya'la said: Abu Sa'id narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Dawud narrated to us, from Fitr, the like of it, and he said: May Allah Almighty bless for him in his sale - or he said: in his transaction."
"4826","4045 - And Musaddad said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Hassan, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, he said: A man brought sugar to Madinah and it became unsold with him, so they said to him: Go to Abdullah ibn Ja'far (may Allah be pleased with them both). So he went to him and he bought it from him: Take and increase, and he said: Whoever wishes may take. So the man said: Shall I take with them? He said: Take."
"4827","Chapter 62: The merit of Abu al-Dahdah (may Allah be pleased with him). 4046 - Abu Ya'la said: Muhriz ibn Awn narrated to us, Khalaf ibn Khalifah narrated to us, from Humayd al-A'raj, from Abdullah ibn al-Harith, from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: When {Who is it that will lend Allah a good loan}... the verse was revealed, Abu al-Dahdah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: O Messenger of Allah, does Allah Almighty want a loan from us? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes, O Abu al-Dahdah. He said: Show me your hand. So he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) extended his hand to him. He said: I have lent my Lord my garden, and in his garden were six hundred palm trees. Then he (may Allah be pleased with him) came walking until he reached the garden, while Umm al-Dahdah was in it with her children, and he called: O Umm al-Dahdah. She said: At your service. He said: Come out, for I have lent my Lord. Humayd is weak."
"4828","Chapter 63: The merit of Abu Sufyan Sakhr ibn Harb (may Allah be pleased with him). 4047/1 - Ishaq said: I said to Abu Usamah: Did Mis'ar narrate to you, from Sa'd ibn Ibrahim, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, from one who narrated to him, that he never heard a voice louder than his voice - meaning Abu Sufyan on the day of Yarmuk - while he was under his son's banner, saying: This is a day of the days of Allah Almighty; O Allah, send down Your help. 4047/2 - He said: Mis'ar said another time in this hadith: One who heard Abu Sufyan on the day of Yarmuk narrated to us... and he mentioned the like of it, so Abu Usamah acknowledged it."
"4829","Chapter 64: The merit of Amr ibn al-As (may Allah be pleased with him). 4048 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Yazid, and he is Ibn Harun, narrated to us, al-Aswad ibn Shayban narrated to us, from Abu Nawfal al-Urayji, he said: When Amr ibn al-As (may Allah be pleased with him) was on his deathbed, he was extremely distressed and began to weep. So his son said to him: Why are you distressed, when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to employ you and draw you near? He said: He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to do that, but I do not know whether that was out of love for me or to conciliate me. But I testify concerning two men: the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away while loving them: Ibn Sumayyah, meaning Ammar, and Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with them both). When death struggles intensified with him - meaning the pangs - he joined his hands and placed them where the shackle would be on his neck, and began to say: O Allah, You commanded us and we neglected, and You forbade us and we committed, so nothing can encompass us but Your mercy. He said: That remained his constant utterance until he passed away. Chapter 65: The chapter of Yasar (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"4830","4049 - al-Tayalisi said: Jisr ibn Farqad narrated to us, Salit ibn Abdullah ibn Yasar al-Ansari (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to us, he said: The grandfather of my father pledged allegiance to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4831","Chapter 66: The merit of Harithah ibn al-Nu'man (may Allah be pleased with him). 4050/1 - al-Harith said: al-Hasan ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, al-Mas'udi narrated to us, from al-Hakam, from al-Qasim, he said: al-Harith ibn al-Nu'man al-Ansari (may Allah be pleased with him) came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was conversing privately with Jibril (peace and blessings be upon him), and he sat and did not greet. So Jibril (peace be upon him) said: Had this one greeted us, we would have returned it upon him. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do you know him? He said: Yes, he is of the eighty who stood firm with you on the day of Hunayn; their provision and the provision of their children are upon Allah - the Almighty - in Paradise. Al-Hasan ibn Qutaybah said so, and he is weak."
"4832","4050/2 - And Bishr ibn al-Mufaddal differed with him, and said from al-Mas'udi with this chain: Harithah ibn al-Nu'man (may Allah be pleased with him) came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was conversing privately with Jibril (peace and blessings be upon him)... and he mentioned it. Al-Tabarani narrated it from the path of Ibn Abi Layla, from al-Hakam, and differed in its chain, saying: from Miqsam, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both)... and he mentioned the like of it. And he differed in the text, saying: The people flee from you except eighty who stand firm with you, and he is of them. The first made it news of what passed, and the second made it news of what is to come, and Allah knows best."
"4833","Chapter 67: The merit of Mu'awiyah (may Allah be pleased with him). 4051 - Abu Bakr said: Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Muhajir, he said: I heard Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr saying: Mu'awiyah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I never ceased hoping for the caliphate since the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said what he said: O Mu'awiyah, if you rule, then do good."
"4834","Chapter 68: The merit of Bashir ibn al-Khasasiyah (may Allah be pleased with him). 4052 - Abu Ya'la said: Abu Kurayb narrated to us, Waki' narrated to us, from Abu Janab, from Iyad ibn Laqit, from al-Jahdamah the wife of Bashir ibn al-Khasasiyah, from Bashir ibn al-Khasasiyah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: From whom are you? I said: From Rabi'ah. He said: From Rabi'ah of the horses, those who say were it not for them the earth would shift with its people; I praise Allah who has favored you above Rabi'ah."
"4835","Chapter 69: The merit of Amr ibn al-Hamiq al-Khuza'i (may Allah be pleased with him). 4053 - Abu Bakr said: al-Mu'alla ibn Mansur narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Hamzah, from Ishaq ibn Abi Farwah, from Yusuf ibn Sulayman, from his grandmother, from Amr ibn al-Hamiq (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: I gave the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) milk to drink, and he said: May Allah grant him enjoyment of his youth."
"4836","Chapter 70: The merit of Aqil ibn Abi Talib (may Allah be pleased with him). 4054 - Ishaq said: Ahmad ibn Ayyub informed us, from Abu Hamzah, from Jabir, and he is al-Ju'fi, from Muhammad ibn Aqil, he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to Aqil (may Allah be pleased with him): O Abu Yazid, I love you with two loves: the love of kinship, and a love for the love of Abu Talib for you. This is a weak chain."
"4837","Chapter 72: The merit of Amr ibn Hurayth (may Allah be pleased with him). 4055/1 - Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, Yahya ibn Yaman narrated to us, Ismail narrated to us, he said: I heard Amr ibn Hurayth (may Allah be pleased with him) saying: My mother took me to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he wiped my head and supplicated for me for provision."
"4838","4055/2 - Wahb, and he is Ibn Baqiyyah, narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yazid al-Wasiti narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from the freedman of Amr ibn Hurayth, from Amr ibn Hurayth (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: My father and my mother took me... the hadith."
"4839","Chapter 73: The merit of Hudhayfah (may Allah be pleased with him). 4056/1 - Ibn Abi Umar said: al-Muqri narrated to us, al-Mas'udi narrated to us, from Ma'mar ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: I prayed beside a man and began supplicating while holding a pebble, so he turned to me and said: O Abu Abdullah, Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) used to say: When you ask your Lord, do not hold the stone in your hand. When I heard him mention Abdullah, I felt at ease with him and introduced myself to him, and he began narrating to me, saying: Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) sought permission from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he gave him permission and gave him glad tidings of Paradise. Then Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) came, and he gave him permission and gave him glad tidings of Paradise. Then Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) came, and he gave him permission and gave him glad tidings. Then another man came, whom I could name if I wished, and he gave him permission and gave him glad tidings of Paradise, while Hudhayfah (may Allah be pleased with him) was sitting. So Hudhayfah (may Allah be pleased with him) said: And where am I, O Messenger of Allah? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You are in good, or toward good."
"4840","4056/2 - And with it, to Ma'mar ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: I prayed beside a man, and he narrated to me that Hudhayfah (may Allah be pleased with him) was sitting with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: Where am I?... and he mentioned it abridged from the first."
"4841","Chapter 74: The merit of Rafi' ibn Khadij (may Allah be pleased with him). 4057 - al-Tayalisi said: Amr ibn Marzuq narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid al-Ansari informed me, my grandmother narrated to me, from Rafi' ibn Khadij (may Allah be pleased with him): An arrow struck him alongside the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in one of his battles, so he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Rafi', if you wish, I shall lift out the arrow and leave the head, and I shall testify for you on the Day of Resurrection that you are a martyr? He said: So he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did."
"4842","Chapter 75: The merit of Anas (may Allah be pleased with him). 4058/1 - al-Tayalisi said: al-Hakam ibn Atiyyah narrated to us, from Thabit, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: I hope to meet the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and say: O Messenger of Allah, your little servant Anas."
"4843","4058/2 - And Abu Ya'la said: Harun narrated to us, Abu Dawud narrated to us with this."
"4844","4059 - Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr, the brother of al-Muqaddami, narrated to us, Ja'far narrated to us, Thabit narrated to us, he said: Whenever I came to Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), he would be informed of my place, and I would enter upon him and take his hands and kiss them, saying: May my father be ransom for these two hands which touched the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); and I would kiss his eyes, saying: May my father be ransom for these two eyes which saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4845","Chapter 76: The merit of Safinah (may Allah be pleased with him). 4060/1 - Abu Ya'la said: Abd al-A'la narrated to us, Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, Usamah ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Safinah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: I rode the sea in a ship and it broke with us, so I rode a plank of it, and it cast me into a thicket in which was a lion, and nothing startled me but it. So I said: O Abu al-Harith, I am Safinah, the freedman of the Messenger of Allah. He said: So he struck me with his shoulder and lowered his head, and kept nudging me with his shoulder, then walked with me until he set me upon the road, then struck me with his hand and hummed for a while, and I saw that he was bidding me farewell."
"4846","4060/2 - And al-Bazzar said: Muhammad ibn Bashshar narrated to us, Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, with it."
"4847","Chapter 77: The merit of Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him). He (may Allah be pleased with him) is mentioned in the biography of Hudhayfah (may Allah be pleased with him). 4061 - And Ishaq said: I said to Abu Usamah: Did Abu Talq ibn Hanzalah narrate to you - my father narrated to me - from Aws ibn Thurayb... and he mentioned a hadith. He said: So Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) struck between the shoulders of Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) and said: Allah Almighty has placed in your heart, O Ibn Mas'ud, no little knowledge. He said: So Abu Usamah acknowledged it. And the hadith in full is mentioned in the treatment of women."
"4848","4062 - And Ibn Abi Umar said: al-Muqri narrated to us, al-Mas'udi narrated to us, from al-Qasim, he said: The first to spread the Qur'an openly in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in Makkah was Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"4849","4063 - al-Muqri narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Abi Ayyub narrated to us, al-Nu'man ibn Amr ibn Khalid al-Lakhmi narrated to us, from Ulayy ibn Rabah, from Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: He entered the mosque and came to a pillar and stood at it praying. He said: And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in the mosque, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Our leader, Ibn Mas'ud, while he did not hear him. So he recited: {Say: O disbelievers}, then bowed and prostrated, then stood for the second rak'ah. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Be sincere, O Ibn Mas'ud. So he recited: {Say: He is Allah, the One}, then bowed and prostrated and sat. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Supplicate, O Ibn Mas'ud, you will be answered, and ask, you will be given. And he (may Allah be pleased with him) all the while did not hear him. So Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) said: O Allah, I ask You for the Highest Companion, and the fullest share of the Gardens of Bliss, and I ask You for guidance, piety, chastity, wealth of contentment, glad tidings at the severance of the world, and I ask You for faith that does not turn back, coolness of eye that does not perish, joy that does not cease, success in praise, the garment of piety, the adornment of faith, and the companionship of Your Prophet Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the highest eternal Paradise. He said: So a man went and gave him glad tidings."
"4850","4064 - And Musaddad said: Abdullah, and he is Ibn al-Mubarak, narrated to us, from al-Mas'udi, from Sulayman ibn Mina, from Nufay' the freedman of Abdullah, he said: Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him) was of the people finest in white garment, and best in scent. And the hadith of Amr ibn al-As (may Allah be pleased with him) in his biography. And the hadith of al-Qasim (may Allah be pleased with him): When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat, Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him) would remove his sandals from his feet. In the Book of Manners."
"4851","4065 - And Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Mu'awiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, from Za'idah, from al-A'mash, from Malik ibn al-Harith, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Uqbah ibn Amr, he said: I do not see any man more knowledgeable of what was revealed to Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) than Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him). So Abu Musa (may Allah be pleased with him) said: If you have said that, he would hear when none could hear, and enter where we could not enter, may Allah be pleased with him."
"4852","4066 - And Ibn Abi Umar said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Abu Umays, from al-Qasim ibn Abd al-Rahman, he said: Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) spoke before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), praising Allah and extolling Him, then said: I am pleased with Allah as Lord, with Islam as religion, and with Muhammad as Messenger. Then he (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I am pleased for you with what Allah and His Messenger are pleased with, and I dislike for you what Allah and His Messenger dislike. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I am pleased for my nation with what Ibn Umm Abd is pleased with for it."
"4853","4067/1 - And al-Tayalisi said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Mu'awiyah ibn Qurrah, he said: Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) went to bring the tooth-stick (siwak) to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and they began looking at the thinness of his leg in amazement. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: They are heavier on the Scale than Uhud."
"4854","4067/2 - Al-Bazzar narrated it from the path of Sahl ibn Hammad, from Shu'bah, from Mu'awiyah ibn Qurrah, from his father, and said: Sahl is alone in it."
"4855","4068 - And al-Harith said: Abd al-Aziz ibn Aban narrated to us, al-Mas'udi narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr, from Abu al-Malih, from Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: I used to screen the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he bathed, wake him when he slept, and walk with him on the earth... the hadith."
"4856","Chapter 78: The merit of Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both). 4069 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Yazid, and he is Ibn Harun, narrated to us, Jarir, and he is Ibn Hazim, narrated to us, from Ya'la, and he is Ibn Hakim, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away, I said to a man of the Ansar: Come, let us ask the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), for they are many today. He said: How strange of you, O Ibn Abbas, do you think the people will need you when among the people are Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) such as are among them?! He said: So I left that, and began asking the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) about hadith. If word of a man reached me, I would come to him while he was napping, and I would lay my cloak as a pillow at his door, the wind blowing dust upon me. Then he would come out and see me, and say: O cousin of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), what brought you? Did you not send to me so that I come to you? So I would say: No, I am more entitled to come to you. Then I would ask him about hadith. That Ansari man lived until he saw me with the people gathered around me asking me, and said: This youth was more intelligent than I."
"4857","4070 - And Musaddad said: Ibn Dawud narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abd al-Malik ibn Maysarah, from Tawus, he said: I sat with seventy - or fifty - elders of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and none of them differed with Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) and met him but said: It is as you said. Or he said: You have spoken the truth. Sound (sahih)."
"4858","4071 - And Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Qutaybah narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from Tawus, he said: I never saw one more knowledgeable than Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), nor more pious than Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both)."
"4859","4072 - Ismail narrated to us, Ayyub narrated to us, he said: I was informed from Tawus, he said: I never saw anyone with greater veneration for the sanctities of Allah Almighty than Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both); by Allah, if I wished when I mention him to weep, I would weep."
"4860","4073 - Ishaq said: Waki' informed us, Mis'ar narrated to us, from Abd al-Malik ibn Maysarah, from Tawus, he said: I never saw anyone differ with Ibn Abbas until he made him affirm it. The hadith has preceded in Hajj."
"4861","Chapter 79: The virtues of Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him). 4074/1 - Ishaq said: Wahb ibn Jarir ibn Hazim informed us, my father narrated to me, he said: I heard Muhammad ibn Ishaq saying: Buraydah ibn Sufyan narrated to me, from al-Qurazi, he said: Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him) went out to al-Rabadhah, and his decreed time befell him, so he enjoined them: Wash me, shroud me, then place me on the roadside; the first riders who pass by you, say to them: This is Abu Dharr, the Companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so help us in his washing and burial. So they did. Then Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) approached in a party from Iraq, with the bier placed on the roadside. A boy stood to him and said: This is Abu Dharr, the Companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: So Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) wept and said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: You walk alone, and you die alone, and you will be raised alone. Al-Qurazi - I do not know him; if he is Muhammad ibn Ka'b, then the hadith is disconnected. 4074/2 - And Ahmad narrated it from the path of Ibrahim ibn al-Ashtar, from his father, from Umm Dharr, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with them) - meaning this."
"4862","4074/3 - al-Mughirah ibn Salamah al-Makhzumi informed us, Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, Asim ibn Kulayb narrated to us, Salamah ibn Nubatah narrated to us, he said: We went out as pilgrims (ummar) and headed to the dwelling of Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), and behold he had come carrying - or being carried with him - the bone of a slaughtered camel. He came to his dwelling, then came to us and greeted us... and he mentioned the hadith. He said to them: For every so-and-so camels they slaughter and eat them, and for me in every camel is a bone. A man said: O Abu Dharr, what wealth do you have? He (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I have a herd of camels, and a flock of sheep; in one of them is my son, and in the other a black boy I bought, and he is free - he serves me until the year then he is free. He said: A man said: O Abu Dharr, by Allah, there is none of the people with us who has more wealth than your companions. He said: By Allah, they have no right in wealth but I have the like of it. He said: So we began seeking his legal opinions, and a man said: O Abu Dharr, with us is a man who fasts the whole year except al-Adha and al-Fitr? He (may Allah be pleased with him) said: He has neither fasted nor broken fast. He said: He does this and that. He said: So he repeated it... the hadith."
"4863","4075 - And Abu Bakr and Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Abu Umayyah ibn Ya'la narrated to us, from Abu al-Zinad, from al-A'raj, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Neither the green (sky) has shaded, nor the dusty (earth) has carried, one with a dialect more truthful than Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him). Whoever is pleased to look at the humility of Isa son of Maryam (peace be upon him), let him look at Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"4864","4076 - And al-Harith said: Abdullah ibn al-Rumi narrated to us, al-Nadr ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Ikrimah narrated to us, Abu Zumayl narrated to us, from Malik ibn Marthad, from his father, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: I was the fourth of four in Islam; three accepted Islam before me and I was the fourth. So I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: Peace be upon you, O Prophet of Allah; I testify that there is no god but Allah alone, with no partner, and I testify that Muhammad is His servant and His Messenger. He said: So I saw joy on his face (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Who are you? I said: Jundub, a man of Banu Ghifar."
"4865","4077 - And with it, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: O Abu Dharr, have you seen that I was weighed against forty, you being among them, and I outweighed them? His wife said to him: It is as if you have been disgraced. He said: Be silent; may Allah fill your mouth with dust."
"4866","4078 - And Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Amr informed us, from Abu Amr ibn Himas, from Malik ibn Aws, he said: I used to hear of Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), and none was more beloved to me to see and meet than he. Then Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) wrote to him to come to him, and Mu'awiyah wrote to him: If you have any need of al-Sham and its people, then expel Abu Dharr, for the people have become heavy upon me. So Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him) came, and the people shouted: This is Abu Dharr, this is Abu Dharr. So I went out to look at him among those who looked. He entered the mosque while Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) was in it, and came to a pillar and prayed two rak'ahs at it, then came to Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) and greeted him, and he neither insulted him nor blamed him. So Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Where were you on the day the milch camels of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were raided? He said: I was at the well, drawing water. Then Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him) raised his loudest voice and recited: {And those who hoard gold and silver and do not spend them}... to His saying: {what you used to hoard}. So Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) ordered him to go out to al-Rabadhah."
"4867","4079 - And Ahmad said in al-Zuhd: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Amr informed us, he said: I heard Irak ibn Malik saying: Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him) said: I am the nearest of you in assembly to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the Day of Resurrection: whoever leaves the world in the state he left it in; by Allah, there is none of you but has clung to something of it other than I."
"4868","4080 - And al-Harith said: Dawud ibn Rashid narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Harb narrated to us, from Safwan, from Abu al-Muthanna al-Mulayki, he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out to his Companions (may Allah be pleased with them), he would say: Uwaymir is the wise one of my nation, and Jundub is the outcast of my nation; he lives alone, dies alone, and Allah - the Almighty - will raise him alone."
"4869","4081 - And Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Sufyan, al-A'mash narrated to me, from Mujahid, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Layla, from Abu Dharr (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: I wish I were a tree that is cut down."
"4870","Chapter 80: The virtues of Thabit ibn Qays ibn Shammas (may Allah be pleased with him). 4082 - Abu Ya'la said: Ahmad ibn Isa narrated to us, Ibn Bakr narrated to us, Ibn Jabir narrated to us, Ata' al-Khurasani narrated to me, he said: I came to Madinah, and a man took me in to the daughter of Thabit ibn Qays ibn Shammas (may Allah be pleased with them both), and she narrated to me the story of Thabit... and he mentioned the hadith... She said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Rather you shall live praiseworthily, be killed as a martyr, and Allah shall admit you to Paradise. When the day of Yamamah came, he went out with Khalid ibn al-Walid (may Allah be pleased with him) to Musaylamah the liar. He said: When he met the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Musaylamah's men charged them, they were uncovered. He said: He said to Salim the freedman of Abu Hudhayfah (may Allah be pleased with him): This is not how we used to fight alongside the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then each of them dug a pit, and the people charged them, and they kept fighting until they were killed, may Allah's mercy be upon them. And upon Thabit (may Allah be pleased with him) was a precious coat of mail of his, and a man of the Muslims passed by him and took it. While a man of the Muslims was sleeping, Thabit ibn Qays (may Allah be pleased with him) came to him in his dream and said: I enjoin upon you a bequest; beware of saying: This is a dream, and neglecting it. When I was killed yesterday, a man of the Muslims passed by me and took my coat of mail; his dwelling is at the far end of the camp, and at his tent is a horse rearing in its tether, and he has covered the coat of mail with a cooking pot, and placed a saddle upon the pot. So go to Khalid ibn al-Walid (may Allah be pleased with him) and order him to send to my coat of mail and take it. And when you come to the Caliph of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), inform him that I owe such-and-such debts, and I am owed such-and-such debts, and so-and-so of my slaves is freed, and so-and-so; and beware of saying: This is a dream, and neglecting it. So the man came to Khalid ibn al-Walid (may Allah be pleased with him) and informed him, and he sent for the coat of mail. He looked at a tent at the far end of the camp, and behold at it was a horse rearing in its tether. He looked in the tent, and behold there was no one in it. So they entered and lifted the saddle, and behold beneath it was a cooking pot. So they lifted it, and behold the coat of mail was beneath it. So it was brought to Khalid ibn al-Walid (may Allah be pleased with him). When he came to Madinah, the man narrated his dream to Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him), and he carried out his bequest after his death. And we know of no one of the Muslims whose bequest was carried out after his death other than Thabit ibn Qays ibn Shammas (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"4871","Chapter 81: The virtues of Abdullah ibn Salam (may Allah be pleased with him). 4083 - Ishaq said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi informed us, Abu Ma'shar al-Madani narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Ka'b al-Qurazi (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The first to enter through the door of the mosque will be a man of the people of Paradise. Then Abdullah ibn Salam (may Allah be pleased with him) entered. A man said to him: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said such-and-such; which deed of yours is most reliable that you hope by? He said: My deed is weak, and the most reliable deed I hope by is soundness of my heart and abandoning what does not concern me. This is a weak hadith, and disconnected too. And its origin is in the Sahih from the narration of Qays ibn Ubad and Kharashah ibn al-Hurr, from Abdullah ibn Salam (may Allah be pleased with him), connected, without what is at its end of the question. And the hadith of Jundub (may Allah be pleased with him) from Ubayy ibn Ka'b (may Allah be pleased with him) - halted (mawquf) - has preceded in Knowledge. And the mursal hadith of al-Hasan in the Tafsir of al-Ahqaf."
"4872","Chapter 82: The virtues of Hanzalah ibn Hidhaym (may Allah be pleased with him). 4084 - Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Uthman narrated to us, Ibn Hanzalah narrated to us, he said: I heard my grandfather Hanzalah saying: My father Hanifah ibn Hidhaym said: O Messenger of Allah, I am a man with sons, and this is the most special of my sons, so invoke blessing upon him. So he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O boy - taking my hand and wiping my head - may Allah bless you. He said: I saw Hanzalah ibn Hidhaym (may Allah be pleased with him) being brought a swollen person, and he would place his hand upon him and say: In the name of Allah, and the swelling would go away."
"4873","Chapter 83: The merit of Abu Ka'b al-Harithi (may Allah be pleased with him). 4085 - Ishaq said: Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Ma'mar narrated to us, from Ziyad ibn Jabal, from Abu Ka'b al-Harithi, he is Dhu al-Idawah, he said: I heard him saying: I went out seeking stray camels of mine, and I provisioned milk in a water-skin (idawah). Then I said to myself: I have not been fair; where is ablution? So I poured out the milk and filled it with water, and said: This is ablution, and this is drink. I would seek my camels; when I wanted to perform ablution I would pour water from the skin and perform ablution, and when I wanted to drink I would pour milk and drink it. I remained so for three days. Asma' al-Bahraniyyah said to him: O Abu Ka'b, was it dried porridge or milk? He said: You are indeed idle; rather it preserved from hunger and quenched thirst. I narrated this hadith to a group of my people among whom was Ali ibn al-Harith, the chief of Banu Qiyan. He said: I do not think what you say is as you said. I said: Allah knows best of that. Then I returned to my dwelling and slept that night, and behold he was at my door at dawn prayer. I said: May Allah have mercy on you, why did you trouble yourself to me? Did you not send to me so that I come to you? He said: I am more entitled to come to you. I did not sleep the night but that a comer came to me and said: Are you the one who belies one who narrates the favors of Allah Almighty? Then I went out until I came to Madinah, and came to Uthman ibn Affan (may Allah be pleased with him) and asked him about something of my religion. I said: O Commander of the Believers, I am a man of Banu al-Harith of the people of Yemen; I want to ask you about things, so order your chamberlain not to veil me. He (may Allah be pleased with him) said: O Waththab, when the Harithi comes, admit him. Whenever I came and knocked on the door, he said: Who is there? I said: The Harithi. He said: Enter. So I entered, and behold Uthman (may Allah be pleased with him) was sitting with a group around him, silent, not speaking, as if birds were upon their heads. So I greeted, then sat, and did not ask him about anything because of what I saw of their state... and he mentioned the hadith."
"4874","Chapter 84: The merit of al-Bara' ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him). 4086 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Hisham informed us, from Muhammad ibn Sirin, from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: I visited al-Bara' ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him) while he was reclining on his bed, reciting verses of poetry as if singing them. So I said to him: May Allah have mercy on you, Allah has replaced it for you with what is better than it: the Qur'an. He said: Do you fear that I die upon my bed! No, by Allah, Allah would not deprive me of that when I have killed a hundred single-handedly, apart from those I shared in killing alongside the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4875","Chapter 85: Reports of Abd Khayr. 4087 - Abu Ya'la said: al-Hasan ibn Hammad al-Kufi narrated to us, Mushir ibn Abd al-Malik ibn Sal' narrated to us, my father informed me, he said: I said to Abd Khayr: How many years have passed over you? He said: One hundred and twenty years. I said: Do you remember anything of the matter of Jahiliyyah? He said: Yes; we were in the land of Yemen, and the letter of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to us calling the people to abundant good. My father was of those who went out, and I was a boy. When he returned, he said to my mother: Order this pot to be poured out for the dogs, for we have accepted Islam. So he accepted Islam."
"4876","Chapter 86: The chapter of Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib. 4088 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from Sa'id ibn al-Musayyib, he said: I was born two years into the caliphate of Umar (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"4877","Chapter 87: Reports of Abu Uthman al-Nahdi. 4089 - Abu Bakr said: Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Asim al-Ahwal, he said: Subayh asked Abu Uthman al-Nahdi, while I was listening, and said to him: Did you reach the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Yes; I accepted Islam in the time of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and paid him three alms, but I did not meet him. And I raided in the time of Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) in several raids; I witnessed the conquest of al-Qadisiyyah, Jalula', Nihawand, Yarmuk, Adharbayjan, Mihran, and Rustum. We would eat ghee and leave the fat. He said: So I asked him about vessels? He said: They were not asked about. Meaning: the vessels of the polytheists."
"4878","Chapter 88: The merit of al-Ashajj, the Ashajj of Abd al-Qays, whose name is al-Mundhir. 4090 - Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn Sudran Abu Ja'far narrated to us, Talib ibn Hujayr al-Abdi narrated to us, Hud al-Asari narrated to us, from his grandfather, he said: While the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was narrating to his Companions, he said: There shall come upon you from this mountain pass a party of the best people of the east. So Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) stood and headed toward that direction, and met thirteen riders. He welcomed them and drew them near, and said: Who are the people? They said: People of Abd al-Qays. He said: What brought you to this land? Trade? They said: No. He said: Will you sell these swords of yours? They said: No. He said: Perhaps you have only come seeking this man? They said: Yes. So he walked with them conversing with them, until he looked toward the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said to them: This is your companion whom you seek. So the people threw themselves from their mounts; of them one who ran swiftly, of them one who trotted, and of them one who walked, until they came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and took his hand kissing it, and sat to him. And al-Ashajj remained, he being the youngest of the people; he made the camels kneel and hobbled them, gathered the baggage of the people, then came walking deliberately until he came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and took his hand and kissed it. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: In you are two traits Allah Almighty and His Messenger love. He said: What are they, O Messenger of Allah? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Forbearance and deliberateness. He said: As a natural disposition I was created upon, or as manners from me? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Rather as natural disposition. So he said: Praise be to Allah who created me upon what Allah and His Messenger love. And the people turned to dates they were eating... and he mentioned the hadith concerning the Barni dates. And the name of Hud's grandfather is Mazidah."
"4879","Chapter 89: Reports of Abu Inabah al-Khawlani, may Allah's mercy be upon him. 4091 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: al-Haytham ibn Kharijah narrated to us, al-Jarrah ibn Malih narrated to us, he said: I heard Bakr ibn Zur'ah al-Khawlani saying: I heard Abu Inabah al-Khawlani, and he was of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he was of those who ate blood in Jahiliyyah."
"4880","Chapter 90: Reports of Abdullah ibn Unays, may Allah have mercy on him. 4092 - Abu Ya'la said: al-Salt ibn Mas'ud narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abdullah ibn Yazid ibn Abdullah ibn Unays narrated to us, my uncle al-Hasan ibn Yazid narrated to me, from Abdullah ibn Unays (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent him alone as a detachment. I say: It is abridged from a long hadith."
"4881","Chapter 91: Reports of Maslamah ibn Mukhallad, may Allah have mercy on him. 4093 - Abu Ya'la said: Uthman narrated to us, Waki' narrated to us, Musa ibn Ulayy narrated to us, from his father, he said: I heard Maslamah ibn Mukhallad saying: I was born when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Madinah, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away when I was ten years old."
"4882","Chapter 93: What is adduced that the daughters of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) are more excellent than his wives, may Allah be pleased with them. 4094 - Abu Ya'la said: Suwayd ibn Sa'id narrated to us, al-Walid ibn Muhammad narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Salim, from his father, he said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Hafsah shall marry one better than Uthman, and Uthman shall marry one better than Hafsah. So he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married him his daughter."
"4883","Chapter 94: The merit of Khadijah, Mother of the Believers (may Allah be pleased with her). 4095 - Abu Ya'la said: Sulayman al-Shadhakuni Abu Ayyub narrated to us, Ismail ibn Aban narrated to us, Atiyyah ibn Ya'la narrated to us, Yazid of the progeny of Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to me, from al-Dahhak al-Shaybani, from Rabi'ah al-Sa'di, he said: Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman (may Allah be pleased with him) narrated to me from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): The mistress of the believing women is so-and-so, and Khadijah bint Khuwaylid (may Allah be pleased with her) is the first of Muslim women in Islam."
"4884","4096 - Surayj ibn Yunus narrated to us, Ismail narrated to us, from Mujalid, from al-Sha'bi, from Jabir ibn Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about Khadijah (may Allah be pleased with her), because she died before the obligatory duties, and he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I saw her in a river of the rivers of Paradise, in a house of reed, wherein is neither noise nor fatigue."
"4885","Chapter 95: The merit of Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her). 4097 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Husayn ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Ibn Abi Dhi'b narrated to us, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah, from Ammar ibn Yasir (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: When the concession of tayammum with clean earth was revealed to them, Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) entered upon Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her) and said: You are indeed blessed; the concession of tayammum has been revealed to us."
"4886","4098/1 - And Ibn Abi Umar said: Sufyan narrated to us, Sa'id ibn al-Marzuban narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Aswad, from his father, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married me while I was wearing hawf (a garment), and no sooner had he married me than modesty was cast upon me."
"4887","4098/2 - Abu Ya'la narrated it: Muhammad ibn Abbad narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us with it."
"4888","4098/3 - And al-Bazzar narrated it from the path of Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad al-Muharibi, from Abu Sa'id al-Baqqal with it, and fuller than it."
"4889","4099 - And Abu Ya'la said: Harun ibn Ma'ruf narrated to us, Abu Usamah narrated to us, from Mujalid, from Amir, from Masruq, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered upon me while I was weeping. He said: What makes you weep? I said: Fatimah (may Allah be pleased with her) insulted me. So he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Fatimah, did you insult Aishah? She said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do you not love whom I love and hate whom I hate? She said: Yes. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: For I love Aishah, so love her. Fatimah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: We shall never say to Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her) anything that harms her, ever."
"4890","4100 - And al-Harith said: Ismail ibn Abi Ismail narrated to us, Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Maryam, from Damrah ibn Habib, he said: Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her) was mentioned before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Leave Aishah, for she fasts much and prays much at night; she is my wife in this world and the Hereafter."
"4891","4101 - And al-Tayalisi said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from one who heard Ammar - and a man mentioned Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her) before him and disparaged her - so Ammar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: Be silent, disgraced and reviled; do you harm the beloved of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)?"
"4892","4102 - And al-Harith said: Yahya ibn Hashim narrated to us, Hisham ibn Urwah narrated to us, from his father, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) carried me upon his shoulder, while the Abyssinians were playing dirkalah (a game)."
"4893","4103 - And Ahmad said in al-Zuhd: Umm Amr bint Hassan, a truthful old woman, narrated to us, she said: Sa'id ibn Yahya ibn Qays, and he is her husband, narrated to me, from his father, he said: Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her) used to say: No person hates me in this world but I shall disown him in the Hereafter."
"4894","4104 - And Abdullah ibn Ahmad said: I read to my father, from Muhammad ibn Ubayd, from Harun al-Barbari, from Abdullah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr, he said: A man came after the death of Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), and Ubayd ibn Umayr asked him: How did you see the grief of the people over her? He said: By Allah, their grief was not all that intense. Ubayd ibn Umayr said: Only one grieves over Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her) to whom she was a mother."
"4895","4105 - And al-Tayalisi said: Zam'ah, and he is Ibn Salih, narrated to us, he said: I heard Ibn Abi Mulaykah saying: Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her) heard the cry over Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), so she sent her slave-girl: See what she has done. She came and said: She has passed away. So she said: May Allah have mercy on her; by Him in Whose Hand is my soul, she was the most beloved of people to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except her father."
"4896","4106 - And Abu Bakr said: Abd al-Rahim narrated to us, from Ismail ibn Abi Khalid, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Dahhak, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Muhammad ibn Zayd ibn Jud'an, Abdullah ibn Safwan narrated to us, and another with him, that they came to Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), and she (may Allah be pleased with her) said: O so-and-so, have you heard the hadith of Hafsah (may Allah be pleased with her)? He said: Yes, O Mother of the Believers. So Abdullah ibn Safwan (may Allah be pleased with him) said: And what is that, O Mother of the Believers? She (may Allah be pleased with her) said: In me are nine which were in none of the women, except what Allah - the Almighty - gave Maryam daughter of Imran (may Allah be pleased with her); by Allah, I do not say this boasting over any of my co-wives. Abdullah ibn Safwan (may Allah be pleased with him) said: And what are they, O Mother of the Believers? She (may Allah be pleased with her) said: The angel descended with my image, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married me at seven years, and I was presented to him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at nine, and he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married me a virgin whom none of the people shared with him in me, and revelation came to him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while I and he were in one blanket, and I was the most beloved of women to him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and verses of the Qur'an were revealed concerning me in which the nation almost perished, and I saw Jibril (peace and blessings be upon him) and none of his wives (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw him but I, and he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away in my house with none present but the angel and me."
"4897","4107 - And Abu Ya'la said: Bishr ibn al-Walid narrated to us, Abu Ja'far narrated to us, from Sulayman al-Shaybani, from Ali ibn Zayd ibn Jud'an, from his grandmother, from Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: I was given nine which no woman was given but Maryam... and she mentioned the like of it. She said: Revelation would descend upon him while he was among his family scattered from him, and it would descend upon him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while I was with him in his blanket; and I am the daughter of his caliph and his truthful one; I was created pure, with a pure one; and I was promised forgiveness and generous provision. And the hadith of Dhakwan, the freedman of Aishah (may Allah be pleased with her), concerning the story of the drawer (durj) which Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) sent to her out of the love of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for her, will come, Allah willing, in the Umari conquests."
"4898","Chapter 96: The merit of Umm Waraqah (may Allah be pleased with her). 4108 - Ishaq said: Abu Nu'aym al-Mula'i informed us, al-Walid ibn Jumay' narrated to us, my grandmother narrated to me, from Umm Waraqah bint Abdullah ibn al-Harith al-Ansari (may Allah be pleased with them both) - and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to visit her and call her the martyr, and she had gathered the Qur'an and hadith - Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) stood among the people and said: Umm Waraqah's boy and slave-girl oppressed her and killed her, and they fled; they were brought and crucified. So Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) spoke the truth; he used to say: Come, let us visit the martyr. Abu Dawud narrated part of it besides what I have mentioned here."
"4899","Chapter 97: The merit of Jamrah al-Yarbu'iyyah al-Hanzaliyyah (may Allah be pleased with her). 4109 - Abu Ya'la said: Abu Ma'mar, and he is al-Qati'i, narrated to us, Atwan, and he is Ibn Mishkan, narrated to us, from Jamrah al-Hanzaliyyah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the charity camels, and he wiped my head and supplicated for me."
"4900","Chapter 98: The merit of Zaynab bint Jahsh (may Allah be pleased with her). 4110 - Abu Ya'la said: Ja'far narrated to us, Abd al-Warith narrated to us, from Abd al-Aziz, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) consummated marriage with Zaynab bint Jahsh (may Allah be pleased with her), and she had been given beauty, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was intensely shy... the hadith."
"4901","4111/1 - And Abu Bakr said: Ahmad ibn Abdullah narrated to us, Umm al-Aswad narrated to me, from Munyah, from Abu Barzah al-Aslami (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had nine wives, and he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said one day: The best of you is the longest of you in hand. So each one stood placing her hand upon the wall. So the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I do not mean this, but the most industrious of you with her hands (i.e. most charitable)."
"4902","4111/2 - And Abu Ya'la said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us with this."
"4903","Chapter 99: The merit of Maymunah (may Allah be pleased with her). 4112 - Abu Bakr said: Affan narrated to us, Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Abdullah ibn al-Asamm narrated to us, from Yazid ibn al-Asamm, he said: Maymunah (may Allah be pleased with her) became gravely ill in Makkah, with none of her sister's sons present, so she said: Take me out of Makkah, for I shall not die in it; the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) informed me that I shall not die in it. Until they brought her to Sarif, to the tree under which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) consummated marriage, in the place of the dome, and she died. When we placed her in her lahd, I took my cloak and placed it under her cheek in the lahd, but Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both) took it and threw it away."
"4904","Chapter 100: The merit of Safiyyah bint Abd al-Muttalib (may Allah be pleased with her). 4113/1 - Abu Ya'la said: Zuhayr ibn Harb narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Hasan, and he is Ibn Zabalah, narrated to us, Umm Urwah, and she is the daughter of Ja'far ibn al-Zubayr, narrated to me, from her father, from her grandfather al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) left his women on the day of Uhud in Madinah, he left them in Fari', and among them was Safiyyah bint Abd al-Muttalib (may Allah be pleased with her), and he left among them Hassan ibn Thabit (may Allah be pleased with him). A man of the polytheists came to enter upon them, so Safiyyah (may Allah be pleased with her) said to Hassan ibn Thabit (may Allah be pleased with him): Deal with the man. But Hassan (may Allah be pleased with him) was cowardly and refused her. So Safiyyah (may Allah be pleased with her) took the sword and struck the polytheist with it until she killed him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was informed of that, and he gave Safiyyah (may Allah be pleased with her) a share as he gave the men. 4113/2 - Ibn Zabalah was followed in it by Ishaq ibn Muhammad ibn Abi Farwah, and he is of the men of al-Bukhari; he narrated it from Umm Urwah; al-Bazzar narrated it from his path, with a fuller wording."
"4905","Chapter 101: The chapter of Sawdah. 4114 - Ishaq said: Mu'adh ibn Hisham informed us, my father narrated to me, al-Qasim ibn Abi Bazzah narrated to me, that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent to Sawdah divorcing her. So she said: Has he divorced me apart from his women? She sat upon his path from the house of Aishah. He passed by her, and she said: I adjure you by the One who revealed the Book to you and chose you over creation: have you divorced me due to ill feeling you found against me? And I adjure you by the One who revealed the Book to you and chose you over creation: take me back. For by Allah, I have grown old, and I have no need of a husband, but I want to be raised as one of your women. So he took her back. Then she said: I give my day and my night to the delight of the Messenger's eye, Aishah."
"4906","Chapter 102: Mention of Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her). 4115 - Abu Ya'la said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Salih al-Azdi narrated to us, Ajlan ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Adi narrated to me, from Malik ibn Dinar, from Anas ibn Malik (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: When death came to Abu Salamah (may Allah be pleased with him), Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: To whom do you entrust me? He (may Allah be pleased with him) said: O Allah, replace for Umm Salamah better than Abu Salamah. When he passed away, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) proposed to her, and she said: I am advanced in age. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I am older than you in age, and dependents are upon Allah Almighty and His Messenger; as for jealousy, I shall supplicate Allah - the Almighty - to remove it. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married her, and sent to her two handmills and a water jar."
"4907","4116/1 - And Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Abu al-Nadr narrated to us."
"4908","4116/2 - And Abu Ya'la said: Hudbah ibn Khalid narrated to us, both saying: Sulayman ibn al-Mughirah narrated to us, from Thabit, Umar ibn Abi Salamah informed me, he said: Abu Salamah (may Allah be pleased with him) came... and he mentioned the hadith of his death, and that Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him) proposed to her and she refused him, then Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) and she refused him, then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent to her proposing to her. She (may Allah be pleased with her) said: In me are three traits. Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) heard what she answered the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with, and became angry for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) more than he became angry for himself. So he came to her and said to her: Are you the one who refuses the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? With what do you refuse him? She said: O Ibn al-Khattab, in me is such-and-such. And in the hadith: As for what you mentioned of jealousy, I shall supplicate Allah Almighty to remove it. He said: So she was among women as if she were not of them, not finding what women find of jealousy. I say: Ahmad narrated it from the path of Hammad ibn Salamah, from Thabit, without this addition."
"4909","4117/1 - And al-Tayalisi said: al-Hakam ibn Atiyyah narrated to us, from Thabit, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: That with which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) married Umm Salamah (may Allah be pleased with her) was something worth ten dirhams."
"4910","4117/2 - And Abu Ya'la said: Harun al-Hammal narrated to us, Abu Dawud narrated to us with it."
"4911","Chapter 103: Mention of Hafsah (may Allah be pleased with her). 4118 - Abu Ya'la said: Abu Kurayb narrated to us, Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abu Salih, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) entered upon Hafsah (may Allah be pleased with her) while she was weeping. He said: What is with you? Has the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divorced you? He had divorced you once, then took you back for my sake; by Allah, if he divorces you another time, I shall never speak to you."
"4912","4119/1 - And al-Harith said: Affan narrated to us, Hammad narrated to us, Abu Imran al-Jawni narrated to us, from Qays ibn Zayd (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) divorced Hafsah (may Allah be pleased with her). Then her maternal uncles - Qudamah and Uthman ibn Maz'un (may Allah be pleased with them both) - came, and she wept and said: By Allah, he did not divorce me out of surfeit. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came, and she veiled herself. He said: Jibril (peace and blessings be upon him) said to me: Take back Hafsah, for she fasts much and prays much at night, and she is your wife in Paradise."
"4913","4119/2 - Yunus ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Hammad narrated to us, from Abu Imran, from Qays ibn Zayd or Yazid, the like of it."
"4914","Chapter 104: Mention of Safiyyah bint Huyayy (may Allah be pleased with her). 4120 - Abu Ya'la said: Abu Sa'id al-Jushami narrated to us, Ulaylah bint al-Kumayt narrated to us, she said: I heard my mother Umaynah saying: Amatullah bint Razinah narrated to me, from her mother Razinah, the freedwoman of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took Safiyyah (may Allah be pleased with her) captive on the day of Qurayzah and al-Nadir, the day Allah - the Almighty - gave him victory. He came leading her captive; when she saw the women, she said: I testify that there is no god but Allah, and that you are the Messenger of Allah. So he released her; her forearm (may Allah be pleased with her) was in his hand (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then he freed her, then proposed to her and married her, and gave Razinah as her dowry. I say: A rejected (munkar) hadith, from unknown women; and what is in the Sahih from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him) is that he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made freeing her her dowry, and likewise has preceded from her herself (may Allah be pleased with her) in the Book of Marriage."
"4915","4121 - And Abu Ya'la said: Shayban ibn Farrukh narrated to us, from Sulayman, and he is Ibn al-Mughirah, Humayd, meaning Ibn Hilal, narrated to us, he said: Safiyyah (may Allah be pleased with her) said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when none of the people was more hateful to me than he. So he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Your people did such-and-such. She (may Allah be pleased with her) said: I did not rise from my seat, with none of the people more beloved to me than he."
"4916","4122/1 - Abu Hisham Muhammad ibn Yazid ibn Rifa'ah narrated to us, Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Ismail ibn Mujammi', Uthman ibn Ka'b narrated to me, from Rabi', from Safiyyah bint Huyayy (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mounted me behind him on the rear of his she-camel. She (may Allah be pleased with her) said: I began to doze, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would touch me with his hand and say: O you, O daughter of Huyayy; and he kept saying: O Safiyyah, I apologize to you for what I did to your people; they said to me such, they said to me such."
"4917","4122/2 - Ibn Numayr narrated to us, Yunus narrated to us with it. And he said: Rabi' - a man of Banu al-Nadir who was in Safiyyah's quarter - narrated to me, from Safiyyah (may Allah be pleased with her), she said: I never saw any better in character than the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)... and he mentioned the like of it."
"4918","Chapter 105: Mention of Umm Ayman (may Allah be pleased with her). 4123 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Rawh narrated to us, Hisham narrated to us, from Uthman ibn al-Qasim, he said: Umm Ayman (may Allah be pleased with her) went out emigrating to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from Makkah to Madinah, walking, with no provision with her, fasting on an intensely hot day. Severe thirst befell her until she almost died of thirst. She said: When the sun set, behold the fluttering of something above my head. I raised my head, and behold a bucket of water with a white rope. It drew near me until it was within my reach; I took it and drank from it until I was quenched. I would fast after that on a hot day, then walk about in the sun to become thirsty, but I never thirsted after it."
"4919","Chapter 106: Mention of Zaynab, the wife of Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with them both). 4124 - Ibn Abi Umar said: Waki' narrated to us, from Abu al-Umays, from Yazid ibn Ju'dubah, from Zaynab, the wife of Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with them both), she said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had given her at Khaybar picked dates of fifty wasqs of dates, and twenty wasqs of barley."
"4920","Chapter 108: The chapter of Umm Hani'. 4125 - Ishaq said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa informed us, Isra'il narrated to us, from al-Suddi, from Abu Salih, whose name is Badhan, the freedman of Umm Hani', from Umm Hani', she said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) proposed to me, and I excused myself to him, and he excused me. Then Allah revealed: {O Prophet, We have made lawful to you your wives}... to His saying: {who emigrated with you}. She said: I was not lawful to him, and I had not emigrated with him; I was of the Tulaqa' (those pardoned at the conquest). Chapter 109: Mention of Umm Malik al-Ansariyyah (may Allah be pleased with her)."
"4921","4126 - Abu Bakr said: Ibn Fudayl narrated to us, from Ata' ibn al-Sa'ib, from Yahya ibn Ja'dah, from a man who narrated to him, from Umm Malik al-Ansariyyah (may Allah be pleased with her), he said: Umm Malik (may Allah be pleased with her) came with a skin of ghee to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered Bilal (may Allah be pleased with him) and he squeezed it out, then returned it to her. She went back, and behold it was full of ghee. So I came and said: Has something been revealed concerning me, O Messenger of Allah? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: And what is that, O Umm Malik? She said: You returned my gift. He said: So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called Bilal (may Allah be pleased with him) and asked him about that, and he said: By Him who sent you with the truth, I squeezed it until I was abashed. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Enjoy it, O Umm Malik; this is a blessing for which Allah has hastened your reward."
"4922","4127 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from al-Hasan, from al-Ahnaf ibn Qays (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: I used to hear Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) saying: No one of Quraysh enters a door but people enter with him. I did not know the interpretation of his saying, until Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) was stabbed, and he ordered Suhayb (may Allah be pleased with him) to lead the people in prayer, and ordered that food be made for the people... and he mentioned the hadith. And it has passed in Funerals."
"4923","4128/1 - And al-Harith said: Abu Nu'aym narrated to us, Talhah, and he is Ibn Amr, narrated to us, from Ata', from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with them both), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said... and he mentioned a hadith. And in it: Were it not lest Quraysh exult, I would inform it of what it has with Allah - the Almighty - O Allah, You made its first taste punishment, so make its last taste bounty."
"4924","4128/2 - And Abu Ya'la said: Mahmud ibn Khidash narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ubayd narrated to us, Talhah narrated to us with this."
"4925","4129 - And al-Tayalisi said: Ja'far ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from al-Nadr ibn Humayd al-Kindi or al-Abdi, from Abu al-Jarud, from Abu al-Ahwas, from Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him): The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not revile Quraysh, for the knowledge of its scholar fills the earth with knowledge. O Allah, You made the first of Quraysh taste punishment - retribution or calamity - so make its last taste bounty."
"4926","4130 - Mansur ibn Abi al-Aswad narrated to us, Mujalid narrated to us, from al-Sha'bi, Ma'mar ibn Abdullah ibn Nadlah narrated to me, he said: I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and heard him saying: Look to Quraysh; hear their word and leave their deed."
"4927","4131/1 - And Ibn Abi Umar said: Bishr ibn al-Sari narrated to us, Hammad narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Amrah, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Seek strength and trustworthiness in the leaders of Quraysh, for the strong of Quraysh has two excellences over the strongest of others, and the trustworthy of Quraysh has two excellences over the trustworthy of others."
"4928","4131/2 - And Abu Ya'la said: Ahmad ibn Umar al-Waki'i narrated to us, Mu'ammal narrated to us, Hammad narrated to us with it."
"4929","4132 - And Ibn Abi Umar said: Bishr ibn al-Sari narrated to us, Hammad narrated to us, Ali ibn Zayd narrated to us, from Zurarah ibn Awfa, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: People follow Quraysh; their best follow their best, and their worst follow their worst."
"4930","4133 - And Abu Bakr said: Abd al-A'la narrated to us, from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, from Sahl ibn Abi Hathmah (may Allah be pleased with him), he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Learn from Quraysh and do not teach them; advance Quraysh and do not put them behind, for the Qurashi has the strength of two men of other than Quraysh."
"4931","111 - Banu Hashim not standing for anyone. 4134 - Abu Yala said: Abu al-Rabi narrated to us, Abbad ibn Abbad narrated, Jafar ibn al-Zubayr narrated, from al-Qasim, from Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A man stands from his seat for his brother, except Banu Hashim, for they do not stand for anyone."
"4932","4135 - Shabab ibn Khayyat narrated to us, al-Fudayl ibn Sulayman narrated, from Muhammad ibn Zayd ibn al-Muhajir ibn Qunfudh, from Abu Ishaq the freedman of Ibn Abbas, from Abdullah ibn Shurahbil ibn Hasanah, from Abdullah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The first of people to perish is Quraysh, and the first of Quraysh to perish is Banu Hashim."
"4933","112 - Excellence of the Muhajirun, may Allah be pleased with them. 4136 - al-Harith said: Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated to us, Qays — he is Ibn al-Rabi — narrated, from Uthman ibn Abi Zuraah, from a freedwoman of Abu Musa, may Allah be pleased with him, concerning His saying, mighty and majestic, about the first Muhajirun, he said: Whoever prayed facing both qiblas with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"4934","113 - Chapter on the excellence of the Ansar, may Allah be pleased with them. 4137 - Abu Bakr said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, from Hisham ibn Harun al-Ansari, Muadh ibn Rifaa ibn Rafi narrated, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, forgive the Ansar, and the offspring of the Ansar, and the offspring of their offspring, and their freedmen and their neighbours. A hadith concerning the bequest regarding the Ansar, may Allah be pleased with them, is in the chapter: The caliphs are from Quraysh."
"4935","4138 - Abu Bakr said: Isa narrated, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir who said: We remained in Madinah two years before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to us, maintaining the mosques and establishing prayer."
"4936","4139 - Abu Yala said: Musab ibn Abdullah narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from Qudamah ibn Ibrahim, who said: I saw al-Hajjaj beating Abbas ibn Sahl during the rule of Ibn al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with them both, then Sahl ibn Sad, may Allah be pleased with him, came to him, an old man with two braids, wearing two garments: a waist-wrapper and a cloak, and he stood before the two rows and said: O Hajjaj, will you not observe the bequest of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning us? He said: What did the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bequeath concerning you? He said: That good be done to the doer of good among the Ansar and the wrongdoer among them be pardoned. He said: So he released him. Ibn Hibban brought it in his Sahih from Abu Yala."
"4937","4140 - Abu Yala said: Ibrahim ibn al-Hajjaj al-Nili narrated to us, Salih al-Murri narrated, from al-Hasan, from one of the Muhajirun, who said: They said: O Messenger of Allah, we have not seen the like of a people among whom we stayed, meaning the Ansar; they shared their wealth with us and sufficed us the burden, and we feared they may have taken all the reward. So he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No; as long as you supplicate to Allah, mighty and majestic, for them and praise them, they have not taken all the reward."
"4938","4141 - Said narrated to us, Rushayd narrated, Thabit narrated, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Allah, bless them. Meaning the slave-girls of Banu al-Najjar."
"4939","4142 - Abu Bakr said: Affan narrated to us, Hammam narrated, a man of the Ansar narrated to me, who said: Abu Bakr ibn Abdullah ibn Qays narrated to us from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to visit the Ansar frequently, privately and publicly; when he visited privately he would come to the man in his house, and when he visited publicly he would come to the mosque."
"4940","4143 - Abu Yala said: Zakariyya ibn Yahya Zahmawayh narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abi Zaidah narrated, Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated, from Husayn ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Mahmud ibn Labid, from Ibn Shafi the physician, who said: Usayd ibn Hudayr, may Allah be pleased with him, called me and I cut his sciatic vein for him, and he narrated to me two hadiths. He said: The people of two houses of my tribe came to me: a household of Banu Zafar and a household of Banu Muawiyah, and they said: Speak to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for us, or something like this. So I spoke to him, and he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes, I shall allot to the people of each house among them one half, and if Allah, mighty and majestic, grants again we shall grant them again. He said: I said: May Allah reward you with good, O Messenger of Allah. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: And you, may Allah reward you with good, for you, as far as I know, are chaste and patient. He said: And I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: You will meet favouritism after me. Then in the time of Umar he distributed robes among the people and sent me one robe from them, and I deemed it small and gave it to my son. While I was praying, a young man of Quraysh passed by me wearing one of those robes and dragging it, so I remembered the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) spoke the truth. Then a man went to Umar and informed him, and he came while I was praying and said: Pray, O Usayd. When I finished my prayer, he said: That robe I sent to so-and-so, and he is a Badri, an Uhudi, an Aqabi, then this youth came to him and bought it from him and wore it; did you think that would happen in my time? He said: I said: By Allah, O Commander of the Faithful, I thought that would not happen in your time."
"4941","From the Book of Virtues. 114 - Chapter on the excellence of tribes of the Arabs. 4144 - Abu Yala said: Muhammad ibn Bahr al-Basri narrated to us, Amr ibn Yahya ibn Said narrated to us, my father narrated to me, he said: I heard Sad ibn Ibrahim narrating from his father, from his grandfather Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Quraysh, the Ansar, Juhaynah, Muzaynah, Aslam, Ghifar, Ashja and Sulaym are allies of mine; they have no ally besides Allah and His Messenger. Amr ibn Yahya said: I met Ishaq ibn Sad ibn Ibrahim in the mosque and said: My father narrated to me from your father, and mentioned it. He said: They are only seven; I do not know which one is missing from them. Amr said: My father mentioned from another than him that the one missing from them is Sulaym. I say: The hadith is in the Sahih with other than this wording through Sad ibn Ibrahim, but he said: from al-Araj, from Abu Hurayrah, and that is more correct."
"4942","115 - Banu Amir and Banu Tamim. 4145/1 - Musaddad said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-Hajjaj, from Awn ibn Abi Juhayfah, from his father, who said: I entered upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), I and a man of Banu Amir, and he said: From whom are you? They said: From Banu Amir. He said: Welcome to you; you are from me."
"4943","4145/2 - Abu Bakr said: Abbad ibn al-Awwam narrated to us, Hajjaj narrated likewise."
"4944","4145/3 - Abu Yala said: Abu Khaythamah narrated to us, Ibn Numayr narrated, from Hajjaj with it."
"4945","4146 - al-Harith said: Abu al-Nadr narrated to us, Sallam ibn Sulaym narrated, from Zayd al-Ammi, from Mansur, from Ibn Sirin, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was asked about the tribes of the Arabs. He said: He was distracted from them that day, or they were distracted from him, except that they asked him about three tribes. They asked him about Banu Amir, and he said: A bright camel eating from the edges of trees. They asked him about Ghatafan, and he said: A flower giving forth water. They asked him about Banu Tamim, and he said: A red hill; whoever opposes them will not harm them. And the people spoke about them, so the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah has decreed nothing but good for Banu Tamim; they are large-headed, weighty in forbearance, firm-footed, the fiercest of people in fighting the Dajjal, and supporters of the truth at the end of time."
"4946","116 - Banu Himyar and al-Sakun. 4147 - Ibn Abi Umar said: al-Muqri narrated to us, al-Ifriqi narrated, Abu al-Ghazi al-Ansi narrated, from Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: He heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Among the best of people are the kings; the kings of Himyar, and Shaban, and al-Sakun, and the Asharis."
"4947","117 - Banu Najiyah. 4148/1 - al-Tayalisi said: Shubah narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, who said: A man narrated to me, from Sad, who said: Banu Najiyah were mentioned before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: They are a tribe from me. He said: And I think he said: And I am from them. Either the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said something else, meaning Samah ibn Luay, and a man said: The relation clung to Samah, or the man said that and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) answered him."
"4948","4148/2 - Abu Yala said: Musa narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Jafar narrated to us, Shubah narrated to us, he said: I asked Sad ibn Ibrahim about Banu Najiyah? He said: They are from us. Sad said: They narrate from Said ibn Zayd, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: They are a tribe from me. Shubah said: And I think he said: And I am from them."
"4949","118 - Najiyah. 4148/3 - Ishaq said: Abd al-Samad ibn Abd al-Warith informed us, Shubah narrated to us, he said: I said to Sad ibn Ibrahim: Did you hear what is mentioned about Banu Najiyah from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): That they are a tribe from me and I am from them — from a trustworthy source? He said: Yes; that is narrated from Said ibn Zayd ibn Amr ibn Nufayl, may Allah be pleased with him. He said: They came to Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, may Allah be pleased with him, and gifted him large feeding-trough saddles. 4148/4 - Shubah said: Simak ibn Harb narrated to us, he said: We used to come to Mudrik ibn al-Muhallab in his camp, and Banu Najiyah were mentioned, and there was a man whose grandfather was Said, and he narrated to me from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: They are a tribe from me, and I am from them."
"4950","119 - The Ansar, may Allah be pleased with them. 4149 - Ishaq said: al-Nadr ibn Shumayl informed us, Shubah narrated to us, from Qatadah, who said: I heard Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, narrating from Usayd ibn Hudayr, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best quarters of the Ansar are Banu al-Najjar, then Banu Abd al-Ashhal, then Banu al-Harith, then Banu Saidah, and in all the quarters of the Ansar there is good. This is a sound hadith; the Two Shaykhs and others narrated it from the hadith of Ghundar from Shubah, from Qatadah, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he did not mention Usayd ibn Hudayr, may Allah be pleased with him; he is established in the chain we have cited, and the addition from one like al-Nadr, with his memory and precision, is accepted."
"4951","120 - Aslam. 4150 - Abu Yala said: Uqbah — he is Ibn Mukram — narrated to us, Yunus — he is Ibn Bukayr — narrated, Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated, from Salih ibn Kaysan, from Urwah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: I do not accept a gift from a Bedouin. Then Umm Sunbulah the Bedouin woman came to him with a cup of milk which she gifted to him. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Pour some of it into this cup. She poured it, and he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took it and drank. I said to him: You said: I do not accept a gift from a Bedouin. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Bedouins of Aslam are not Bedouins; rather they are the people of our desert, and we are the people of their settled land; if we call them they answer us, and if they call us we answer them."
"4952","121 - Abd al-Qays. 4151 - Abu Yala said: Shabab — Khalifah ibn Khayyat — narrated to us, Awn ibn Kahmas narrated to us, Hisham narrated, from Muhammad, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The best people of the east are Abd al-Qays."
"4953","122 - Ahmas. 4152 - al-Tayalisi said: Shubah narrated to us, from Mukhariq, from Tariq, that he heard him say: The delegation of Bajilah came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Begin with the Ahmasis. And he supplicated for us."
"4954","123 - Rabiah and Mudar. 4153/1 - Abu Bakr said: Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn al-Muammal, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When people differ, the truth is with Mudar; and when Rabiah grows mighty, that is the humiliation of Islam."
"4955","4153/2 - Abu Yala said: Abu Bakr narrated to us with it."
"4956","124 - Bakr ibn Wail. 4154 - Abu Yala said: Umayyah ibn Bistam narrated to us, Mutamir narrated to us, Maslamah ibn Muharib narrated to us, from a shaykh of the people of Kufah, who said: al-Hakam ibn Amr al-Ghifari wrote to Muawiyah, may Allah be pleased with him, from Khurasan: The polytheists — meaning — have grown numerous against him, so he wrote to him: Place Bakr ibn Wail facing them, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The enemy will not prevail over Bakr ibn Wail."
"4957","125 - Chapter on the censure of al-Ubbad, who are a group of the Christians of the Arabs. 4155 - al-Harith said: Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Abu Ishaq — meaning al-Fazari — narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Musa ibn Abi Aishah, from Sulayman, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The furthest of people from Islam are al-Ubbad of the Romans."
"4958","126 - Chapter on the censure of the Berbers. 4156 - Ibn Abi Umar said: al-Muqri narrated to us, Haywah narrated to us, Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, Abu Hani narrated to us, he said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Qays al-Qurashi informed him from one who informed him, from Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, that he heard the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Wickedness is sixty-one parts: one part is in the jinn and mankind, and sixty are in the Berbers."
"4959","127 - Chapter on the excellence of the Companions and the Followers in general. 4157 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Sufyan, Nusayr ibn Dhuluq narrated to me, who said: I heard Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, say: Do not revile the Companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), for the standing of one of them is better than the deeds of one of you for his whole life."
"4960","4158 - Ibn Abi Umar said: Abdullah ibn Ali narrated to us, from Sallam al-Tawil, from Zayd al-Ammi, from Yazid al-Raqashi, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: The likeness of my Companions among my nation is like the stars by which they are guided; when they disappear, they are perplexed. Its chain is weak."
"4961","4159 - Abd ibn Humayd said: Ahmad ibn Yunus informed me, Abu Shihab narrated to us, from Hamzah al-Jazari, from Nafi, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The likeness of my Companions is like the stars by which guidance is sought; whichever of them you take his saying from, you will be guided. Hamzah is very weak."
"4962","4160 - al-Tayalisi said: Musa ibn Mutayr narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If a man had Uhud in gold and spent it in the path of Allah the Exalted and on widows, the poor and orphans, to attain the merit of a man of my Companions for an hour of the day, he would never attain it."
"4963","4161/1 - Abu Bakr said: Ibn Idris narrated to us, from his father, from his grandfather, from Ja'dah ibn Hubayrah, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best of people is my generation, then those who follow them, then those who follow them, then those who follow them, then the rest are worse."
"4964","4161/2 - Abd said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us with this."
"4965","4161/3 - Abu Yala said: Zakariyya ibn Yahya narrated to us, Ibn Idris narrated — and he mentioned it."
"4966","4162 - Abu Bakr said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, Abdullah ibn al-Ala ibn Zabr narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Amir informed me, from Wathilah ibn al-Asqa, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You will remain in good as long as there remains among you one who saw me and accompanied me; by Allah, you will remain in good as long as there remains among you one who saw one who saw me and accompanied one who accompanied me."
"4967","4163 - Ahmad ibn Mani said: Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, a man narrated, from Mujahid, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: Do not revile the Companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), for Allah, mighty and majestic, commanded seeking forgiveness for them, while He knows they will innovate and do deeds."
"4968","4164 - Mansur ibn Ammar narrated to us, Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Abu al-Khayr, from Hudhayfah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: There will be a slip for my Companions after me which Allah the Exalted will forgive them for their precedence with me; people after me will act upon it, and Allah the Exalted will throw them into the Fire upon their nostrils."
"4969","4165/1 - Abu Bakr said: Jafar ibn Awn narrated to us, from al-Amash, from Abu Sufyan, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There will come upon people a time when an army goes out and it is said: Is there among you anyone of the Companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? They will seek him and not find him. Then an army will go out and it is said: Is there among you one who saw anyone of the Companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? They will seek him and not find him. If one of my Companions were beyond the sea, they would come to him."
"4970","4165/2 - Abu Yala said: Uqbah ibn Mukram narrated to us, Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated, from al-Amash likewise. Its wording: There will come upon people a time when an army of their armies goes out and it is said: Is there among you one who accompanied Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) so you may seek victory through him and be granted victory? It is said: No. Then it is said: Is there among you one who accompanied his Companions? And it is said: One who saw one who accompanied his Companions? If they heard of him from beyond the seas, they would come to him."
"4971","4165/3 - Ibn Numayr narrated to us, Muhadir narrated to us, from al-Amash, and he mentioned it with the wording: A detachment is sent out, and it is said: Is there among you anyone who accompanied Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? It is said: Yes. He is sought and a man is found, and victory is sought through the man. Then a detachment is sent out, and it is said: Is there among you one who saw the Companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He is sought and not found, until if he were beyond the sea you would come to him. Then there will remain a people who recite the Quran not knowing what it is."
"4972","4165/4 - al-Haytham ibn Kulayb said: Ahmad ibn Hazim ibn Abi Gharzah narrated to us, Jafar ibn Awn narrated to us with it. This chain is sound, but Abu Sufyan shortened it. Al-Bukhari and Muslim narrated it through Amr ibn Dinar, and Muslim through Abu al-Zubayr, both from Jabir, from Abu Said, may Allah be pleased with him, and that is correct."
"4973","4165/5 - Abu Yala said: Ishaq narrated to us, Yahya ibn Ishaq narrated to us, Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: There will come upon people a time when, if they heard of a man of my Companions from beyond the sea, they would come to him. Thus Ibn Lahiah shortened both its chain and its text."
"4974","4167 - Abu Yala said: al-Azraq ibn Ali narrated to us, Hassan narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Fadl narrated to us, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: People will increase and my Companions will decrease, so do not revile them; may Allah curse whoever reviles them."
"4975","4168 - Abd said: Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us, Israil narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from al-Harith, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Hour will not come until a man of my Companions is sought as a stray animal is sought and not found."
"4976","4169 - al-Harith said: Khalid ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, Rushdayn ibn Sad narrated to us, Abu Hani Humayd ibn Hani al-Khawlani narrated to us, from al-Ghifari: That he heard Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, in Madinah say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to his Companions, may Allah be pleased with them: What will become of you when you are satiated with bread and oil? They clamoured and magnified Allah for a while, then said: When, O Messenger of Allah? He said: When the cities are conquered. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them: What will become of you when different colours come upon you, and you go out in one garment in the morning and come in another? They said: When will that be, O Messenger of Allah? He said: When the cities are conquered, and Persia and Rome are conquered. They said: Then they are better than us, O Messenger of Allah; they will witness the conquests. He said: Rather you are better than them, and your sons are better than their sons, and the sons of your sons are better than the sons of their sons; they did not take with gratitude, they did not take with gratitude, they did not take with gratitude."
"4977","4170/1 - Abu Yala said: Suwayd ibn Said narrated to us, Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from Ismail, from al-Hasan, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The likeness of my Companions is like salt in food; food is not good except with salt."
"4978","4170/2 - al-Bazzar said: Taliq ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Abu Muawiyah narrated to us with it. He said: Ismail is alone in it, and he is not a preserver."
"4979","4171 - Abu Bakr said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, from Musa ibn Ubaydah, from Muhammad ibn Kab, from Awf ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Would that I had met my brothers. They said: O Messenger of Allah, are we not your brothers and your Companions? He said: Yes; but a people who will come after you, believing in me as you believe, affirming me as you affirm me, aiding me as you aid me — would that I had met my brothers. The hadith of Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, has preceded in Faith."
"4980","128 - Chapter on the prohibition of mentioning the Companions, may Allah be pleased with them, with evil. 4172 - Ishaq said: Zakariyya ibn Adi informed us, from Ubaydullah ibn Amr al-Raqqi, from Zayd ibn Abi Unaysah, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Khaythamah, who said: Sad ibn Abi Waqqas, may Allah be pleased with him, was among a group, and they mentioned Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, and reviled him. So Sad, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Gently with the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), for we committed a sin with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Allah, mighty and majestic, revealed: Had it not been for a prior decree from Allah, a great punishment would have touched you for what you took, and I hope it is a mercy from Allah the Exalted that preceded for us. One of them said: By Allah, he hates you and calls you al-Ukhaynis. So Sad, may Allah be pleased with him, laughed until laughter overcame him, then said: Does a man not find resentment against his brother over a matter between him and him, yet that does not reach his trustworthiness? And he mentioned another word. This is a sound chain, and it contains grave benefits."
"4981","4173 - Ahmad ibn Mani said: Shababah的h narrated to us, Fudayl ibn Marzuq narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Khalid, from a man of the Ansar, who said: We accompanied Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, and he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Leave my in-laws and my Companions; whoever preserves me concerning them, a preserver from Allah will be with him, and whoever does not preserve me concerning them, Allah will withdraw from him, and whoever Allah withdraws from, He is about to seize him."
"4982","130 - Chapter on the excellence of the first generations. 4174/1 - al-Tayalisi said: Hammad ibn Yazid narrated to us, from Muawiyah ibn Qurrah al-Muzani, who said: I came to Madinah in the time of dried curd and clarified butter, and the Bedouins would come with skins drawing water with them, when I saw a man with lofty gaze looking at people, and I thought he was a stranger. So I drew near and greeted him, and he returned the greeting to me, and said to me: Are you of the people of Madinah? I said: Yes. I sat with him and said: From whom are you? He said: From Banu Hilal, and my name is Kahmas. Then he said to me: Shall I not narrate to you a hadith I witnessed from Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him? I said: Yes. He said: While we were sitting with him — and he mentioned the story — then Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The best of my nation is the generation of which I am part, then the second, then the third; then a people will arise whose oaths precede their testimony; they testify without being asked to testify, and they have clamour in their markets. Muawiyah said: Kahmas said: Do you fear these are of those? And he mentioned the hadith. Part of it has preceded in Fasting, and part in Marriage. Its chain is strong. 4174/2 - Ibn Abi Asim narrated it from Yunus ibn Habib, from Abu Dawud with it. 4174/3 - al-Bukhari narrated it in his Tarikh and Samawayh in his Fawaid from Musa ibn Ismail from Hammad ibn Yazid likewise. 4174/4 - al-Hakim brought it in al-Kuna through Musa with it."
"4983","4175 - Abu Yala said: Dawud ibn Rushayd narrated to us, Baqiyyah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Irq al-Himsi, from Abdullah ibn Busr, the Companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Be upright and rejoice, for Allah the Exalted is not swift to your punishment; and there will come a people who have no proof."
"4984","4176 - And with it: Glad tidings to whoever saw me and believed in me, and glad tidings to whoever believed in me and did not see me; glad tidings to them and a good return."
"4985","4177/1 - al-Tayalisi said: Talhah — he is Ibn Amr — narrated to us, from Nafi, who said: A man came to Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, did you look at the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with your eyes? He said: Yes. He said: And did you speak to him with these tongues of yours? He said: Yes. He said: And did you pledge to him with these right hands of yours? He said: Yes. He said: Glad tidings to you, O Abu Abd al-Rahman. He said: Shall I not inform you of something I heard from him? I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Glad tidings to whoever saw me and believed in me, and glad tidings to whoever believed in me and did not see me — three times."
"4986","4177/2 - Abd said: Abu Nuaym narrated to us, Talhah ibn Amr narrated to us with it."
"4987","4178/1 - Ibn Abi Umar said: al-Muqri narrated to us, Ibn Lahiah narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, who said: Abu al-Khayr informed him that a man of Juhaynah informed him: That two men came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he looked at them and said: Two Madhhijis or two Kindis. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Rather two Kindis. They came to him, and they were two Kindis. One of them said: What think you, O Messenger of Allah, of one who follows what you were sent with, affirms you, and did not see you? He said: Glad tidings to him, then glad tidings to him."
"4988","4178/2 - Abu Bakr said: Muhammad ibn Ubayd narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Marthad ibn Abdullah, from Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Juhani, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: While we were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), two riders appeared. When he saw them he said: Two Kindis, two Madhhijis, until they came, and they were two men of Madhhij. He said: One of them drew near to him to pledge to him; when he took his hand he said: O Messenger of Allah, what think you of one who believed in you, affirmed you and followed you — what is for him? He said: Glad tidings to him. He wiped over his hand and he departed. Then the other came until he took his hand to pledge to him, and said: O Messenger of Allah, what think you of one who believed in you, affirmed you and followed you, and did not see you? He said: Glad tidings to him. Then he wiped over his hand and he departed."
"4989","131 - Chapter on the excellence of this nation. 4179 - Ishaq said: Yala ibn Ubayd informed us, Abu Sinan narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Malik, from Makhul, who said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, had a right over a Jewish man, so he came to him seeking it, and met him. Umar said: No, by the One Who chose Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) above mankind, I shall not leave you while I seek something from you. The Jew said: By Allah, Allah did not choose Muhammad above mankind. Umar slapped him, and he said: Between me and you is Abu al-Qasim. He said: Umar said: No, by the One Who chose Muhammad above mankind. I said: By Allah, Allah did not choose Muhammad above mankind, so he slapped me. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: As for you, O Umar, satisfy him for his slap. Rather, O Jew: Adam is the chosen of Allah, Ibrahim is the beloved friend of Allah, Musa is the confidant of Allah, Isa is the spirit of Allah, and I am the beloved of Allah. Rather, O Jew: Allah, mighty and majestic, named Himself with two names with which He named my nation: He is al-Salam, and He named my nation with it the Muslims; and He is al-Mumin, and He named my nation with it the believers. Rather, O Jew: You sought a day that was stored for us: Ours is today, yours is tomorrow, and the day after tomorrow is for the Christians. Rather, O Jew: You are the first and we are the last, the foremost on the Day of Resurrection. Rather, Paradise is forbidden to the prophets until I enter it, and it is forbidden to the nations until my nation enters it."
"4990","4180 - Abu Bakr said: Surayj ibn al-Numan narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz ibn Abdullah narrated to us, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Yazid, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I asked my Lord, mighty and majestic, for my nation apart from mankind that He not punish them, and He granted it to me."
"4991","4181 - al-Harith said: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us, Maysarah ibn Abd Rabbihi narrated to us, from Abu Aishah al-Sadi, from Yazid ibn Umar, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abu Hurayrah and Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us; he commanded Bilal, who called out that prayer is congregational before he spoke, and the people gathered to him, and he ascended the pulpit and said: O people, draw near and make room for those behind you — three times. The people drew near and pressed together, and turned around and saw no one. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Draw near and make room for those behind you. The people drew near and pressed together, and turned around and saw no one. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said that the third time, and they saw no one. A man stood and said: For whom shall we make room? For the angels? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No; when they are with you they are not in front of you nor behind you, but on your right and your left. He said: And they are not in front of us nor behind us — are they better than us? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Rather you are better than the angels. Sit down — and he sat."
"4992","4182 - Ibn Abi Umar said: al-Muqri narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad ibn Anum narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Yazid, from Abdullah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The likeness of my nation is like rain; it is not known whether its beginning is more beneficial or its end."
"4993","4183 - Abu Yala said: Shayban — he is Ibn Farrukh — narrated to us, Harb ibn Surayj narrated to us, Zaynab bint Yazid al-Atakiyyah narrated to me, she said: I heard Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The past nations are one hundred nations; when they testify good for a servant, Paradise becomes due for him; and my nation — the fiftieth of them is a nation — when they testify good for a servant, Paradise becomes due for him."
"4994","4184 - Ubayd ibn Jannad al-Halabi narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi al-Rijal narrated to us, from Ishaq ibn Yahya ibn Talhah, who said: Thabit al-Araj said to me: Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, informed me, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: This nation will remain in good as long as, when it speaks, it speaks truthfully, and when it judges, it judges justly, and when mercy is sought from it, it shows mercy."
"4995","4185 - Wahb ibn Baqiyyah narrated to us, Khalid narrated to us, from Yunus, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Abu Burdah, from a man of the Muhajirun, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The punishment of this nation is by the sword."
"4996","4186 - Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Abu Dawud al-Hafari narrated to us, Ibn Abi Zaidah narrated to us, from Sad ibn Tariq, from Abu Hazim, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: This nation is shown mercy; there is no punishment upon it except what it inflicts upon itself. I said: How does it punish itself? He said: Was the Day of al-Nahr not a punishment? Was the Day of the Camel not a punishment? Was the Day of Siffin not a punishment?"
"4997","132 - Chapter on the excellence of the people of Yemen. 4187 - Abd said: Ibrahim ibn al-Hakam ibn Aban narrated to us, my father narrated to me, Abdullah ibn Isa ibn Abdullah ibn Buhayr narrated to me: My father narrated to me that he was in the mosque when Amr ibn al-As, may Allah be pleased with him, appeared, and he said to me: Are you of the people of Yemen? I said: Yes. He said to me: Draw near to me and I shall narrate to you a hadith that will delight your eye. He said: I drew near to him, and Amr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: While we were one day sitting, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came and sat with us, then said: Where are my brothers of whom I am part and who are part of me; I shall enter Paradise and they will enter with me? Then he rose and went away, and it was not long before he returned and sat, then said: Where are my brothers of whom I am part and who are part of me; I shall enter Paradise and they will enter with me? Then he rose and went away, and some of us said to others: Would that we ask him: Are they other than us, O Messenger of Allah? It was only a little before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned and said: Where are my brothers of whom I am part and who are part of me; I shall enter Paradise and they will enter Paradise with me? We said: O Messenger of Allah, are they other than us? He said: Yes; they are the people of Yemen, cast aside at the edges of the earth, repelled from the doors of rulers; one of them dies with his need in his breast, never fulfilling it."
"4998","4188/1 - Abu Yala said: Ismail ibn Musa narrated to us, Husayn ibn Isa narrated to us, from Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Abu Hazim, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: While the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in Madinah, he said: Allah is Greatest, Allah is Greatest; the help of Allah and the victory have come, and the people of Yemen have come. It was said: O Messenger of Allah, who are the people of Yemen? He said: A people whose hearts are tender and whose obedience is gentle; faith is Yemeni and wisdom is Yemeni."
"4999","4188/2 - al-Bazzar said: Abdullah ibn Said narrated to us, Husayn narrated to us, and he mentioned it. He said: al-Zuhri did not narrate from Abu Hazim except this. I say: Husayn is weak."
"5000","133 - Chapter on the excellence of the non-Arabs and Persia. 4189/1 - Ishaq said: Jarir informed us, from al-Amash, from al-Minhal, from a man, who said: I was in the mosque on Friday while Ali ibn Abi Talib was preaching upon a pulpit of brick, and behind me was Sasaa ibn Sawhan, when a man spoke to him about something hidden from us, and we recognized anger in his face, and he fell silent. Then al-Ashath ibn Qays came, stepping over the necks of people until he was near, and said: O Commander of the Faithful, these red ones have overcome us in your presence. Sasaa struck between my shoulders with his hand and said: To Allah we belong and to Him we return; today he will reveal concerning the Arabs a matter he used to conceal. He said: He grew angry and said: Who will excuse me from these fat ones? One of them rolls upon his cushions and forsakes peoples who remember Allah, mighty and majestic, then commands me to drive them away and be of the wrongdoers. By the One Who split the grain and created the soul, I heard Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: By Allah, they will strike you back to the religion as you struck them to it at first. Ishaq said: Other than Jarir named him Abbad ibn Abdullah al-Asadi. I say: It is as he said."
"5001","4189/2 - al-Harith said: Abu al-Nadr narrated to us, Abu Muawiyah narrated to us, from al-Amash, from al-Minhal ibn Amr, from Abbad ibn Abdullah al-Asadi, who said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, was preaching while the freedmen had surrounded him, and he mentioned the hadith in full."
"5002","4189/3 - Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah brought it from Sharik, from al-Amash, from al-Minhal, from Abbad ibn Abdullah."
"5003","4189/4 - Abu Yala said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us with it."
"5004","4190/1 - Abu Bakr said: Ibn Uyaynah narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Najih, from his father, from Qays ibn Sad, may Allah be pleased with him — as a narration — who said: If faith were suspended from the Pleiades, men of the people of Persia would attain it."
"5005","4190/2 - Abu Yala said: Abu Bakr and Harun ibn Maruf narrated to us; both said: Sufyan narrated to us with it. al-Bazzar narrated it and stated it raised, and said in it: And perhaps he said: Of the sons of the red ones, the sons of the freedmen. Sound."
"5006","41 - Excellence of lands. 1 - Chapter on Asqalan. 4191 - Abu Yala said: Muhammad ibn Bakkar narrated to us, Attaf ibn Khalid narrated to us, my brother al-Miswar ibn Khalid narrated to me, from Ali ibn Abdullah ibn Buhaynah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: While the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was sitting among his Companions, he said: May Allah bless that graveyard — three times. No one asked him: Which graveyard is it? And he named nothing to them. Then one of his Companions entered upon one of his wives, as if she were Aishah, and said to her — then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered, and she asked him about it, and he said: It is a graveyard in Asqalan."
"5007","4192 - Muhammad ibn Bakkar narrated to us, Bashir ibn Maymun narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Yusuf, from Abdullah ibn Umar ibn al-Khattab, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while mentioning the people of a graveyard one day, bless them and bless them abundantly. He was asked about them and said: The people of the graveyard of Asqalan; they will be escorted to Paradise as a bride is escorted to her husband."
"5008","2 - Chapter on Basrah and Kufah. 4193/1 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad said in the additions of al-Zuhd: Umar ibn Shabbah narrated to me, Yahya ibn Bistam narrated to me, Unays ibn Sawwar narrated to me, Malik ibn Dinar narrated to us, from al-Ahnaf ibn Qays, who said: I came to Madinah during the rule of Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, when a man with a thick beard sat with them and spoke harshly, and they dispersed. I said: O servant of Allah, I see you have done ill. He said: These are flatterers. Do you know me? I said: No. He said: I am Abu Dharr. Who are you? I said: Of the people of Basrah. He said: Shall I not narrate to you a hadith I heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He says: Trials in Iraq, and that is in Kufah; as for the people of Basrah, they are the most upright of cities in qibla and the most numerous in muezzins; Allah wards off from them what they dislike."
"5009","4193/2 - Abu Ishaq al-Tabari narrated to us, Hashim ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, from Salih al-Murri, from Said al-Rabai, from Malik ibn Dinar, from al-Ahnaf, from Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned the people of Kufah, and mentioned that grave trials will descend upon them. Then he mentioned the people of Basrah, and mentioned that they are the best people of the cities in qibla and the most numerous in muezzins; what they dislike is warded off from them."
"5010","4194 - al-Tayalisi said: Sallam narrated to us, from Said ibn Masruq, from Nuaym ibn Abi Hind, who said: Hudhayfah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I have not seen reed-huts except reed-huts that were with Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); nothing wards off from this — meaning Kufah. Abu Dawud said: Al-akhsas are houses of reed in our land."
"5011","3 - Chapter on the people of Egypt. 4195/1 - Abu Yala said: Zuhayr ibn Harb narrated to us, Abdullah ibn Yazid narrated to us, Haywah narrated to us, Abu Hani Humayd ibn Hani al-Khawlani informed me: That he heard Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Hubuli and Amr ibn Hurayth and others say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You will come upon a people with curly hair on their heads, so treat them well, for they are a strength for you and a conveyance to your enemy by the permission of Allah the Exalted — meaning the Copts of Egypt. 4195/2 - Ibn Hibban narrated it in his Sahih from Abu Yala. Abu Abd al-Rahman is a Follower without doubt, and Amr ibn Hurayth is not the Makhzumi, but another whose companionship is disputed."
"5012","4 - Chapter on the excellence of whoever of the Companions, may Allah be pleased with them, settled in Hims. 4196 - al-Harith said: Dawud ibn Rushayd narrated to us, Abu Haywah narrated to us, from Artaah, from Abu al-Dahhak, who said: I came to Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, and asked him about something of knowledge. He said: From whom are you? I said: Of the people of Syria. He said: Which people of Syria? I said: Of Hims. He said: From Hims you came seeking knowledge here? I said: What prevents me from seeking knowledge from one like you, while you are a Companion of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Then I inform you that the first detachments marched after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until they settled in Syria, then your army in particular; so see what they were upon, for it is the Sunnah."
"5013","5 - Chapter on the excellence of Syria. The hadith of al-Nawwas, may Allah be pleased with him: The stronghold of the believers abode is in Syria — it will come, Allah the Exalted willing, in the Tribulations. 4197 - Abu Yala said: Abd al-Jabbar ibn Asim Abu Talib narrated to us, Ismail ibn Ayyash al-Himsi narrated to us, from al-Walid ibn Abbad, from Amir al-Ahwal, from Abu Salih al-Khawlani, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: A group of my nation will continue fighting at the gates of Damascus and around it, and at the gates of Bayt al-Maqdis and around it; whoever forsakes them will not harm them, manifest upon the truth until the Hour comes."
"5014","4198 - al-Harith said: Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated to us, Abu Ishaq — he is al-Fazari — narrated to us, from Said ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Abu Idris, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You will raise armies — the hadith concerning the excellence of Syria."
"5015","4199 - Said said, and Ibn Halbas narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I saw the pillar of the Book taken from beneath my pillow, and I followed it with my sight, and it was a radiant light directed to Syria; indeed faith, when tribulations occur, is in Syria."
"5016","4200 - Abu Yala said: Zuhayr narrated to us, Waki narrated to us, from Said ibn Abd al-Aziz, from Rabiah ibn Yazid, from a man called Khawli, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You will raise armies: an army in Syria and an army in Yemen. Khawli said to him: O Messenger of Allah, choose for me. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Upon you is Syria; whoever refuses, let him join his Yemen and drink from his ponds, for Allah the Exalted has guaranteed me Syria and its people."
"5017","4201 - Ishaq said: Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Muhammad ibn Muslim narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah: I heard Said ibn al-Musayyab say: Wajj is a sacred valley. I say: It is with fathah on the waw and shaddah on the jim, and it is at al-Taif."
"5018","6 - Excellence of al-Taif. 4202 - al-Humaydi said: Abdullah ibn al-Harith ibn Abd al-Malik narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Insan narrated to me, from Abdullah ibn Abd Rabbihi ibn al-Hakam ibn Uthman, from Abu Bakr ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Harith ibn Hisham, from Kab, who said: I heard him say: Wajj is sacred since the Lord ascended to heaven the day He decreed creation. al-Humaydi said: Wajj is at al-Taif."
"5019","7 - Excellence of Naman. 4203 - Ibn Abi Umar said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Jurayj, from a man, from Talhah ibn Amr al-Hadrami, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: How excellent nurses are the people of Naman."
"5020","8 - Excellence of Makkah, may Allah honour it the Exalted. 4204 - Musaddad said: Khalid narrated to us, Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Sabit, from Ayyash ibn Abi Rabiah, who said: This nation will remain in good as long as they magnify this sanctuary with its true magnification."
"5021","42 - Book of Sirah and Military Expeditions. 1 - Chapter on the birth of our master the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 4205 - Ishaq said: Wahb ibn Jarir ibn Hazim informed us, my father narrated to us, he said: I heard Muhammad ibn Ishaq say: Salih ibn Ibrahim narrated to me, from Abdullah ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Asad ibn Zurarah, one I choose of the men of my people narrated to me, from Hassan ibn Thabit, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I was a young boy of seven or eight years, hearing what I see and understanding, when a Jew looked over a fortress crying at the top of his voice: O assembly of Jews. They gathered to him and said: What is with you? He said: Tonight has risen the star of Ahmad with which he was born. He said: I asked Said ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Hassan ibn Thabit: How old was Hassan ibn Thabit when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Madinah? He said: Sixty years old."
"5022","4206/1 - Wahb ibn Jarir ibn Hazim informed us, my father narrated to us, he said: I heard Muhammad ibn Ishaq say: Jahm ibn Abi al-Jahm narrated to me, from Abdullah ibn Jafar, or from one who narrated to him from Abdullah ibn Jafar, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was born, Halimah bint al-Harith came among women of Banu Sad ibn Bakr seeking sucklings in Makkah. Halimah said: I went out among the first women upon a white she-ass of mine, with my husband al-Harith ibn Abd al-Uzza, one of Banu Sad ibn Bakr, then of Banu Nasirah; our she-ass had grown weak, and with the riders was an old she-camel that, by Allah, did not yield a drop of milk in a rainless year; people had hungered until hardship reached them. With me was a son of mine who, by Allah, would not sleep our night, and I found nothing in my breasts to soothe him with, except that we hoped for rain; and we had sheep we hoped in. When we came to Makkah, none of us remained but the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was offered to her and she disliked him. We said: He is an orphan; only the father honours the wet-nurse and treats her well. We said: What can his mother or his uncle or his grandfather do for us? Every one of my companions took a suckling and I found nothing. When I found none but him I returned to him and took him; by Allah I took him only because I found none else. I said to my companion: By Allah, I shall take the orphan of Banu Abd al-Muttalib; perhaps Allah will benefit us through him, and I shall not return among my companions taking nothing. He said: You are right. She said: I took him and brought him to the baggage; by Allah, no sooner had I brought him to the baggage than in the evening my breasts flowed with milk until I satiated him and satiated his brother, and his father rose to that old she-camel of ours feeling her, and she was full, so he milked her and satiated me and was satiated. He said: O Halimah, know, by Allah, you have taken a blessed soul; Allah the Exalted has granted them what we did not hope for. She said: We spent the best night, satiated, whereas we would not sleep our night with our boy. Then in the morning we returned to our land, I and my companions; I rode my white she-ass carrying him with me, and by the One in Whose Hand is the soul of Halimah, I outstripped the riders, until the women said: Hold back for us — is this your she-ass you went out upon? I said: Yes. They said: She had grown weak when we came; what is with her? She said: I said: By Allah, I carried upon her a blessed boy. She said: We went out, and Allah the Exalted kept increasing us in good every day, until we arrived while the land was in drought. Our shepherds would drive out then bring back, and the sheep of Banu Sad would return hungry while my sheep would return satiated, full-sided, full-uddered, milked and drunk from. They would say: What is with the sheep of al-Harith ibn Abd al-Uzza and the sheep of Halimah that they return satiated and full while your sheep return hungry? Woe to you, pasture where their shepherds pasture. So they pastured with them, yet theirs returned only hungry as before, while mine returned as before. She said: He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) grew as none of the boys grow: growing in a day as a boy grows in a month, and in a month as in a year. When he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) completed two years, we brought him to Makkah, I and his father, and said: By Allah, we shall never part from him while we can. When we came to his mother we said to her: O wet-nurse, by Allah we have never seen a boy greater in blessing than him, and we fear for him the epidemic of Makkah and its sicknesses, so leave him; we shall return with him until you recover from your ailment. We kept at her until she permitted, and we returned with him. We stayed three or four months, when while he was playing behind the houses, he and his brother, among sheep of theirs, his brother came running, while I and his father were in the house, and said: My Qurashi brother — two men in white clothes came to him, seized him, laid him down and split his belly. I and his father went out running and found him standing, his colour changed. When he saw us he sobbed to us and wept. She said: I and his father embraced him and pressed him to us, and said: What is with you, may my father and mother be ransom for you? He said: Two men came to me, laid me down and split my belly, and did something with it, then returned it as it was. His father said: By Allah, I see my son has been afflicted; join his family and return him to them before there appears in him what is feared. She said: We carried him and came to his mother. When she saw us she disapproved of our affair and said: What brought you back with him before I asked you for him, when you were keen to keep him? We said: Nothing, except that Allah the Exalted has decreed the suckling complete, and we were pleased with what we see, and we said returning him to you as you love is dearer to us. He said: I said: You have some affair; inform me what it is. She did not leave us until we informed her. She said: No, by Allah, Allah will not do that with him; my son has an affair — shall I not inform you of his news? I carried him, and by Allah I never carried a pregnancy lighter upon me than him nor easier; then I saw when I carried him that a light came out of me by which the necks of camels in Busra were lit, or she said: the palaces of Busra. Then I bore him when I bore him, and by Allah he did not fall as boys fall; he fell leaning upon his hand on the ground, raising his head to heaven. Leave him to you both. She took him and we departed."
"5023","4206/2 - Yahya ibn Adam informed us, Ibn Idris informed us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, Jahm ibn Abi Jahm narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Jafar, or from one who narrated to him from Abdullah ibn Jafar, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: Halimah bint al-Harith, the mother of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), al-Sadiyyah, said: I came among a party of Banu Sad ibn Bakr seeking sucklings in Makkah — and he mentioned likewise."
"5024","4206/3 - Abu Yala said: Masruq ibn al-Marzuban and al-Hasan ibn Hammad narrated to us, and I copied it from the hadith of Masruq, Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Zaidah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated to us, from Jahm ibn Abi al-Jahm, from Abdullah ibn Jafar, may Allah be pleased with them both, from Halimah bint al-Harith, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) — and he mentioned it at length."
"5025","4207 - Abu Yala said: Yahya ibn Umar ibn al-Numan al-Shami narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Yala al-Kufi narrated to us, Umar ibn Subh narrated to us, from Thawr ibn Yazid, from Makhul, from Shaddad ibn Aws, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: While we were sitting with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), a man of Banu Amir came to him, the chief of his people, their elder and their spokesman, leaning upon a staff. He stood before the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and traced the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to his grandfather, and said: O son of Abd al-Muttalib, I was told you are the Messenger of Allah to people, sent with what Ibrahim, Musa, Isa and others of the prophets were sent with. You have uttered a grave thing: prophets and kings were in two houses of the Children of Israel, a house of prophethood and a house of kingship, and you are of neither of these; you are of the Arabs who worship stones and idols — what have you to do with prophethood? Every matter has a reality, so bring me the reality of your saying and the beginning of your affair. He said: His question pleased the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then he said: The account you ask about has a report and a sitting, so sit. He folded his leg and knelt as a camel kneels. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: O brother of Banu Amir, the reality of my saying and the beginning of my affair is the supplication of my father Ibrahim, and my brother Isa son of Maryam gave glad tidings of me. I was a firstborn to my mother, and she carried me as the heaviest of what women carry, until she complained to her companions of the heaviness she felt. My mother saw in sleep that what was in her belly was a light. She said: I kept following the light with my sight, and the light kept outstripping my sight until the easts of the earth and its wests were lit for me. Then she bore me. When I grew up, idols were made hateful to me, and poetry was made hateful to me. I was suckled among Banu Jusham ibn Bakr. While I was one day in the belly of a valley with peers of mine among boys, three men came with a basin of gold full of light and snow. They took me from among my companions, and my companions fled until they reached the edge of the valley, then turned to the men and said: What have you to do with this boy? He is a boy not of us; he is of the sons of the chief of Quraysh, suckled among us, an orphan boy with no father. What will killing him bring you? If you must do it, choose any of us you wish; we shall come to you, so take us in his place and leave this boy. They did not answer them. When the boys saw the people would not answer them, they fled swiftly to the tribe informing them and calling them for help against the men. One of them turned to me and laid me gently upon the ground, then split between my chest to the end of my pubic region while I looked, and I felt nothing of it. Then he took out the bowels of my belly and washed them with that snow, washing thoroughly, then returned them to their place. Then the second stood and said to his companion: Step aside. Then he put his hand into my belly and took out my heart while I looked; he split it and took out a black clot and cast it away. Then with his hand to his right as if taking something, he brought a seal in his hand of the light of prophethood and wisdom, dazzling the eyes of beholders, and sealed my heart, and it filled with light; he sealed it then returned it to its place, and I felt the coolness of that seal in my heart for ages. Then the third stood, moved his companion aside, and passed his hand between my breasts and the end of my pubic region, and that split closed by the permission of Allah the Exalted. Then he took my hand and raised me gently from my place. Then the first who split my belly said: Weigh him against ten of his nation. They weighed me and I outweighed them. Then he said: Weigh him against a hundred of his nation. They weighed me and I outweighed them. Then he said: Weigh him against a thousand of his nation. They weighed me and I outweighed them. He said: Leave him; if you weighed him against his whole nation he would outweigh them. Then they stood to me, pressed me to their chests, kissed my head and between my eyes, and said: O beloved, fear not; if you knew what good is intended for you your eye would be delighted. He said: While we were thus, the tribe came in full, with my wet-nurse at their front crying at the top of her voice, saying: O my weak one. He said: They crowded over me kissing me and saying: How lovely you are as a weak one. Then she said: O my lonely one. He said: They crowded over me kissing me and saying: How lovely you are as a lonely one; you are not lonely: Allah is with you and His angels and the believers of the people of earth. Then she said: O my orphan one; you were deemed weak among your companions and killed for your weakness. They crowded over me, pressed me to their chests and kissed my head, and said: How lovely you are as an orphan; how honoured you are to Allah, mighty and majestic, if you knew what good is intended for you. He said: They reached the edge of the valley, and when my wet-nurse saw me she said: O my son, do I still see you alive? She came until she bent over me and pressed me to her chest; by the One in Whose Hand is my soul, I was in her lap with her pressing me to her while my hand was in the hand of one of them, and I thought the people saw them, yet they did not see them. One of the tribe came and said: This boy is touched by madness or a visitation of jinn; take him to the soothsayer to look at him and treat him. I said to him: Man, there is nothing in me of what you mention; I see myself sound and my heart healthy, with no ailment. My father — the husband of my wet-nurse — said: Do you not see my son speaks soundly? I hope my son has no harm. The people agreed to take me to the soothsayer, so they carried me to him and told him my story. He said: Be silent until I hear from the boy, for he knows his affair best. I told him my affair from first to last. When he heard my saying he pressed me to his chest and called at the top of his voice: O Arabs, kill this boy and kill me with him; by al-Lat and al-Uzza, if you leave him he will change your religion, deem foolish your minds and the minds of your fathers, oppose your affair, and bring you a religion the like of which you never heard. He said: My wet-nurse snatched me from his hand. He said: You are more foolish and madder than him; had I known this would be your saying I would not have brought him to you. Then they carried me and returned me to my family. In the morning I was consoled for what was done to me, and the trace of the split between my chest to the end of my pubic region remained like a shoelace. That is the reality of my saying and the beginning of my affair. The Amiri said: I bear witness there is no god but Allah, and your affair is true; inform me of things I ask you about. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Ask what concerns you. He used to say to questioners before that: Ask whatever occurs to you; that day he said to the Amiri: Ask what concerns you, speaking to him in the dialect of Banu Amir, speaking to him with what he knows. The Amiri said: Inform me, O son of Abd al-Muttalib, what increases evil? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Persistence. He said: Does righteousness benefit after wickedness? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes; repentance washes away sin; good deeds remove evil deeds, and when the servant remembers his Lord in ease He aids him in affliction. He said: How is that? He said: That is because Allah the Exalted says: I do not combine for My servant two securities, nor do I combine for him two fears. He said: To what do you call? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I call to the worship of Allah the Exalted alone with no partner, to cast off rivals, disbelieve in al-Lat and al-Uzza, affirm what has come from Allah the Exalted of Book and Messenger, pray the five prayers with their realities, fast a month of the year, pay the alms of your wealth so Allah the Exalted purifies you with it and your wealth becomes wholesome for you, and affirm the resurrection after death, and Paradise and Fire. He said: O son of Abd al-Muttalib, if I do that, what is for me? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Gardens of Eden beneath which rivers flow. He said: Is there with this anything of this world, for comfortable living pleases us? The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes; victory and establishment in the lands. He said: The Amiri answered and turned back."
"5026","2 - Chapter on the love of Abd al-Muttalib, his grandfather, for him, and his blessing (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his childhood. 4208 - Abu Yala said: Wahb ibn Baqiyyah narrated to us, Khalid informed us, from Dawud, from Abbas, from Kindir ibn Said, from his father, who said: I performed pilgrimage in the pre-Islamic time, when a man was circumambulating the House chanting verse. I said: Who is this? They said: This is Abd al-Muttalib ibn Hashim; camels of his strayed, and he sent a son of his after them seeking them, and he was detained from him; he never sent him on a need but he brought it. He said: I did not move until Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came bringing the camels. He said: O my son, by Allah, I grieved for you this time with a grief; never leave me again. al-Hakim brought it likewise, saying at its end: I was distressed for you, O my son, with a distress I never felt for anything; by Allah, I shall never send you on a need again, nor shall you leave me after this ever."
"5027","3 - Chapter on the precedence of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and the nobility of his origin. 4209 - Ibn Abi Umar said: Umar ibn Khalid narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abdullah narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn al-Furat, from Uthman ibn al-Dahhak, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: Quraysh was a light before Allah, mighty and majestic, two thousand years before He created Adam; that light glorified and the angels glorified with its glorification. When Allah created Adam He placed that light in his loins. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah sent it down to earth in the loins of Adam, placed it in the loins of Nuh in the Ark, cast into the Fire in the loins of Ibrahim; He kept moving me from the loins of the noble to the wombs, until He brought me forth from between my parents, who never met in fornication ever."
"5028","4210 - He said: Muhammad ibn Jafar ibn Muhammad narrated to us, he said: I bear witness upon my father that he said: He narrated to me, from his father, from his grandfather, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I came out of marriage and did not come out of fornication, from Adam until my father and mother bore me; nothing of the fornication of the pre-Islamic time touched me."
"5029","4211 - Sufyan narrated to us, from Jafar ibn Muhammad, from his father, who said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Every cause and lineage is cut off except my lineage and my cause. The hadith of al-Miswar is in the virtues of Fatimah, may Allah be pleased with her."
"5030","4 - Chapter on the protection by Allah, Blessed and Exalted, of His Messenger Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) before the mission. 4212 - Ishaq said: Wahb ibn Jarir informed us, my father narrated to us, from Ibn Ishaq, Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Qays ibn Makhramah narrated to me, from al-Hasan ibn Muhammad ibn Ali ibn Abi Talib, from his father, from his grandfather Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: I never intended an ill deed of what the people of the pre-Islamic time intended, except twice in time, and in both Allah the Exalted protected me from them. I said one night to a youth with me of Quraysh in the upper part of Makkah, pasturing sheep of its people: Watch my sheep until I spend this night in Makkah as youths spend the evening. He said: Yes. I went out until I came to the nearest house of the houses of Makkah; I heard singing and beating of tambourines and pipes. I said: What is this? They said: So-and-so married so-and-so — a man of Quraysh married a woman of Quraysh. I was diverted by that singing and that sound until my eye overcame me, and nothing woke me but the touch of the sun. I returned to my companion. He said: What did you do? I informed him. Then I said to him another night likewise, and he did so. I went out and heard the like, and the like of what was said to me was said to me; I was diverted by what I heard until my eye overcame me, and nothing woke me but the touch of the sun. Then I returned to my companion. He said: What did you do? I said: I did nothing. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: By Allah, I never intended after them an evil of what the people of the pre-Islamic time do, until Allah, mighty and majestic, honoured me with His prophethood. I say: Thus Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated it in the Sirah, and this route is good and grave; I have not seen it in any of the Musnads except in this Musnad of Ishaq, and it is a good connected hadith, its men trustworthy."
"5031","4213 - al-Tayalisi said: Amr ibn Thabit narrated to us, from Simak, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both; and Talhah, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I was forbidden nudity, and that was before prophethood descended upon him."
"5032","5 - Chapter on his (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) witnessing the gatherings of the polytheists before the mission, disapproving of them. 4214 - Abu Yala said: Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Jarir narrated to us, from al-Thawri, from Ibn Aqil, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) used to attend with the polytheists their gatherings. He said: He heard two angels behind him, one of them saying to his companion: Let us go stand behind the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: How shall we stand behind him when his contact but recently was with touching idols? He never again attended with the polytheists their gatherings. I say: People criticised this hadith against Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah at length; the objectionable in it is his saying from the angel that he said: His contact was with touching idols, for its outward meaning is that he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) directly touched; that is not intended — rather what is intended is that the angel disapproved of his witnessing the polytheists touching their idols."
"5033","4215 - Abu Bakr said: Yala ibn Ubayd narrated to us, Salih ibn Hayyan narrated to us, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Jibril, peace be upon him, entered the Sacred Mosque and began turning about; he saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sleeping in the shade of the Kaaba, so he woke him. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rose shaking dust from his head and beard, and he took him toward the gate of Banu Shaybah; Mika'il met them both, and Jibril said to Mika'il: What prevented you from shaking hands with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: I find upon his hand the smell of brass. It was as if Jibril, peace be upon him, disapproved of that, and said: Did you do that? It was as if the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forgot, then remembered and said: My brother spoke the truth; the day before yesterday I passed by Isaf and Nailah and placed my hand upon one of them, and said: A people who are pleased with you two as gods besides Allah are an evil people. The hadith of Zayd ibn Harithah, may Allah be pleased with him, has preceded in the virtues of Zayd ibn Amr ibn Nufayl, may Allah be pleased with him, that he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was forbidden in the pre-Islamic time from touching idols."
"5034","6 - Chapter on the description of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 4216 - Abu Dawud said: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, from Salih the freedman of al-Tawamah, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was broad in the forearms, wide between the shoulders, with long eyelashes; he was not loud-voiced in the markets, nor obscene nor deliberately obscene; he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would turn wholly and withdraw wholly."
"5035","4217 - Abu Yala said: Yaqub ibn Ibrahim al-Nukri narrated to us, Uthman ibn Umar narrated to us, Harb ibn Surayj narrated to us: A man of Baladawiyyah narrated to me, my grandfather narrated to me, who said: I set out to Madinah and alighted at the valley, when two men had one goat between them, and the buyer was saying to the seller: Deal with me well. It was not long before the buyer called and said: O Messenger of Allah, tell him to deal with me well. He extended his hand and said: You own your wealth; I hope from Allah the Exalted on the Day of Resurrection that none of you will demand of me anything of one I wronged in wealth, blood or honour, except with its right. May Allah have mercy upon a man easy in selling, easy in buying, easy in taking, easy in giving, easy in settling, easy in demanding. Then he went on. I said to myself: Is this the Hashimi who misled people? It is he. I looked, and he was a man handsome in body, large in forehead, fine in nose, fine in eyebrows, with from the hollow of his throat to his navel like a black thread of black hair, wearing two worn garments. He said: He drew near us and said: Peace be upon you. We returned it upon him. It was not long before I said: By Allah, I shall follow this man, for he speaks well. I followed him and said: O Muhammad. He turned to me wholly and said: What do you wish? I said: Are you the one who misled people, destroyed them and barred them from what their fathers worshipped? He said: That is Allah. I said: To what do you call? He said: I call the servants of Allah to Allah. He said: I said: What do you say? He said: Do you bear witness there is no god but Allah and Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, believe in what was revealed to me, disbelieve in al-Lat and al-Uzza, establish prayer, and pay alms? He said: I said: What is alms? He said: Our rich returns upon our poor. He said: I said: An excellent thing you call to. He said: There was none on earth breathing more hateful to me than him, and he did not move until he became dearer to me than my child, my parents and all people. He said: I said: I have understood. He said: Have you understood? I said: Yes. He said: Do you bear witness there is no god but Allah and Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah, and believe in what was revealed to me? I said: Yes, O Messenger of Allah; I frequent a water with many people upon it, so I shall call them to what you called me to, hoping they will follow you? He said: Yes; call them. So the people of that water accepted Islam, men and women, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wiped his head."
"5036","4218 - Musaddad said: Abu Awanah narrated to us, from Abu Sa'id al-Shami, who said: I entered with my master upon one of the wives of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and she brought out to us red hair, and said: This is the hair of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"5037","7 - Chapter on the building of the Kaaba. 4219/1 - Ishaq said: al-Nadr ibn Shumayl informed us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from Khalid ibn Ararah, and he mentioned a story in it: Then he narrated, meaning Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Ibrahim, peace and blessings be upon him, was commanded to build the House, and it straitened him, and he knew not how to build. Allah, mighty and majestic, sent down tranquillity — a swift wind — which encircled for him like a shield, and he built upon it. Each day he would build one course — meaning a structure — while Makkah was intensely hot. When he, peace be upon him, reached the place of the Stone, he said to Ismail, peace and blessings be upon him: Go seek a stone. Ismail, peace be upon him, went roaming the mountains, and Jibril, peace be upon him, descended with the Stone. Ismail, peace be upon him, came and said: From where is this? He said: From One Who does not rely upon my building and your building. He placed it. Then it collapsed and the Amalekites built it; then it collapsed and Jurhum built it; then it collapsed and Quraysh built it. When they wanted to place the Stone they disputed over it, and said: The first to come out of this gate, the gate of Banu Shaybah. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out, and they said: This is the Trustworthy. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered a garment, spread it, placed it in it, ordered a man of every tribe, each took a side of the garment and lifted it, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took it and placed it."
"5038","4219/2 - al-Tayalisi said: Hammad, and Qays — he is Ibn al-Rabi — and Sallam — he is Abu al-Ahwas — all narrated, from Simak ibn Harb, from Khalid ibn Ararah, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: When the House was demolished after Jurhum, Quraysh built it. When they wanted to place the Stone they disputed who would place it, and agreed the first to enter through this gate would place it. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered through the gate of Banu Shaybah; he ordered a garment, placed the Stone in its middle, ordered each clan to take a side of the garment, they lifted it, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took it."
"5039","4219/3 - Abu Bakr said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, from Simak, from Khalid ibn Ararah, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: When they wanted to raise the Stone, meaning Quraysh, they disputed over it, and said: We shall arbitrate between us the first man to come out of this lane. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was the first to come out to them; they placed it in a cloak, then all the tribes together lifted it, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was then a young man — meaning before the mission."
"5040","4219/4 - al-Harith said: al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl al-Azraq narrated to us, at Baghdad by dictation, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from Khalid, who said: A man said to Ali, may Allah be pleased with him: Inform me of its building. He said: Allah the Exalted revealed to Ibrahim to build for Him a House. He said: Ibrahim, peace be upon him, was straitened. Allah the Exalted sent a wind called al-Sakinah, also called al-Khajuj, with two eyes and a head. Allah the Exalted revealed to Ibrahim to march when it marches and halt when it halts. It marched until it reached the place of the House and encircled it like a shield; it is opposite the Frequented House, which seventy thousand angels enter each day, never returning to it until the Day of Resurrection. Ibrahim and Ismail began building one course each day; when heat pressed them they shaded in the mountain shade. When they reached the place of the Stone, Ibrahim said to Ismail: Bring me a stone I shall place as a sign for people. Ismail faced the valley and brought a stone; Ibrahim deemed it small and cast it away, and said: Bring me another. Ismail went, and Jibril descended upon Ibrahim with the Black Stone. Ismail came, and Ibrahim said to him: One has come to me who did not entrust me to your stone. He said: He built the House, and they began circumambulating it, circumambulating and praying, until they died and passed away; the House collapsed, and the Amalekites built it; they circumambulated it until they died and passed away; then Quraysh built it. When they reached the place of the Stone they differed over placing it, and said: The first to appear from the gate — the hadith."
"5041","4220 - Ishaq said: Abd al-Razzaq narrated to us, Mamar informed us, from Ibn Khuthaym, from Abu al-Tufayl, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Kaaba in the pre-Islamic time was built of piled stones with no clay, about as high as a young goat could leap, not feared — its cloths were only placed upon it then let hang down over it. The Black Corner was placed upon its wall, exposed. It had two corners like the shape of a ring, square on one side and round on one side. A ship came from the land of Rome until, near Juddah, it broke; Quraysh went out to take its wood and found a Roman with it, so they took the wood and he gave it to them. The ship was bound for Abyssinia, and the Roman in the ship was a merchant. They brought the wood and brought the Roman. Quraysh said: We shall build with this wood the House of our Lord. When they wanted to demolish it, they found a serpent upon the wall of the House, white-bellied, black-backed; whenever anyone drew near the House to demolish it and take of its stones, it hastened to him opening its mouth. Quraysh gathered at the Station and cried out to Allah the Exalted. He said: They said: Our Lord, we were not frightened; we intended honouring Your House and adorning it — if You are pleased with that, otherwise do what seems to You. They heard an answer in the sky: a bird greater than an eagle, black-backed, white-bellied and legged, drove its talons into the belly of the serpent, then went off dragging it with its tail hanging, until it went with it toward Jiyad. Quraysh demolished it and began building it with stones of the valley, Quraysh carrying them upon their necks; they raised it into the sky twenty cubits. While the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was carrying its stones from Ajyad wearing a striped garment, the garment straitened upon him, part of it went upon his shoulder and his nakedness showed from the smallness of the garment, and he was called: O Muhammad, cover your nakedness. He was never seen bare after that. Between its building and what Allah revealed to him was fifteen years. When the army of al-Husayn ibn Numayr came — and he mentioned its burning in the time of Ibn al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with them both. Ibn Khuthaym said: Ibn Sabit informed me that when Ibn al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with them both, built it, they uncovered the foundations, and the stone in them was like a ring, interlocked together; when moved with a crowbar, what was on the other side moved. Ibn Sabit said: Zayd showed it to me after nightfall on a moonlit night. He said: I saw them like a ring with ends of each interlocked with another. Mamar said: Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad informed me, from Mujahid, who said: When they demolished the House in the pre-Islamic time until they reached the place of the Corner, a serpent came out against them as if its neck were the neck of a camel; people feared to draw near it, and a bird came shading half of Makkah, seized it with its feet, then soared with it until it cast it into the sea. Mujahid said: They went out one day, and a man removed a stone of the House and stole of the ornament of the House, then returned and stole, and the stone stuck upon his head."
"5042","4221 - Abu Yala said: Musa narrated to us from Muhammad ibn Abi al-Wazir, Yahya ibn al-Ala narrated to us, Shuayb ibn Khalid narrated to us, from Simak ibn Harb, from Ikrimah, who said: I heard Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, say: From his father al-Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: We used to carry stones to the House when Quraysh built it; men carried stones and women carried plaster — plaster is what is placed between rocks. al-Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I and my nephew Muhammad used to carry upon our necks, placing our waist-wrappers under the rock; when people came upon us we wrapped ourselves. While I and Muhammad was before me, he fell prostrate; I hastened to him and found him looking to heaven. I said to him: What is with you? He stood and wrapped himself, and said (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): I was forbidden to walk bare. al-Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I concealed that from people fearing they would deem it madness."
"5043","8 - Chapter on the mission. 4222/1 - al-Tayalisi said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, Abu Imran al-Jawni informed me, from a man, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Khadijah secluded themselves a month, coinciding with Ramadan. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out and heard: Peace be upon you. She said: He said: I thought it a sudden visitation of jinn. She said: Rejoice, for peace is good. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw one day Jibril, peace be upon him, upon the sun, one wing in the east and one wing in the west, and I feared him. She said: He set out toward his family, when Jibril, peace be upon him, stood between him and the door. He said: He spoke to me until I was comforted with him. Then he promised me an appointment, and I came to his appointment, but Jibril, peace be upon him, was held back from me. When he was about to return, there he was with Mika'il, peace be upon them both. Jibril, peace be upon him, descended to earth, while Mika'il remained between heaven and earth. He said: Jibril, peace be upon him, seized me and struck me for the sweetness of the nape, split my belly, took out of it what Allah the Exalted willed, washed it in a basin of gold, returned it therein, then tilted me as a vessel is tilted, then sealed in my back until I felt the touch of the seal. Then he said to me: Read in the name of your Lord. I had never read a book ever. He seized me by the throat until I sobbed weeping. Then he said: Read in the name of your Lord Who created, to His saying, mighty and majestic: What he knew not. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I never forgot anything after. Mika'il, peace be upon him, said: His nation followed him, by the Lord of the Kaaba. Until I came to my house; no stone nor tree met me but said: Peace be upon you, O Messenger of Allah, until I entered upon Khadijah, and she said: Peace be upon you, O Messenger of Allah."
"5044","4221/2 - al-Harith said: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us, Hammad narrated to us, from Abu Imran al-Jawni, from Yazid ibn Babanus, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) vowed to seclude himself a month, he and Khadijah, coinciding with the month of Ramadan. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out one night and heard: Peace be upon you. He said: I thought it a sudden visitation of jinn. I came hastening until I entered upon Khadijah, and she covered me with a garment, and said: What is with you, O son of Abdullah? And he mentioned the hadith likewise until he said: Until we reached five verses of it; I never forgot anything after. Then he weighed me against a man and I outweighed him, then weighed me against another and I outweighed him, until I was weighed against a hundred men. Mika'il, peace be upon him, said from above him: His nation followed him, by the Lord of the Kaaba."
"5045","4223 - Musaddad said: Abu al-Ahwas narrated to us, Said ibn Masruq narrated to us, from Abu al-Duha, from a man of Aslam, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was commissioned when he was forty-three years old."
"5046","9 - Chapter on the harm of the polytheists concerning their idols. 4224 - Ishaq said: Shabbabah ibn Sawwar al-Madaini informed me, Nuaym ibn Hakim narrated to us, Abu Maryam narrated to us that Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to him, who said: Usamah ibn Zayd and I would go to the idols of Quraysh around the Kaaba; we would come to excrement, take small lumps in our hands, go to the idols of Quraysh and smear them. In the morning they would say: Who did this to our gods? They would go to them and wash them with milk and water. Its chain is sound."
"5047","4225 - Uthman ibn Umar informed us, Ibn Abi Dhib narrated to us, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Mihran, from Umayr the freedman of Ibn Abbas, from Usamah ibn Zayd, who said: I entered with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) the Kaaba, and he saw pictures in it. He said to me: Seek water for me. I brought him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) water in a bucket; he began wetting a garment with it then striking the pictures with it, saying: May Allah destroy peoples who picture what they do not create."
"5048","10 - Chapter on what the polytheists harmed the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with, and his steadfastness upon his affair. 4226 - Abu Bakr said: Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, Yazid ibn Ziyad ibn Abi al-Jad narrated to us, Abu Sakhrah Jami ibn Shaddad narrated to us, from Tariq ibn Abdullah al-Muharibi, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) twice at the market of Dhu al-Majaz, while I was among goods of mine selling them. He passed wearing a red cloak of his, calling at the top of his voice: O people, say: There is no god but Allah, you will prosper. He said: A man followed him with stones, having bloodied his ankles and Achilles tendons, saying: O people, do not obey him, for he is a liar. I said: Who is this? They said: This is a youth of Banu Abd al-Muttalib. I said: Who is this following him pelting him? They said: His uncle Abd al-Uzza, and he is Abu Lahab. He said: When Islam appeared before Madinah, we came in a riding party from al-Rabadhah until we alighted near Madinah — and he mentioned the hadith."
"5049","4227 - Abu Yala said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated to us, Talhah ibn Yahya narrated to us, from Musa ibn Talhah, Aqil ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to us, who said: Quraysh came to Abu Talib and said: O Abu Talib, your nephew harms us in our assembly and in our mosque; forbid him our harm. He said: O Aqil, bring me Muhammad. I went and brought him to him. He said: O son of my brother, your cousins claim you harm them; desist from that. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raised his sight to heaven and said: Do you see this sun? They said: Yes. He said: I am no more able to leave that for you than for you to kindle a flame from it. He said: Abu Talib said: My nephew has not lied to us; go back. This is a good chain."
"5050","4228/1 - al-Humaydi said: Sufyan — he is Ibn Uyaynah — narrated to us, al-Walid ibn Kathir narrated to us. 4228/2 - Abu Yala said: Abu Musa al-Harawi narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from al-Walid ibn Kathir, from Ibn Tadrus the freedman of Hakim ibn Hizam, from Asma bint Abi Bakr, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: They said to her: What is the severest you saw the polytheists reach against the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? She said: The polytheists were sitting in the mosque mentioning the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and what he says about their gods, when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came. They rose against him all together. The cry reached Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him; they said: Reach your companion. He went out from us with four braids of his, saying: Woe to you — would you kill a man for saying: My Lord is Allah, when he has come to you with clear proofs from your Lord? He said: They were diverted from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and turned upon Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him. She said: Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, returned to us, touching nothing of his braids but it came with him, saying: Blessed are You, O Possessor of Majesty and Honour."
"5051","11 - Chapter on the Islam of Umar, may Allah be pleased with him. 4229 - Abu Bakr said: Yahya ibn Yala narrated to us, Abdullah ibn al-Muammal narrated to us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The first Islam of Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, was: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Labour seized my sister at night; I went out of the house and entered the coverings of the Kaaba on a cold night. He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came, entered the Hijr wearing his sandals, prayed what Allah willed, then departed. He said: I heard something the like of which I never heard; I went out following him. He said: Who is this? He said: Umar. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Umar, will you not leave me night or day? He said: I feared he would supplicate against me, so I said: I bear witness there is no god but Allah and you are the Messenger of Allah. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Umar, conceal it. I said: No; by the One Who sent you with truth, I shall proclaim it as I proclaimed polytheism."
"5052","4230/1 - Abu Yala said: Mahmud ibn Khidash narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Yusuf al-Azraq narrated to us, al-Qasim ibn Uthman Abu al-Ala al-Basri narrated to us, from Anas ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: A man of Banu Zuhrah met Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, before he accepted Islam, wearing his sword, and said: Where are you heading, O Umar? He said: I intend to kill Muhammad. He said: How will you be safe among Banu Hashim or Banu Zuhrah when you have killed Muhammad? He said: I see you have but apostatized and left your religion. He said: Shall I not guide you to something wondrous, O Umar? Your brother-in-law and sister have apostatized and left their religion. He said: He walked to them both angrily — Ishaq said: meaning enraged — until he neared the door, with a man called Khabbab with them both, teaching them Surah Ta-Ha. He said: When Khabbab, may Allah be pleased with him, heard the footfall of Umar, he entered beneath a bed of theirs. He said: What is this murmur I heard with you? They said: We have no talk we conversed between us. He said: Perhaps you apostatized and left your religion? His brother-in-law said: O Umar, what if truth lies in other than your religion? He said: He turned upon his brother-in-law and trampled him severely. He said: His sister pushed him away from her husband, so he struck her face and her face bled. She said: What if truth lies in other than your religion? I bear witness there is no god but Allah, and I bear witness Muhammad is His servant and Messenger. He said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Show me this book you were reading. He said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, used to read books. His sister said: No; you are impure. Give us a covenant of Allah the Exalted that you will return it to us, and rise, wash and perform ablution. He said: He did so. He said: He read: Ta-Ha; We did not send down the Quran upon you to distress you, to His saying: And establish prayer for My remembrance; the Hour is coming, I almost conceal it. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Guide me to Muhammad. When Khabbab, may Allah be pleased with him, heard the speech of Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, he came out to him and said: Rejoice, O Umar, for I hope you are the supplication of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for you on Thursday evening: O Allah, strengthen the religion with Umar ibn al-Khattab or with Amr ibn Hisham. They said: He is in the house at the foot of al-Safa — meaning the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) — receiving revelation. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, set out, with Hamzah ibn Abd al-Muttalib, may Allah be pleased with him, and people of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at the door. When Hamzah, may Allah be pleased with him, saw, the people feared Umar, may Allah be pleased with him. He said: Yes, this is Umar; if Allah intends good for him he will accept Islam and follow the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and if otherwise, killing him will be easy upon us. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out to him, seized the folds of his garment and the sword-harness, and said: Will you not desist, O Umar, until Allah the Exalted sends upon you the disgrace and exemplary punishment He sent upon al-Walid ibn al-Mughirah? O Allah, this is Umar ibn al-Khattab; O Allah, strengthen the religion with Umar. Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I bear witness you are the Messenger of Allah. He accepted Islam, then said, may Allah be pleased with him: Come out, O Messenger of Allah."
"5053","4230/2 - Mujahid ibn Musa narrated to us, Ishaq al-Azraq narrated to us, and he mentioned likewise."
"5054","4230/3 - Abd al-Rahman narrated to us at Basrah, Ishaq narrated to us, and he mentioned it likewise. Its beginning: He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on Thursday evening: O Allah, strengthen the religion with Umar ibn al-Khattab or with Amr ibn Hisham. He said: Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, wore his sword, and a man met him of Banu Zuhrah — the hadith."
"5055","12 - Chapter on the emigration to Abyssinia. 4231 - Abu Yala said: Abu Yaqub Ishaq ibn Abi Israil narrated to us, al-Nadr ibn Shumayl narrated to us, Ibn Awn narrated to us, from Umayr ibn Ishaq, who said: Jafar ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, sought permission of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: Permit me to go to a land where I worship Allah the Exalted fearing no one. He permitted him, and he came to the Negus. He said: Amr ibn al-As, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to me, he said: When I saw his place I envied him. I said: By Allah, I shall strive to kill him and his companions. I came to the Negus and entered with him upon him, and said: In your land is a man whose cousin is in our land; he claims people have but one god, and by Allah, if you do not kill him and his companions, I shall never again cross this semen to you — neither I nor any of my companions. He said: Summon him. I said: He will not come with me, so send a messenger with me. He came, and when he reached the door I called: Permit Amr ibn al-As. He called from behind me: Permit Abdullah. He said: He heard his voice and permitted him before me. He said: He and his companions entered. He said: He permitted me and I entered, and he was sitting — and he mentioned where his seat was from the couch. When I saw him I came until I sat before him, placing him behind my back, seating a man of my companions between every two men. He said: The Negus said: Nahhiru, nahhiru — meaning: Speak. Amr said: The cousin of this man is in our land; he claims people have but one god, and by Allah, if you do not kill him and his companions, I shall never cross this semen to you again, nor any of my companions. He said: He testified, and that was the first I heard the testimony that day. He said: My cousin spoke the truth, and I am upon his religion. He said: He cried out and said: Awh, until I said: The Abyssinians do not speak. He said: A law like the law of Musa? What does he say about Isa? He said: He says: He is the Spirit of Allah and His word. He said: He took something from the ground and said: He did not err from what he said, not even this. Were it not for my kingship I would follow you. He said to me: I would not care if you and none of your companions ever came to me again. He said to Jafar, may Allah be pleased with him: Go, you are safe in my land; whoever strikes you I shall kill, and whoever reviles you I shall fine. He said to his chamberlain: Whenever he comes seeking permission of me, permit him, unless I am with my family; if I am with my family, inform him, and if he refuses, permit him. He said: We dispersed, and none was dearer to me to have met alone than Jafar. I met him once on a road, seeing no one, looking behind me and seeing no one. He said: I drew near, took his hand and said: Do you know I bear witness there is no god but Allah, and Muhammad is His servant and Messenger? He said: He said: May Allah the Exalted guide you; be firm. He said: He left me and went. He said: I came to my companions, and it was as if they witnessed with me; they seized me, cast a striped cloak or garment over me, and began smothering me. I kept bringing my head out from this side once and from this side once, until I escaped with not a peel upon me. He said: I met an Abyssinian woman and took her head-cover, placing it upon my nakedness. She said such-and-such, and I said such-and-such. I came to Jafar, may Allah be pleased with him, and he said: What is with you? I said: Everything of mine is gone until not a peel is left upon me, and what you see upon me is but an Abyssinian womans head-cover. He said: He set out, and I set out with him until we came to the kings door. He said: Permit the party of Allah. He said: Admit him; he is with his family. He said: Seek permission. He sought permission and he permitted him. He said: Amr has followed me upon my religion. He said: No. He said: Yes. He said: No. He said: Yes. He said to a man: Go with him; if he did so, write down everything he says. He said: Yes. He began writing what I say until I left nothing, even the cup. Had he wished to take of their wealth to my wealth, he would have done. This is a good chain, except it contradicts the well-known that the Islam of Amr, may Allah be pleased with him, was at the hands of the Negus himself. Umayr ibn Ishaq is alone in it; none narrated from him but Abdullah ibn Awn. Ibn Ma'in said: He is worth nothing, and once declared him trustworthy; in sum his hadith is written. al-Bazzar said: We know it narrated from Amr, may Allah be pleased with him, only with this chain."
"5056","4232 - Ishaq said: al-Mulai informed me, Zakariyya narrated, from al-Shabi, who said: The emigration from Abyssinia was on the nights of Khaybar."
"5057","[The ancients acknowledging the signs of prophethood.] 4233 - Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah said: Ali ibn Mushir narrated to us, from al-Ajlah, from al-Dhayyal ibn Harmalah al-Asadi, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Quraysh gathered one day and said: Look for your most knowledgeable in magic, soothsaying and poetry; let him come to this man who split our unity, scattered our affair and faulted our religion; let him speak to him and see what he answers him. They said: We know none but Utbah ibn Rabiah. They said: You, O Abu al-Walid. Utbah came to him and said: O Muhammad, are you better or Abdullah? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fell silent. He said: If you claim they are better than you, they worshipped the gods you faulted; if you claim you are better than them, speak until we hear your saying. By Allah, we never saw a young sheep more ill-omened upon its people than you: you split our unity, scattered our affair, faulted our religion, exposed us among the Arabs, until it flew among them that among Quraysh is a sorcerer and among Quraysh is a soothsayer. By Allah, we await but like the cry of a woman in labour, that some of us advance upon others with swords until we perish. Man, if need is with you, we shall collect for you until you become the richest single man of Quraysh; if desire is with you, choose whichever women of Quraysh you wish and we shall marry you ten. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Have you finished? He said: Yes. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate: Ha-Mim; a revelation from the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate, until he reached: If they turn away, say: I warn you of a thunderbolt like the thunderbolt of Ad and Thamud. Utbah said to him: Enough, enough; have you aught besides this? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No. He returned to Quraysh and they said: What is behind you? He said: I left nothing I saw you would speak to him with but I spoke to him with it. They said: Did he answer you? He said: Yes. He said: By the One Who erected it as a structure, I understood nothing of what he said except that he warned you of a thunderbolt like the thunderbolt of Ad and Thamud. They said: Woe to you — a man speaks to you in Arabic and you know not what he said? He said: No, by Allah, I understood nothing of what he said except mention of the thunderbolt. Abd and Abu Yala narrated it, both from Abu Bakr; al-Hakim declared it sound through Jafar ibn Awn from al-Ajlah."
"5058","15 - Chapter on the Night Journey. 4234 - Abu Yala said: Zakariyya ibn Yahya narrated to us, Nasr ibn Muzahim narrated to us, from Jafar ibn Ziyad, from Hilal ibn Miqlas, from Abdullah ibn Asad ibn Zurarah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When I was taken up to heaven, I was brought to a palace of pearl with door-leaves of glittering gold; it was revealed to me — or he commanded me — concerning Ali three qualities: That you are the chief of the messengers, the leader of the godfearing, and the guide of the bright-limbed."
"5059","4235 - Muhammad ibn Ismail ibn Ali al-Ansari narrated to us, Damrah ibn Rabiah narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Abi Amr al-Shaybani, from Abu Salih the freedman of Umm Hani, from Umm Hani, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to me at dawn, sat while I was upon my bed, and said (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): I felt I spent the night in the Sacred Mosque; Jibril, peace be upon him, came to me and took me to the mosques gate, where a white beast above a donkey and below a mule, restless-eared. I rode, its hoof falling at its sights end; when it took me downhill its forelegs lengthened and its hind legs shortened, and when it took me uphill its hind legs lengthened and its forelegs shortened, while Jibril, peace be upon him, never left me, until we reached Bayt al-Maqdis. I tied it with the ring the prophets used to tie with. A group of prophets was spread out for me, among them Ibrahim, Musa and Isa, peace and blessings be upon them; I prayed with them and spoke to them. Two vessels were brought to me, red and white; I drank the white. Jibril, peace be upon him, said to me: You drank milk and left wine; had you drunk wine your nation would have apostatized. Then I rode it, came to the Sacred Mosque and prayed the dawn with it. She said: I clung to his cloak and said: I adjure you by Allah, O cousin, not to narrate this to Quraysh, lest whoever believed you belie you. He struck his cloak with his hand, pulling it from my hand; it rose from his belly, and I saw his sides above his waist-wrapper like folded sheets, with a radiant light at his heart almost snatching my sight. I fell prostrate. When I raised my head, he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had gone out. I said to my slave-girl Nabah: Woe to you, follow him and see what he says and what is said to him. When Nabah returned she informed me the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reached a group of Quraysh in al-Hatim, among them al-Mutim ibn Adi, Amr ibn Hisham and al-Walid ibn al-Mughirah. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I prayed the night prayer tonight in this mosque, prayed the dawn with it, and in between came to Bayt al-Maqdis; a group of prophets was spread out for me, among them Ibrahim, Musa and Isa, peace and blessings be upon them, and I prayed with them and spoke to them. Amr ibn Hisham said mockingly: Describe them to me. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: As for Isa, peace be upon him, he is above medium and below tall, broad-chested, fair-skinned, curly-haired with reddish tint, as Urwah ibn Masud al-Thaqafi; as for Musa, peace be upon him, he is bulky, dark, tall, as of the men of Shanuah, with overlapping teeth, short lip, protruding gum, frowning; as for Ibrahim, peace be upon him, by Allah, he is the most like people to me in character and form. He said: They clamoured and deemed it grave. al-Mutim ibn Adi said: Everything of your affair before today was bearable except your saying today; I bear witness you lie. We strike camels livers to Bayt al-Maqdis, ascending a month and descending a month; you claim you came to it in a night. By al-Lat and al-Uzza, I shall never believe you, and what you say never was. al-Mutim ibn Adi had a basin upon Zamzam which Abd al-Muttalib gave him; he demolished it, swearing by al-Lat and al-Uzza never to draw a drop from it again. Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: O Mutim, evil is what you said to your nephew; you confronted and belied him. I bear witness he is truthful. They said: O Muhammad, describe Bayt al-Maqdis to us. He said: I entered it by night and left it by night. Jibril, peace be upon him, came and placed it in his wing; he began saying: A gate of it thus in such place, and a gate of it thus in such place, while Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: You spoke the truth, you spoke the truth. Nabah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say that day: O Abu Bakr, I have named you al-Siddiq. They said: O Mutim, let us ask him of what avails us more than Bayt al-Maqdis. O Muhammad, inform us of our caravans. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I came upon the caravan of so-and-so at al-Rawha; they had lost a camel of theirs and went seeking it. I reached their baggage with none of them there, with a cup of water, and I drank from it; ask them of that. They said: This, by God, is a sign. Then I reached the caravan of so-and-so, and the camels shied from me; a red camel among them knelt bearing a sack stitched with white — I know not whether the camel broke or not; ask them of that. They said: This, by God, is a sign. Then I reached the caravan of so-and-so at al-Tanim, led by a grey camel; it is emerging upon you from the pass. al-Walid ibn al-Mughirah said: A sorcerer. They went and looked and found the affair as he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, and accused him of sorcery, saying: al-Walid ibn al-Mughirah spoke the truth in what he said. Allah, mighty and majestic, revealed: We made the vision We showed you but a trial for people, and the tree cursed in the Quran. I said to Umm Hani, may Allah be pleased with her: What is the tree cursed in the Quran? She said: Those who were frightened, yet fright increased them only in transgression and disbelief."
"5060","4236/1 - al-Harith said: al-Hasan ibn Musa narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Abu Hamzah, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah ibn Qays, from Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was brought al-Buraq and rode it behind Jibril, peace be upon him. They travelled; when he came upon a mountain his hind legs rose, and when he descended his forelegs rose. He travelled with us in a dark, foul land, until we emerged to a spacious, pleasant land. I said: O Jibril, we travelled in a dark, foul land until we emerged to a spacious, pleasant land. Jibril, peace be upon him, said: That is the land of Fire, and this is the land of Paradise. He said: I came upon a man standing praying. He said: Who is this with you, O Jibril? He said: This is your brother Muhammad. He welcomed me, supplicated for blessing for me, and said: Ask ease for your nation. I said: Who is this, O Jibril? He said: This is your brother Isa. He said: Then we travelled and heard a voice and clamour. We came upon a man. He said: Who is this, O Jibril? He said: He said: This is your brother Muhammad. He said: He welcomed and supplicated blessing for me, and said: Ask ease for your nation. I said: Who is this, O Jibril? He said: This is your brother Musa. He said: I said: Against whom is his complaint and voice? He said: Against his Lord. I said: Against his Lord? He said: Yes; he knows that of him and his severity. Then we travelled and saw lamps. I said: What is this, O Jibril? He said: This is the tree of your father Ibrahim; will you draw near him? I said: Yes. He said: We drew near him; he welcomed and supplicated blessing for me. Then we went until we entered Bayt al-Maqdis; he tied the beast with the ring the prophets tie with. Then I entered Bayt al-Maqdis, and the prophets were spread out for me, those Allah the Exalted named of them and those He named not; I prayed with them except these three: Musa, Isa and Ibrahim, peace and blessings be upon them."
"5061","4236/2 - Abu Yala said: Hudbah ibn Khalid and Shayban ibn Farrukh narrated to us; both said: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us."
"5062","16 - Chapter on the emigration of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Madinah. 4237 - Ishaq said: I was told from Ibn Ishaq, who said: When Suraqah returned from seeking the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and seeking Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, he began mentioning what he saw of the horse and what hardship befell him in seeking them both. Abu Jahl heard of that and feared he would accept Islam when he saw what he saw, so he said verses concerning that; Suraqah answered him in what he said, saying: With proof — the people you make peace with."
"5063","4238 - Abu Yala said: Musa ibn Muhammad ibn Hayyan narrated to us, Abu Ali al-Hanafi narrated to us, Musa ibn Mutayr narrated to us, my father narrated to me, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to me, who said: A polytheist man came until he faced the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with his nakedness, urinating. I said: O Messenger of Allah, does the man not see us? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Had he seen us he would not have faced us with his nakedness — meaning while they were in the cave."
"5064","4239 - Ibn Abi Umar narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibn Judan, who said: The oldest of the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) — meaning in emigration — were Abu Bakr al-Siddiq and Sahl ibn Bayda, may Allah be pleased with them both."
"5065","17 - Chapter on the pledge of al-Aqabah. 4240 - Abu Bakr said: Isa — he is Ibn Yunus — narrated to us, from Ibn Abi Layla, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: We remained in Madinah two years before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to us, maintaining the mosques and establishing prayer."
"5066","4241/1 - Muawiyah ibn Hisham narrated to us, Sufyan narrated to us, from Dawud, from al-Shabi, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) met the chiefs of the Ansar, he said: Will you shelter me and protect me? They said: What is for us? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: For you is Paradise. Abridged and sound; they brought it at length."
"5067","4241/2 - Abu Yala said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us."
"5068","18 - From the chapter of emigration. 4242/1 - Abu Bakr said: Aswad ibn Amir narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Ali ibn Zayd, from al-Hasan, who said: Suraqah ibn Malik al-Mudliji narrated to them that Quraysh placed forty ounces upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Abu Bakr. He said: While I was sitting, a man came to me and said: The two men for whom Quraysh placed what they placed are near you in such-and-such place. He said: I came to my horse in the pasture and startled it, then took my spear and rode it. He said: I began dragging the spear fearing the people of the water would share in them both with me. He said: When I saw them both, Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: A seeker seeking us. He said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned and said: O Allah, suffice us against him with what You will. He said: My horse sank with me, though I was upon hard ground; I fell upon a stone and overturned. I said: Supplicate the One Who did what I see to release him — pledging him never to disobey him ever. He said: He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) supplicated for him, and the horse was released. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Will you grant it to me? He said: Yes. He said: Here, divert people from us. He took the coast by the sea. He said: I was for them a seeker in the first of the day and a guard for them in the last of the day. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When we settle in Madinah, if you see fit to come to us, come to us. When he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to Madinah, prevailed over the people of Badr and Uhud, and those around him accepted Islam, Suraqah said: It reached me he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended to send Khalid ibn al-Walid, may Allah be pleased with him, to Banu Mudlij; I came to him — and he mentioned the hadith past in the commentary of Surah al-Nisa."
"5069","4242/2 - al-Harith said: Bishr ibn Umar al-Zahrani narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated at length. al-Bukhari brought this mentioned hadith with its meaning through another route from Suraqah, may Allah be pleased with him; in this are differences in places."
"5070","4243 - Abu Yala said: Jafar ibn Humayd narrated to us, Ubaydullah ibn Iyad narrated to us, from his father, from Qays ibn al-Numan, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, set out hiding in the cave, they passed a servant pasturing sheep and asked him for milk to drink. He said: By Allah, I have no ewe to milk, except here is a young goat that conceived in wintertime; no milk remains for her, and she is lean. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Bring her to us. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) supplicated blessing over her, then milked a large cup and gave Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, to drink; then milked another and gave the shepherd to drink; then milked and drank he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The servant said: By Allah, who are you? I never saw your like ever. He said: If I inform you, will you conceal me? He said: Yes. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I am Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah. He said: Are you the one Quraysh claim has apostatized? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: They say that. He said: I bear witness you are the Messenger of Allah, what you brought is true, and none does what you did but a prophet. Then he said: Shall I follow you? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: No, until you hear we have prevailed; when that reaches you, go out. He followed him after he went out of the cave."
"5071","20 - Chapter on the expedition of Badr. 4244 - Musaddad said: Yahya or Khalid narrated to us — Muadh ibn al-Muthanna the narrator from Musaddad doubted — Amr ibn Yahya ibn Umarah narrated to us, from Amr ibn Amir ibn Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, from his father, from Amir ibn Rabiah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The morning of Badr was Monday, seventeen gone of Ramadan."
"5072","4245 - Ishaq said: Wahb ibn Jarir ibn Hazim informed us, my father narrated to us, he said: I heard Muhammad ibn Ishaq say: Abdullah ibn Abi Bakr narrated to me, from some of Banu Saidah, from Abu Usayd Malik ibn Rabiah, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said after his sight went: Had I sight I would now show you at Badr the pass from which the angels came out, doubting not nor disputing."
"5073","4246 - Amr ibn Muhammad informed us, Abu Bakr al-Hudhali narrated to us, from Abu al-Malih, Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Masud narrated to me, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I came upon Abu Jahl on the day of Badr; I drew near him and struck him, and Allah the Exalted killed him. I came to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him, finding Aqil ibn Abi Talib with him a captive. He said: Did you kill him? I said: Yes. He said: You lie. I said: O enemy of Allah, do you belie me? He said: What did you see in him? I said: I saw upon his thigh a ring like a camels ring. He said: You spoke the truth; it is a brand of fire branded from a thorn. He said: Abu Jahl says: Sadees. I say: The story of Abu Jahl was narrated by Abu Dawud and others from the hadith of Abu Ubaydah from his father with other than this wording. This chain is weak."
"5074","4247/1 - Amr ibn Muhammad and Yahya ibn Adam informed us; both said: Israil narrated to us, and he mentioned it."
"5075","4247/2 - Ahmad ibn Mani said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, Israil narrated to us, from Abu Ishaq, from Abu Ubaydah ibn Abdullah, from his father, concerning His saying the Exalted: When He showed them to you, when you met, few in your eyes, and made you few in their eyes. He said: They were made few in our eyes until I said to a man beside me: Do you deem them seventy? He said: I deem them a hundred. Until we took a man of them and asked him, and he said: We were a thousand. I say: This is a sound chain if Abu Ubaydah heard it from his father, for there is disagreement over his hearing from him."
"5076","4248 - Ishaq said: Wahb ibn Jarir informed us, my father narrated, he said: I heard Muhammad ibn Ishaq saying: Abdullah ibn Abi Najih narrated to me, from Ata, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: Allah the Exalted obligated upon them that one man fight ten, and that was heavy upon them and hard upon them, so Allah the Exalted reduced it for them to one man fighting two men, and Allah the Exalted revealed concerning that: {If there are among you twenty steadfast, they will overcome two hundred} to the end of the verses, and He said: {Had it not been for a decree from Allah that preceded, a great punishment would have touched you for what you took} meaning the spoils of Badr, saying: Were it not that I do not punish one who disobeys Me until I have given him prior warning, then He said: {O Prophet, say to those captives in your hands} the verse. Al-Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, said: By Allah, it was revealed concerning me, when I informed the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of my Islam and asked him to reckon for me the twenty ounces I had brought with me, and he gave me for them twenty slaves, each of them having traded with wealth in his hand, along with what I hope for of the forgiveness of Allah the Exalted. This is a sound chain, Ibn Mardawayh narrated it in the supported commentary from Ahmad ibn al-Husayn, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad, from Ishaq likewise. Al-Tabarani brought it from the hadith of Yazid ibn Harun, from Ibn Ishaq."
"5077","4249 - Wahb ibn Jarir ibn Hazim informed us, my father narrated, he said: I heard Ibn Ishaq saying: My father narrated to me, from Jubayr ibn Mutim, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I saw before the rout of the people, while the people were fighting, something like a black striped cloak coming from the sky like black ants, and I had no doubt that they were the angels, and nothing happened except the rout of the people. Its chain is good, if Ishaq ibn Yasar heard it from Jubayr."
"5078","4250 - Al-Harith said: Muhammad ibn Umar narrated us, Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Umar ibn Ali narrated us, Ishaq ibn Salim narrated us, from Zayd ibn Ali, who said: The slogan of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Badr was: O Mansur, kill."
"5079","4251 - Abu Yala said: Yahya ibn Abd al-Hamid narrated us, Abdullah ibn Jafar narrated us, from Abu Awn, from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah, who said: I said to Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, may Allah be pleased with him: O uncle, inform me of your story on the day of Badr. He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Read after the one hundred and twentieth verse of Al Imran and you will find our story: {And when you went out in the morning from your household to assign the believers their positions for battle} up to His saying: {That you lose heart}. He said: They are those who sought safety from the polytheists, up to His saying: {While you were looking on}. He said: It is the believers wishing to meet the enemy, up to His saying: {When you were slaying them by His permission}."
"5080","4252 - Ubaydullah ibn Umar narrated us, Yusuf ibn Khalid narrated us, Harun ibn Sad narrated us, from Abu Salih al-Hanafi, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded me to damage their wells, meaning on the day of Badr."
"5081","4253 - Muhammad ibn Ismail ibn Abi Saminah narrated us, Muhammad ibn Khalid al-Hanafi narrated us, Musa ibn Yaqub al-Zamai narrated us, from Abu al-Huwayrith, from Muhammad ibn Jubayr ibn Mutim, from Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I was at the well of Badr drawing water from it, when a severe wind came, then a severe wind came the like of which I had not seen except the one before it, then a severe wind came. The first was Mikail with a thousand angels on the right of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), the second was Israfil with a thousand angels on the left of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the third was Jibril, peace be upon him, with a thousand angels. Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, was on his right, and I was on his left. When Allah the Exalted routed the disbelievers, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mounted me on a horse, and when I settled on it he carried me and I came upon its neck, so I supplicated Allah the Exalted and He made me firm upon it, and I thrust with my spear until the blood reached my armpit."
"5082","4254 - Amr ibn Muhammad al-Naqid narrated us, Ali ibn Thabit al-Jazari narrated us, al-Wazia ibn Nafia narrated us, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: We were praying with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the campaign of Badr when he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) smiled in his prayer. When he finished the prayer we said: O Messenger of Allah, we saw you smiling? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Mikail, peace be upon him, passed by me with traces of dust on his wing, returning from pursuing the people, and he laughed to me, so I smiled to him."
"5083","21 - Mention of the virtues of those who witnessed Badr. 4255 - Al-Harith said: Yaqub ibn Muhammad narrated us, Abd al-Muhaymin ibn al-Abbas narrated us, my father narrated me, from his father, who said: His father Sad, may Allah be pleased with him, went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Badr, and when he was at al-Rawha he died, so he wrote his will at the end of his saddle, and bequeathed to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) his camel and his saddle and three wasqs of barley, and he accepted them then returned them to his heirs, and he allotted him his share."
"5084","4256 - Yaqub ibn Muhammad narrated us, Abd al-Aziz ibn Muhammad narrated us, from Jafar ibn Muhammad, from his father: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) allotted to Jafar ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, his share on the day of Badr. And the hadith of Hatib, may Allah be pleased with him, has preceded in the commentary of al-Mumtahanah."
"5085","22 - Mention of those killed at Badr. 4257 - Al-Harith said: Ubaydullah ibn Muhammad narrated us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated us, from Ata ibn al-Saib, from al-Shaabi, who said: When it was the day of Badr, Uqbah ibn Abi Muayt was brought as a captive. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I shall surely kill you. He said: Will you kill me apart from Quraysh? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes. Then he turned to his Companions, may Allah be pleased with them, and said: He came to me while I was prostrating and stepped on my neck, and by Allah he did not lift it until I thought my eyes would fall out, and he brought the afterbirth of a camel and cast it upon me, until Fatimah came and removed it from my head. He said: Then he commanded concerning him and he was killed."
"5086","4258 - Musaddad said: Wahb ibn Jarir narrated us, my father narrated, he said: I heard Ibn Sirin saying: The two sons of Afra struck him, and Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, finished him off, meaning Abu Jahl."
"5087","23 - Chapter on the killing of Kab ibn al-Ashraf. 4259 / 1 - Ishaq said: Wahb ibn Jarir informed us, my father narrated, he said: I heard Muhammad ibn Ishaq saying: Thawr ibn Yazid narrated to me, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: They gathered with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he walked with them until he reached Baqi al-Gharqad on a moonlit night, and said: Go forth in the name of Allah, O Allah, help them. And the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned to his house. He said: They went on until they reached his fortress, meaning Kab ibn al-Ashraf, and Abu Nailah called out to him, and he came down to him though he was newly married. His wife said to him: You are a warrior, and a man of war does not come down at such an hour. He said to her: It is Abu Nailah; by Allah, if he found me asleep he would not wake me. She said: By Allah, I recognize evil in his voice. He said to her: If a young man is called to his thrusting wound he would answer. So he came down to them and they talked for a while, then they said: If we walked to the ravine of al-Ajuz and talked this night of ours, for we have not done that for a long time. He said: Yes. So they went out walking, then Abu Nailah passed his hand through the side of his head and said: I have never seen perfume more fragrant than tonight. Then he walked a while, then repeated the like of it until he felt secure, then he put his hand into the side of his head and seized his hair, then said: Strike the enemy of Allah. He said: Their swords differed upon him. He said: The enemy of Allah cried out a cry, and no fortress remained except fire was lit upon it. He said: The leg of al-Harith was wounded. Muhammad ibn Maslamah said: When I saw the swords availing nothing, I remembered a dagger in my sword, so I took it and placed it upon his navel and leaned upon it until it reached his pubes, and he fell. Then we went out and passed by Banu Umayyah, then by Banu Qurayzah, then by Buath, then we traveled by night in Harrat al-Urayd. Al-Harith lagged behind and bled, so we stopped for him, then we carried him until we brought him to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at the end of the night while he was praying. He came out to us and we informed him of the killing of the enemy of Allah, and he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) spat upon the wound of al-Harith. We returned with him to his house, and the people dispersed to their lodgings. In the morning the Jews feared because of our striking the enemy of Allah, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whomever you find among the men of the Jews, kill him. So Muhayyisah ibn Masud leapt upon Ibn Sunaynah, a man of the Jewish merchants whom he used to trade with and mix with, and killed him. He said: Huwayyisah ibn Masud, who was then a polytheist and was older than him, began to strike him, saying: O enemy of Allah, did you kill him? By Allah, much of the fat in your belly is from his wealth. He said: By Allah, a man commanded me to kill him who, had he commanded me to kill you, I would have struck your neck. He said: By Allah, if Muhammad commanded you to kill me would you kill me? He said: Yes, by Allah. He said: By Allah, a religion that brings you to this is a wondrous religion. So the first Islam of Huwayyisah was because of the word of his brother. Muhayyisah said poetry concerning that. This is a good connected chain. Ahmad brought from it up to his saying: O Allah, help them, only, and that is the raised connected part of it, and the rest is interpolated. It has a witness in the Sahih from the hadith of Amr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him."
"5088","4259 / 2 - Al-Humaydi said: Sufyan narrated us, al-Absi narrated us, from Ikrimah, who said: His wife said: I hear a voice in which I sense the smell of blood? He said: It is only Abu Nailah, a brother of mine; if he found me asleep he would not wake me. The noble one, when called to a thrusting wound, surely answers. And he named those who came to him with Abu Nailah: Muhammad ibn Maslamah, Abbad ibn Bishr, al-Harith ibn Muadh, and Abu Ubays ibn Jabir."
"5089","24 - Chapter on the battle of Uhud. 4260 / 1 - Ishaq said: Wahb ibn Jarir ibn Hazim informed us, my father narrated, he said: I heard Muhammad ibn Ishaq saying: Yahya ibn Abbad narrated to me, from his father, from Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, from al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: By Allah, I was looking that day at the anklets of the women with their garments tucked up, hurrying, when the people were routed, and I saw nothing standing between us and seizing them, and we reckoned them slain with none of them returning to us. The bearers of the banner were struck down, and they stood firm by it until it came to an Abyssinian slave of his called Sawab, then Sawab was killed and cast down the banner, and no one of Allah's creation came near it until Amrah bint Alqamah al-Harithiyyah leapt to it and raised it for them, and the people rallied to it. Al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: By Allah, while we were thus having overcome them and prevailed over them, the archers disobeyed the command of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and came toward the camp when they saw it in disorder, after we had driven them from it, desiring the spoils and abandoning the covenant of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and they began taking the goods. Then the cavalry came upon us from behind and crushed us, and the people turned back fleeing. A crier cried out, whom they thought was Satan: Verily Muhammad has been killed. The people were stricken, and some of them rode over others, and they became three thirds: a third wounded, a third slain, and a third fleeing, while the battle raged. The archers had differed among themselves: one party, seeing the people fallen upon the spoils, said: Allah the Exalted has routed the polytheists, and the Muslims have taken the spoils, so what are you waiting for? Another party said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had instructed you beforehand and forbade you to leave your place, whether it be against him or for him. They disputed concerning that, then the first party of archers insisted on joining the camp, so the people dispersed and left their place, and thereupon the cavalry of the polytheists charged. This is a sound chain, with a witness in the Sahih from the hadith of al-Bara, may Allah be pleased with him."
"5090","4260 / 2 - And with this chain to al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: By Allah, drowsiness was overcoming me when I heard Ibn Qushayr saying it, and I heard it from him only as in a dream. Then he recited: {Indeed those of you who turned away on the day the two hosts met, Satan only caused them to slip because of some of what they had earned, and Allah has already pardoned them; indeed Allah is Forgiving, Forbearing}. He said: Those who turned away at the retreat of the people were Uthman ibn Affan, Said ibn Uthman al-Zuraqi, and his brother Uqbah ibn Uthman, until they reached a mountain near Madinah called al-Hajib in Batn al-Awas, where they stayed three days. They claimed that when they returned to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: You went far afield in it. Then he said: {O you who believe, be not like those who disbelieved} meaning the hypocrites, {And said to their brothers when they traveled in the land or were fighters: Had they been with us they would not have died nor been killed, that Allah may make it a regret in their hearts} the verse. He said: Seeking and regretting, and that avails them nothing. Then came the account of what He commands His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and enjoins upon him, until it reached His saying: {Has it befallen you, when a calamity befell you though you had inflicted twice as much} meaning the day of Badr, of those killed and captured, {You said: Whence is this? Say: It is from yourselves} which was from the archers. He said: Then He said: {And what befell you on the day the two hosts met was by Allah's permission, that He might know the believers} saying: Their affair openly manifest, {And that He might know the hypocrites} so their affair becomes open, meaning Abdullah ibn Ubayy and those with him who turned back from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when he marched to his enemy, {And it was said to them: Come, fight in the way of Allah or defend. They said: Had we known fighting we would have followed you} and that was because of their saying when the Companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them while marching to Uhud as they turned away from them: Do you forsake us and surrender us to our enemy? They said: We do not think there will be fighting; had we thought there would be fighting we would have followed you. Allah, mighty and majestic, says: {That day they were nearer to disbelief than to faith, saying with their mouths what was not in their hearts, and Allah knows best what they conceal. Those who said to their brothers} of their kinsmen, and He the Exalted did not mean their brothers in religion, {Had they obeyed us they would not have been killed}. Allah, mighty and majestic, said: {Say: Then avert death from yourselves, if you are truthful}. Ishaq said: Wahb narrated it to us thus, and I think some of the commentary is from Ibn Ishaq, meaning his saying: such means such. I say: Rather the hadith of al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with him, ends at His saying, blessed and Exalted: {Forgiving, Forbearing}, and from his saying: He said: And those who turned away, to the end of the hadith, is from the hadith of Ibn Ishaq without a chain."
"5091","4260 / 3 - Yahya ibn Adam informed us, Ibn Abi Zaidah narrated us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, from Yahya ibn Abbad, from his father, from Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: You saw me with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Uhud when fear intensified upon us and sleep was sent upon us, and there was none of us except his chin was upon his chest, and by Allah I heard as in a dream the saying of Muattib ibn Qushayr: Had we any part in the affair we would not have been killed here, and I memorized it, and Allah, mighty and majestic, revealed concerning that: {Then He sent down upon you after distress security, slumber} up to His saying: {We would not have been killed here} for the saying of Muattib ibn Qushayr. He said: {Had you been in your houses} until it reached: {And Allah knows what is in the breasts}."
"5092","4260 / 4 - Wahb informed us, my father narrated, he said: I heard Muhammad ibn Ishaq saying: Yahya ibn Abbad narrated to me, from his father, from Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, from al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ascending at Uhud... and he mentioned the hadith. He said: Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded Ali ibn Abi Talib to go to al-Mihras, and he brought him water in his shield, and brought it to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He wanted to drink from it but found an odor in it and refused it, so he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) washed his face with it from the blood that had afflicted him, saying: Severe is the anger of Allah upon one who bloodied the face of the Messenger of Allah. The one who bloodied him that day was Utbah ibn Abi Waqqas."
"5093","4261 - Hamzah ibn al-Harith, meaning Ibn Umayr, informed us, from his father, from Amr ibn Yahya al-Mazini, who said: When it was the day of Uhud, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was scratched and his front tooth was broken, and Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, came to him and bent over him weeping. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Bring me water. So he brought him water in a bowl from al-Mihras, and when he brought it near him he refused it, and began washing the blood from him, saying: Severe is the anger of Allah upon people who wounded the face of His Prophet. Then he said: See what Sad ibn al-Rabi has done, for I saw twelve spears directed at him. So the messenger of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to him and said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me to see what has become of you. He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Convey greetings from me to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and inform him that I am at my last breath, and convey greetings to your people and say to them: If the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) perishes while an eyelid of yours blinks, then you have no excuse with Allah the Exalted. Then he said: Who will take this sword with its right? He said: This hadith al-Zubayr narrates of himself. He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, I will. He turned away from me once, and I said: He only turned away from me because of some evil in me. Then he said: Who will take this sword with its right? I said: I will. He turned away from me two or three times. Then Abu Dujanah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I will take it and strike with it until it bends, or a word like it. So he gave him the sword. Al-Zubayr said: I followed him to see what he would do. He came upon no man of the polytheists except he killed him. He came to a man exhausted in fighting and killed him, and came upon a woman saying: Cushions, and raised the sword against her, then withheld his hand from her. I said: O Abu Dujanah, you did such and such, until you came to the woman and raised the sword against her then withheld your hand from her? He said: I honored the sword of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from her."
"5094","4262 - Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Mamar narrated us, from al-Zuhri, who said: Satan cried out on the day of Uhud: Muhammad has been killed. Kab ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I was the first who recognized the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); I saw his eyes from beneath the helmet, so I called at the top of my voice: This is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He gestured to me to be silent. Then Allah, mighty and majestic, revealed: {And Muhammad is but a Messenger; messengers have passed before him. If he dies or is killed, will you turn back on your heels} the verse. Its men are trustworthy, but it is mursal or mudal."
"5095","4263 - Al-Fadl ibn Musa informed us, from Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Alqamah, from Sad ibn al-Mundhir, from Abu Humayd al-Saidi, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out on the day of Uhud until, when he had passed Thaniyyat al-Wada, he looked behind him and saw a rugged brigade. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Who is this? It was said: This is Abdullah ibn Ubayy ibn Salul with his allies among the Jews of Banu Qaynuqa, the clan of Abdullah ibn Salam. He said: Have they embraced Islam? It was said: They are upon their religion. He said: Tell them to return, for we do not seek help from the polytheists against the polytheists. This is a good chain."
"5096","4264 - Al-Harith said: Muhammad ibn Umar narrated us, Bukayr ibn Mismar narrated us, from Amir ibn Sad, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Uhud wearing two coats of mail, and he said: Would that I had been left with my companions at the foot of the mountain, meaning the martyrs of Uhud."
"5097","4265 / 1 - Abu Yala said: Suwayd ibn Said narrated us, Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated us, from Yazid ibn Khusayfah, from al-Saib ibn Yazid, from a man of Banu Tamim called Muadh, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wore two coats of mail together on the day of Uhud."
"5098","4265 / 2 - Abd al-Ala ibn Hammad narrated us, Bishr ibn al-Sari narrated us, Ibn Uyaynah narrated us, but he said: From al-Saib ibn Yazid, from one who narrated to him, from Talhah."
"5099","4266 - Abu Bakr said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated us, Muhammad ibn Salih narrated me, Yazid ibn Zayd, the freedman of Abu Usayd al-Saidi, narrated me, from Abu Usayd, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at the grave of Hamzah, may Allah be pleased with him. The striped cloak was drawn over his head and his feet showed, then drawn over his feet and his head showed. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Draw it over his head, and place upon his feet some of the harmal shrubs."
"5100","4267 - Abu Yala said: Abu Musa narrated us, Muhammad ibn Marwan al-Uqayli narrated us, from Umarah ibn Abi Hafsah, from Ikrimah, who said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said to me: When the people dispersed from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Uhud, I looked at the slain and did not see the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) among them. I said: By Allah, he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would not flee, and I do not see him among the slain, but I think Allah, mighty and majestic, became angry with us for what we did and raised His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Nothing is better for me than to fight until I am killed. So I broke the scabbard of my sword, then charged the people and they were cleared for me, and there I was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) among them."
"5101","4268 / 1 - Abd al-Rahman ibn Salih narrated us, Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, who said: Dawud ibn al-Husayn narrated to me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Uqbah, from his father Uqbah, the freedman of Jabr ibn Atik, who said: I witnessed Uhud with my patrons. I struck a man of the polytheists, and when I killed him I said: Take it from me, I am the Persian man. That reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said: Why did you not say: Take it from me, I am the Ansari man? For the freedman of a people is of themselves."
"5102","4268 / 2 - Al-Hasan ibn Sufyan said: Shayban ibn Abi Shaybah narrated us, Yahya ibn al-Ala narrated us, from Dawud ibn al-Husayn, from Uqbah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I witnessed Uhud with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he mentioned it."
"5103","4268 / 3 - Abu Nuaym said: Abu Amr ibn Hamdan narrated us, al-Hasan narrated us with it. It follows from this that Abu Nuaym and Salamah ibn Nafia listed Abd al-Rahman ibn Uqbah among the Companions, and there is no basis for it, and Allah knows best."
"5104","4269 - Abu Bakr said: Khalid ibn Makhlad narrated us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Abd al-Aziz al-Ansari narrated us, al-Zuhri narrated me, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab ibn Malik, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on the day of Uhud: Who has seen where Hamzah was killed? An unarmed man said: I saw where he was killed. He said: Go and show it to us. So he went out until he stood over Hamzah, may Allah be pleased with him, and saw him with his belly split open and mutilated. He said: O Messenger of Allah, by Allah he has been mutilated. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) disliked looking at him. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood among the slain and said: I am a witness over these. Shroud them in their blood, for no wound is wounded for Allah except it will come on the Day of Resurrection bleeding, its color the color of blood and its scent the scent of musk. Put forward the one of them with most Quran and place him in the grave niche."
"5105","4270 / 1 - Ahmad ibn Mani said: Husayn ibn Muhammad narrated us. 4270 / 2 - Abu Yala said: Muhammad ibn Bakkar narrated us. Both said: Abu Mashar narrated us, Ayyub narrated us, from Abu Umamah ibn Sahl ibn Hunayf, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Ali came with his sword to Fatimah on the day of Uhud and said: Wash this sword of mine, for I fought well today. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If you fought well, then Asim ibn Thabit, Sahl ibn Hunayf, and al-Harith ibn al-Simmah fought well."
"5106","4271 / 1 - Al-Tayalisi said: Ibn al-Mubarak narrated us, from Ishaq ibn Yahya ibn Talhah ibn Ubaydullah, who said: Isa ibn Talhah informed me, from the Mother of the Believers Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: When Abu Bakr mentioned the day of Uhud he would say: That day was wholly the day of Talhah. Then he began to narrate. He said: I was the first who returned to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Uhud, and I saw a man fighting alongside the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in front of him - he said: I think he was shielding him - he said: I said: Be Talhah, since what eluded me eluded me. I said: That he be a man of my people is dearer to me. Between me and the east was a man I did not know, and I was nearer to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) than him, yet he was striding swiftly in a way I could not. Then he was Abu Ubaydah ibn al-Jarrah. I reached the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with his front tooth broken and wounded in his face, with two rings of the helmet links entered into his cheeks. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Attend to your companion - meaning Talhah, who had bled profusely. We paid no heed to his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I went to pull that from his face (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), but Abu Ubaydah said: I adjure you by my right to leave me. So he left him. He disliked to take it with his hand lest he hurt the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so he clamped upon it with his mouth and extracted one of the rings, and his front tooth fell out with the ring. I went to do what he had done, and he said: I adjure you by my right to leave me. He did as he had done the first time, and his other front tooth fell out with the ring. So Abu Ubaydah was among the most handsome of people though toothless. We attended to the affair of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then we came to Talhah in one of the pits, and he had some seventy or fewer or more stabs, blows, and shots, and his finger had been cut off, so we attended to his affair. 4271 / 2 - Ibn Hibban brought it through Shabab Ibn Sawwar, from Ishaq ibn Yahya, with it."
"5107","25 - Chapter on the campaign of al-Ahzab and Qurayzah. 4272 - Ishaq said: al-Walid ibn Muslim informed us, from Marzuq ibn Abi al-Hudhayl, from Ibn Shihab, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Kab, from Kab ibn Malik, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) returned from pursuing al-Ahzab and alighted at Madinah, he washed, used incense, and laid aside his armor. This is a good chain."
"5108","4273 / 1 - Abu Bakr said: al-Fadl ibn Dukayn narrated us, Yusuf ibn Suhayb narrated us, from Musa ibn Abi al-Mukhtar, from Bilal, from Hudhayfah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The people dispersed from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the night of al-Ahzab, and only twelve men remained with him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to me while I was crouching from the cold, and said: O son of al-Yaman, rise and go to the camp of al-Ahzab and see their condition. I said: O Messenger of Allah, by Him who sent you with the truth, I only rose to you out of embarrassment from the cold. He mentioned the cold of the lava field and the cold of the salt marsh. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Go, O son of al-Yaman, and no harm of cold or heat shall be upon you until you return to me. He said: I went until I came to their camp and found Abu Sufyan kindling fire among a band around him, while al-Ahzab had dispersed from him. I came until I sat among them, and Abu Sufyan sensed that one not of them had entered among them, so he said: Let each man take the hand of his companion. He said: I struck with my right upon the one on my right and took his hand, and struck with my left upon the one on my left and took his hand. I remained among them a while, then I rose and came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was standing in prayer. He gestured to me with his hand to come near, so I drew near him until he cast over me part of the garment upon him to warm me. When he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) finished his prayer, he said: O son of al-Yaman, sit and inform the people? He said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, the people dispersed from Abu Sufyan, and only a band kindling fire remained, and Allah the Exalted poured upon them cold like that He poured upon us, but we hope from Allah what they do not hope. This is a good hadith, its basis is in the Sahih, with additions in this. Al-Bazzar, when he brought it through this Yusuf, said: It is not narrated from Bilal, from Hudhayfah, may Allah be pleased with him, except with this chain."
"5109","4273 / 2 - Ibn Abi Umar said: al-Muqri narrated us, al-Masudi narrated us, from al-Qasim, from Hudhayfah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: When it was the night of al-Ahzab, the people suffered severe hardship, and cold afflicted them the like of which had never afflicted them, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) stood praying. He prayed as much as Allah willed him to pray, then said: Who will rise now and learn for us the news of the people; may Allah whiten his face on the Day of Resurrection? He said: By Allah, none of them could rise because of the hardship upon them. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) prayed as much as Allah willed him to pray, then said: Who will rise now and learn for us the news of the people; may Allah place him with me in Paradise? He said: By Allah, none of them could rise because of the hardship they were in. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O so-and-so, rise. He said: By Him who sent down the Book upon you, I shall not rise to you now. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Hudhayfah, rise. Hudhayfah said: I wanted to swear as my companion had sworn, but the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You are much given to oaths. He said: I rose to him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he said to me: Go and learn for us the news of the people, and do nothing until you return to me. Hudhayfah said: He supplicated for me that Allah protect me from before me and behind me until I return to him. So I went, with a dry salt flat between me and them, and soon crossed it, and there they were in great turmoil, with Abu Sufyan warming himself at their fire from the cold. A small flame of theirs would light at times and dim at times, and when it lit I saw those around it. I said: What am I waiting for? This enemy of Allah, I have seen his place. So I took an arrow from my quiver and placed it in the bow's notch, then I remembered the saying of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Do nothing until you return to me, so I cast it back into the quiver. Then I came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and informed him of their condition, and he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) began praising Allah the Exalted. Then Allah, mighty and majestic, sent the wind, and he mentioned the verse: {O you who believe, remember Allah's favor upon you} the verse."
"5110","4274 - Al-Harith said: Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated us, Abu Ishaq, he is al-Fazari, narrated us, from Sulayman al-Taymi, from Abu Uthman, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) struck in the Trench with his hands, then said: In the name of Allah, and with Him we began; had we worshipped other than Him we would have been wretched. What an excellent Lord, and what an excellent religion."
"5111","4275 - And with it to Abu Ishaq, from Ibn Uyaynah, from Ibn Tawus, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said on the day of al-Khandaq: O Allah, there is no life except the life of the Hereafter, so forgive the Ansar and the Muhajirah, and curse Adal and al-Qarah; they burdened us with carrying stones."
"5112","4276 - And with it to Abu Ishaq: A man of al-Anum narrated us, from Abdullah ibn Yazid, from Abdullah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) ordered the Trench around Madinah. Some people came to him and informed him that they had found a rock they could not pierce. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) rose, and we rose with him. He took the pickaxe and struck, and I never heard a strike of a man louder than it. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah is Greatest, Persia has been conquered. Then he struck another like it, and said (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Allah is Greatest, Rome has been conquered. Then he struck another like it, and said (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Allah is Greatest, Allah has brought Himyar as helpers and supporters."
"5113","4277 - Ishaq said: Rawh, he is Ibn Ubadah, narrated us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated us, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent al-Zubayr and another man on the night of al-Ahzab on a moonlit night, and they observed, then they came while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was in a woolen cloak of Umm Salamah, and he admitted them into the cloak, while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) clung to Umm Salamah. I say: This is mursal with a sound chain, in which there is noted scrutiny."
"5114","26 - Mention of Qurayzah. 4278 - Al-Harith said: Muhammad ibn Umar narrated us, Muhammad ibn Salih narrated us, from Sad ibn Ibrahim, from Amir ibn Sad, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Sad ibn Muadh judged that day that those whom the razor had reached be killed. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You have judged concerning them with the judgment of Allah the Exalted from above seven heavens."
"5115","4279 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated us, from Sufyan ibn Uyaynah, from Amr ibn Dinar, from Ikrimah, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Khawwat ibn Jubayr to Banu Qurayzah to call them, and they said: Our likeness is but the likeness of a man who had two wings, one of them cut off and the other remaining. So they refused. Mursal, its chain sound."
"5116","4280 - Abu Yala said: Sufyan, he is Ibn Waki, narrated us, my father narrated us, from Israil, from Jabir, he is al-Jufi, from Amir, he is al-Shaabi, from Abdullah ibn Yazid, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: When it was Banu Qurayzah, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Call to me your chief to judge concerning his servants, meaning Sad ibn Muadh. He said: He came, and he said to him: Judge. He said: I fear I may not hit Allah's judgment concerning them. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Judge concerning them. So he judged, and he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You have hit the judgment of Allah, mighty and majestic, and His Messenger. This is a Kufan chain, in it two weak narrators: Jabir and Sufyan."
"5117","4281 / 1 - Abu Yala said: al-Qawariri narrated us, Khalid ibn al-Harith narrated us, from Ibn Awn, from al-Hasan, from Umm al-Hasan, who said: The Mother of the Believers Umm Salamah, may Allah be pleased with her, said: I have not forgotten the day of al-Khandaq when he was handing them milk, his hair dusted - meaning the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - while saying: Indeed the good is the good of the Hereafter, so forgive the Ansar and the Muhajirah."
"5118","4281 / 2 - Abu Khaythamah narrated us, Affan narrated us, Yazid ibn Zuray narrated us, Ibn Awn narrated us likewise, and the first is more general. And the hadith of Safiyyah, may Allah be pleased with her, has preceded in her virtue."
"5119","27 - Chapter on the story of the Uraniyyin. 4282 - Ahmad ibn Mani said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated us, Muhammad ibn Amr ibn Himas informed us, from Malik ibn Aws ibn al-Hadathan, who said: Uthman said to Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with them both: Where were you on the day the milch camels of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) were raided? He said: I was at the well drawing water."
"5120","28 - Chapter on the dispatch against Banu Lihyan. 4283 / 1 - Abu Bakr said: Abd al-Rahim ibn Sulayman al-Razi narrated us, Shayban narrated us, from Yahya ibn Abi Kathir, from Said, the freedman of Abu Said, who said: Abu Said, may Allah be pleased with him, informed him that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to them in a campaign in which he dispatched them against Banu Lihyan: Let one man of every two men of you go forth, and the reward is between them."
"5121","4283 / 2 - Ahmad ibn Mani said: Husayn ibn Muhammad narrated us, Shayban narrated us, likewise."
"5122","29 - Chapter on the letter of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to Caesar. 4284 - Al-Harith said: Abu Ubayd narrated us, Abbad ibn al-Awwam narrated us, from Husayn ibn Abd al-Rahman, from Abdullah ibn Shaddad, raising it, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote to Heraclius, the chief of Rome: From Muhammad, the Messenger of Allah, to Heraclius, the chief of Rome. I call you to Islam. If you embrace Islam, yours is what is for the Muslims and upon you is what is upon them. If you do not enter Islam, then pay the jizyah, for Allah the Exalted says: {Fight those who do not believe in Allah} the verse. Otherwise, do not stand between the peasants and Islam, preventing them from entering it or paying the jizyah."
"5123","30 - Chapter on the dispatch of Amr ibn Umayyah al-Damri. 4285 / 1 - Ishaq said: Yahya ibn Adam narrated us, Ibn Abi Zaidah narrated us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq: One of the household of Amr ibn Umayyah al-Damri narrated to me, from his uncles and his family, from Amr ibn Umayyah al-Damri, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me, and sent with me a man of the Ansar, and said: Go to Abu Sufyan and kill him in his courtyard. They became aware of us, so we climbed the mountain. A man of Banu Tamim came to us and I killed him. Then I entered a cave, and a man of Banu Dil ibn Bakr came to us and entered with us. I said: Who are you? He said: Of Banu Bakr. I said: I too am of Banu Bakr. So he lay down and raised his voice singing. He said: I said: Sleep, and you shall know. He slept and I killed him. Then I went out and found two men whom Quraysh had sent. I said to them: Surrender. One of them refused, so I killed him, and the other surrendered. I brought him to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"5124","4285 / 2 - Wahb ibn Jarir informed us: My father narrated, he said: I heard Muhammad ibn Ishaq saying: One of the household of Amr ibn Umayyah al-Damri narrated to me, from his uncles, from Amr ibn Umayyah al-Damri, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me, and sent with me a man of the Ansar, after Khubayb and his companions, may Allah be pleased with them, had been killed, and said: Kill Abu Sufyan in his courtyard. I went out with my companion until we reached Batn Yajaj from the side of al-Shib. He said: My companion was an easy man with no mount of his own, so I said to him: If you fear anything, go to your camel and ride it until you join the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: My companion said to me: Would you circumambulate the House? I said: I know the people of Makkah best; when it darkens they sprinkle their courtyards and sit in them, and I am better known among them than a piebald horse. He kept at me until we circumambulated seven times. Then we went out until we passed their gatherings, and they said: This is Amr; by Allah, no good has brought him. Amr, may Allah be pleased with him, was a daring man called al-Khali. He said: We hastened until we climbed the mountain. I entered a cave, and there was Uthman ibn Malik, or Ubaydullah ibn Malik al-Taymi, cutting fodder for a horse. When he drew near the cave, I said to my companion: By Allah, if this one sees us he will surely point them to us. He said: I went out to him and stabbed him with the dagger beneath his breast, delivering the fatal blow. He cried a cry heard by the people of Makkah. He said: They came, and I returned to my place and entered it. The people of Makkah came and found life still in him. They said: Who stabbed you? He said: Amr ibn Umayyah. Then he died, and they could not get from him what enabled them to inform them of our place. He said: Then we went out, and there was Khubayb upon his cross. My companion said to me: Would you bring Khubayb down from his cross and bury him? I said: Yes. Step aside from me; if I am late for you, take the road. I went to Khubayb and brought him down from his cross, and carried him upon my back. I had not walked with him twenty cubits when the guards surprised me, for they had placed guards over him. He said: I cast him down; I shall never forget the thud on the ground when I cast him. Then I took to al-Safrawat until I descended upon al-Alil, the Alil of Dajnan, with them following me. I entered a cave, and he mentioned the story of the one he killed. Then I went out of the cave by lands I knew, then I took to Rukubah, and saw two men whom Quraysh had sent spying for news. I said to one of them: Surrender. He refused, so I shot him and killed him, and the other surrendered. I brought him to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"5125","4285 / 3 - Abu Bakr said: Jafar ibn Awn narrated us, from Ibrahim ibn Ismail, from al-Zuhri, who said: Jafar ibn Amr ibn Umayyah al-Damri informed me, from his father, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent him alone as a scout to Quraysh. I came to the cross of Khubayb, fearing watchers, and climbed it and freed Khubayb, and he fell to the ground. I withdrew not far, then looked and did not see Khubayb, as if the earth had swallowed him. He said: Khubayb was never seen as remains until this hour. 4285 / 4 - Jafar ibn Awn had said: From Jafar ibn Amr ibn Umayyah, from his father, from his grandfather, may Allah be pleased with them."
"5126","31 - Chapter on al-Hudaybiyyah. 4286 - Ishaq said: Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Ikrimah ibn Ammar informed us, Abu Zumayl Simak al-Hanafi informed us that he heard Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, saying: The writer of the document on the day of al-Hudaybiyyah was Ali ibn Abi Talib. This is a sound chain, with a witness in the Sahih from the hadith of al-Miswar, may Allah be pleased with him, and others."
"5127","4287 - Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Mamar informed us, who said: I asked al-Zuhri: Who was the writer of the document that day? He laughed and said: He was Ali, may Allah be pleased with him. Had you asked these, meaning Banu Umayyah, they would have said: He was Uthman."
"5128","4288 - Abu Bakr said: Waki narrated us, from Ismail, from Qays, from al-Mughirah ibn Shubah, who said: He was standing over the head of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with the sword, veiled, while Urwah, meaning Ibn Masud al-Thaqafi, kept touching the beard of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) as he spoke to him. Al-Mughirah said to him: You shall surely withhold your hand, or your hand shall not return to you. Al-Mughirah wore a sword. Urwah said: Who is this, O Messenger of Allah? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: This is your nephew al-Mughirah. He said: Yes, O treacherous one; I have not yet washed my head of your treachery. This is a chain of utmost soundness, and it is in Sahih al-Bukhari through al-Zuhri, from Urwah, from Marwan ibn al-Hakam and al-Miswar ibn Makhramah, in the long hadith on the story of al-Hudaybiyyah and Umrat al-Qadiyyah, with some disconnection in it, while this is better connected, so I have added it for that reason. And it has preceded in Jihad in the chapter on standing over the leader with the sword."
"5129","4289 - Abu Yala said: Hawtharah ibn Ashras narrated us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated us, from Ali ibn Zayd ibn Judan, who said: Urwah ibn Masud said to his people at the time of al-Hudaybiyyah: O people, I have seen kings and spoken to them, so send me to Muhammad that I may speak to him. So he came to him at al-Hudaybiyyah, and Urwah began speaking to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and touching the beard of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), while al-Mughirah ibn Shubah stood armed over the head of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Al-Mughirah said to him: Withhold your hand before it does not reach you. Urwah raised his head and said: Is it you? By Allah, I am still in your treachery, not yet out of it. Urwah returned to his people and said: O people, I have seen kings and spoken to them; I never saw the like of Muhammad at all. He is no king. I saw the sacrificial animals held back, and I see only a calamity befalling you. He and those of his people who followed him turned away, climbed the wall of Taif, and testified that there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah. A man of his people shot him with an arrow and killed him. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Praise be to Allah who placed in my nation the like of the companion of Yasin. This is mursal or mudal, its basis is also in al-Bukhari from the hadith of al-Miswar and Marwan without what is at its end. What is at the end of this is mistaken; he was only shot with the arrow after the campaign of Taif, after the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had departed from them. Urwah came to him and embraced Islam, returned to them, and they killed him, then they embraced Islam afterwards."
"5130","4290 - Abu Bakr said: Abu Khalid al-Ahmar narrated us, from Yahya ibn Said, from Shurahbil, he is Ibn Sad, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: We came with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in the year of al-Hudaybiyyah, until when we were at al-Suqya, Muadh said: Who will water us in our skins? He said: I went out with some youths with me until we came to al-Uthayah, and we watered and drew water. He said: After the late darkness of the night, a man whose camel disputed with him over the water appeared. He said: It was the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). I took his mount and made it kneel. He said: He advanced and prayed Isha, with me on his right, then prayed thirteen rakahs. Its chain is good."
"5131","32 - The story of the killing of Ibn Abi al-Huqayq. 4291 - Abu Yala said: Abu Kurayb Muhammad ibn al-Ala narrated us, Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated us, Ibrahim ibn Ismail ibn Mujammi al-Ansari narrated us, Ibrahim ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Abdullah ibn Kab ibn Malik narrated me, my father narrated me, from my grandfather, my mother's father, from Abdullah ibn Unays, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me, Abu Qatadah, an ally of theirs of the Ansar, and Abdullah ibn Atik to Ibn Abi al-Huqayq to kill him. We went out by night and followed up their doors, fastening them upon them from outside, then gathered the keys. The people climbed into the palm trees, while I and Abdullah ibn Atik entered the stairway of Ibn Abi al-Huqayq. Abdullah ibn Atik spoke, and Ibn Abi al-Huqayq said: May your mother be bereft of you, Abdullah, how did you come to this town? Get up and open for him, for the noble one is not turned from his door at this hour. She rose, and I said to Abdullah ibn Atik: Take him. He brandished the sword against them, and his wife went to scream, so he brandished the sword against her, but the saying of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that he forbade killing women and children caught up with him, so he held back. Abdullah ibn Unays said: I entered upon him in his upper chamber and stood looking at his intense whiteness in the darkness of the night. When he saw me he took a cushion and shielded himself with it. I went to raise the sword to strike him but could not because of the shortness of the room, so I stabbed him with a thrust. Then I went out, and my companion said: Did you do it? I said: Yes. He entered and stood over him. Then we went out and descended the stairs, and Abdullah ibn Atik fell on the stairs and said: O my leg, my leg is broken. I said: There is nothing wrong with your leg. I laid down my bow and carried him - Abdullah was short and slight - and set him down, and his leg was sound. We went on until we joined our companions. The woman cried: O my houses, and the people of Khaybar rose for his killing. I remembered the place of my bow and said: I shall not return until I take my bow. So I returned while the people of Khaybar were aroused, with no talk among them except: Who killed Ibn Abi al-Huqayq? I looked at no man's face nor did any look at mine except I said as they said: Who killed Ibn Abi al-Huqayq? Until I came to the stairs and climbed with the people, took my bow, then joined my companions. We traveled by night and hid by day, and when we hid by day we posted a watcher to watch for us, until when we neared Madinah and were at al-Bayda I was their watcher. Then I signaled to them with my garment and they descended, coming out at a quick pace, and I descended after them and caught up with them until we reached Madinah. My companions said to me: Did you see anything? I said: No, but I saw the exhaustion that had befallen you, and wished alarm would carry you. We came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was addressing the people, and he said: Successful faces. We said: May your face be successful, O Messenger of Allah. He said: Did you kill him? We said: Yes. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called for the sword with which he was killed and said: This is his food in the sword's edge."
"5132","33 - Chapter on the campaign of Khaybar. 4292 - Al-Harith said: Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated us, Abu Ishaq, he is al-Fazari, narrated us, from Bishr ibn Numayr, from al-Qasim, from Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said in the campaign of Khaybar: Whoever is of doubled mount or of difficult mount, let him return. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded a caller who called out with that. Some people returned. Among the people was a man upon a difficult young camel; he passed in the night over a dark figure, and it shied with him and threw him and broke his neck. When he was brought to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), he said: What is the matter with your companion? They said: Such and such happened to him. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Bilal, had you not called among the people: Whoever is of doubled mount or of difficult mount, let him return? He said: Yes. He said: And he refused to pray over him. Bishr is very weak."
"5133","4293 - Muhammad ibn Umar narrated us, Khalid ibn Rabiah narrated us, from Ata ibn Abi Marwan, from his father, who said: I heard Umm al-Muta al-Aslamiyyah, who had witnessed Khaybar with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), saying: I saw Aslam when they complained to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of straitened circumstances, and he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) urged the people, so they rose. I saw Aslam the first to reach the fortress, and the sun of that day did not set before Allah the Exalted opened it for us. It is the fortress of al-Sab ibn Muadh at al-Natat."
"5134","4294 - Abu Bakr said: Muhammad ibn Fudayl narrated us, from Hajjaj, from Abu Salih, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) apportioned on the day of Khaybar three shares to the horseman and one share to the foot soldier."
"5135","4295 - Al-Harith said: Dawud ibn Amr narrated us, al-Muthanna ibn Zurah Abu Rashid narrated us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq: Buraydah ibn Sufyan ibn Farwah al-Aslami narrated me, from his father, from Salamah ibn al-Akwa, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent Abu Bakr al-Siddiq with his banner to one of the fortresses of Khaybar. He fought and returned without conquest, having striven hard. Then he sent Umar ibn al-Khattab the next day; he fought then returned without conquest, having striven hard. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I shall surely give the banner to a man who loves Allah and His Messenger, through whose hand Allah will grant conquest. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called Ali and spat in his eye, then said: Take this banner and go with it until Allah grants you conquest. Salamah says: He went out with it, by Allah trotting, with us behind him following his track, until he planted his banner in a pile of stones beneath the fortress. A Jew looked down upon him from the top of the fortress and said: Who are you? He said: Ali ibn Abi Talib. The Jew said to his companions: You are overcome, by what was revealed to Musa - or as he said. He did not return until Allah granted conquest through his hand."
"5136","34 - Chapter on the campaign of Mutah. 4296 - Ahmad ibn Mani said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated us, Ismail ibn Abi Khalid informed us, from Qays ibn Abi Hazim, who said: Usamah ibn Zayd came after the killing of his father and stood before the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the eyes of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) shed tears. He came the next day and stood in that same place, and he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Shall I meet from you today what I met from you yesterday? This in form is mursal; if Qays heard it from Usamah, may Allah be pleased with him, then it is sound upon the condition of the two Shaykhs."
"5137","35 - Chapter on the campaign of the Conquest. 4297 - Abu Yala said: Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated us, Abdullah ibn Idris narrated us, from Hizam ibn Hisham ibn Hubaysh, from his father, that he informed him, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, that she said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) angered over the affair of Banu Kab with an anger I had not seen him angered with for a long time, and he said: May Allah not aid me if I do not aid Banu Kab. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Tell Abu Bakr and Umar to prepare for this campaign. He said: They came to Aishah and said to her: Where does the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intend? She said: I saw him angered over the affair of Banu Kab with an anger I had not seen him angered with for a long time."
"5138","4298 - Zuhayr narrated us, Muhammad ibn al-Hasan narrated us, Umm Urwah narrated me, from her sister Aishah bint Jafar, from her father, from her grandfather al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with them, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): That he gave him on the day of the conquest of Makkah the banner of Sad ibn Ubadah, so al-Zubayr entered Makkah with two banners. Muhammad, he is Ibn Zabalah, very weak."
"5139","4299 / 1 - Abu Bakr said: Ishaq ibn Mansur narrated us, al-Hakam ibn Abd al-Malik narrated us, from Qatadah, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered Makkah, he granted safety to the people except four."
"5140","4299 / 2 - Al-Bayhaqi said in al-Dalail: Abu Abdullah al-Hafiz informed us, Abu al-Abbas Muhammad ibn Yaqub informed us, Abu Zurah al-Dimashqi narrated us, al-Hasan ibn Bishr narrated us, al-Hakam ibn Abd al-Malik narrated us, from Qatadah, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) granted safety to the people on the day of the conquest of Makkah except four men: Abd al-Uzza ibn Khatal, Miqyas ibn Subabah, Abdullah ibn Sad, and Umm Sarah. As for Abd al-Uzza ibn Khatal, he was killed while holding the curtains of the Kabah. He said: A man of the Ansar vowed to kill Abdullah ibn Sad if he saw him, and he was the milk brother of Uthman. He brought him to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to intercede for him. When the Ansari saw him he wrapped himself with the sword then came to him, finding him in the circle of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Ansari kept hesitating, disliking to advance upon him because he was in the circle of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) extended his hand and pledged him, then said (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) to the Ansari: I waited for you to fulfill your vow. He said: O Messenger of Allah, I was awed by you; why did you not gesture to me? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: It is not for a Prophet to gesture treacherously. He said: As for Miqyas ibn Subabah, he had a brother with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who was killed by mistake. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent with him a man of Banu Fihr to take his blood money from the Ansar. When the blood money was gathered for him and they returned, the Fihri slept, and Miqyas leapt, took a stone, and struck his head with it and killed him, saying: Nobles. As for Umm Sarah: She was a freedwoman of Quraysh. She came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) complaining of need, and he gave her something. Then a man came to her and sent with her a letter to Makkah... and he mentioned the story of Hatib."
"5141","4299 / 3 - Al-Harith said: Abu Salamah, he is al-Khuzai, narrated us, who said: The name of Ibn Khatal is Abdullah. He had two singing girls who sang satire of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) declared all the people safe except Ibn Khatal, his two singing girls, Abdullah ibn Sad ibn Abi Sarh, and Miqyas ibn Subabah al-Laythi, for he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) granted them no safety. They were all killed except one of the two singing girls, who embraced Islam."
"5142","4300 - Musaddad said: Umayyah ibn Khalid narrated us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated us, from Dawud ibn Abi Hind, from Muhammad ibn Abbad ibn Jafar, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent to Quraysh: To proceed: If you dissociate from the alliance of Banu Bakr or pay blood money for Khuzaah, otherwise I proclaim war to you. Qarazah ibn Abd Amr ibn Nawfal ibn Abd Manaf, the in-law of Muawiyah, said: Banu Bakr are an ill-omened people; we shall not pay for what they killed, leaving us neither little nor much, nor dissociate from their alliance, for none remains upon our religion but them. Rather we proclaim war to him. This is mursal, its chain sound."
"5143","4301 - Ishaq said: Wahb ibn Jarir informed us, my father narrated me, Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrated us, who said: al-Zuhri narrated me, from Ubaydullah ibn Utbah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out to Makkah ten days past of Ramadan, fasting with the people, until when he was at al-Kadid he broke fast. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) alighted at Marr al-Zahran with ten thousand people, among them a thousand of Muzaynah and seven hundred of Banu Sulaym. News had been blinded to Quraysh, so no news of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) reached them, nor did they know what he would do. That night Abu Sufyan ibn Harb, Hakim ibn Hizam, and Budayl ibn Warqa al-Khuzai went out seeking news. Al-Abbas said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) alighted where he alighted, I said: O morning woe of Quraysh; by Allah, if the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) enters Makkah by force, their destruction will last to the end of time. So I rode the white mule of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until I came to al-Arak, hoping to find some woodcutter, milkman, or needy one going to Makkah to inform them of the affair of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) that they might come out to him. By Allah, as I rode seeking what I came for, I heard the speech of Abu Sufyan and Budayl ibn Warqa conversing. Abu Sufyan said: By Allah, I never saw fires or a camp like tonight. Budayl said: By Allah, this is Khuzaah, war has scratched it. Abu Sufyan said: By Allah, Khuzaah is too few and lowly for these to be their fires. I said: O Abu Hanzalah. He recognized my voice and said: Abu al-Fadl? I said: Yes. He said: What is with you, may my father and mother be ransom for you? I said: By Allah, this is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) among the people; O morning woe of Quraysh. He said: What is the device, may my father and mother be ransom for you? I said: By Allah, if he captures you he will strike your neck, so ride behind this mule. He rode, and his two companions turned back. I went out with him, and whenever I passed a fire of the Muslims they said: What is this? When they saw the mule of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) they said: This is the mule of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with his uncle upon it, until I passed the fire of Umar ibn al-Khattab. He said: Who is this? And rose toward me. When he saw him on the mule's rear he recognized him and said: By Allah, the enemy of Allah; praise be to Allah who has enabled you. He went hastening toward the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). I spurred the mule and outpaced him by as much as a slow beast outpaces a slow man. I leapt from the mule and entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and Umar entered and said: This is Allah's enemy Abu Sufyan, whom Allah has enabled without pact or covenant; let me strike his neck. I said: I have granted him protection, O Messenger of Allah. Then I sat by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and took hold of his head, saying: By Allah, no man shall privately converse with him tonight but me. When Umar persisted I said: Gently, O Umar; by Allah, had he been a man of Banu Adi you would not have said this, but he is of Banu Abd Manaf. He said: Gently, O Abbas, do not say this; by Allah, your Islam when you embraced Islam was dearer to me than the Islam of Abu al-Khattab had he embraced Islam, for I knew your Islam was dearer to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) than the Islam of al-Khattab. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Abbas, take him to your lodging, and in the morning bring him to us. I took him to the lodging, and in the morning I brought him. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw him he said: O Abu Sufyan, has the time not come for you to know there is no god but Allah? He said: May my father and mother be ransom for you, how forbearing, generous, joining, and greatly pardoning you are. It had nearly occurred to my mind that had there been another god he would have availed something by now. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Woe to you, O Abu Sufyan, has the time not come for you to know that I am the Messenger of Allah? He said: May my father and mother be ransom for you, how forbearing, generous, joining, and greatly pardoning you are. As for this, there remains something of it in the soul even now. Al-Abbas said: I said: Woe to you, embrace Islam and testify there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah before your neck is struck. So he testified there is no god but Allah and that Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah. Al-Abbas said: I said: O Messenger of Allah, Abu Sufyan is a man fond of pride, so grant him something. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes; whoever enters the house of Abu Sufyan is safe, and whoever locks his door is safe. When he turned to Makkah to inform them, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Detain him at a narrow pass of the valley by the crushing of the cavalry until Allah's troops pass by him. Al-Abbas detained him where the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded. The tribes passed with their banners, and whenever a banner passed he said: Who are these? I said: Banu Sulaym. He said: What have I to do with Banu Sulaym? Then another passed and he said: Who are these? I said: Muzaynah. He said: What have I to do with Muzaynah? He kept saying that until the green brigade of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed, with the Muhajirun and Ansar, of whom only the eyes could be seen. He said: Who is this? I said: This is the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) among the Muhajirun and Ansar. He said: None has power against these. By Allah, the kingship of your nephew has become great today. I said: Woe to you, O Abu Sufyan, it is prophethood. He said: Then yes. I said: Safety to your people. He went out until he came to them at Makkah, crying at the top of his voice: O company of Quraysh, this is Muhammad come to you with what you have no power against. His wife Hind bint Utbah rose, seized his mustache, and said: Kill the fat glutton; the camel has grown spirited from the scout of a people. Abu Sufyan said: Let not this one delude you about yourselves; whoever enters the house of Abu Sufyan is safe. They said: May Allah fight you; what does your house avail us? He said: And whoever locks his door is safe. This is a sound hadith. Mamar, Ibn Uyaynah, and Malik narrated part of it from al-Zuhri concerning the fast, which the two Shaykhs and others brought. Ahmad narrated part of it from the hadith of Ibn Ishaq. Abu Dawud narrated part of it from the story of Abu Sufyan very briefly. None of the six Imams and Ahmad transmitted it in full. Al-Dhuhli narrated it in full among the Zuhriyyat through Abu Idris, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq, but without Ibn Ishaq's explicit statement of hearing it from al-Zuhri. The context here is very good."
"5144","4302 - Abu Yala said: Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Ararah narrated us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi narrated us, Yaqub al-Qummi narrated us, from Jafar ibn Abi al-Mughirah, from Said ibn Jubayr, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) conquered Makkah, Iblis wailed a wail, and his offspring gathered to him. He said: Despair of turning the nation of Muhammad back to polytheism after this day of yours, but spread among them - meaning at Makkah - wailing and poetry."
"5145","4303 - Abu Bakr said: Shabbabah, he is Ibn Sawwar, narrated us, from al-Mughirah ibn Muslim, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: We entered Makkah with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), with three hundred and sixty idols in or around the House worshipped besides Allah the Exalted. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded and they were toppled face down, then said: {Truth has come and falsehood has perished; indeed falsehood is ever perishing}. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) entered the House and prayed two rakahs in it. He saw in it images of Ibrahim, Ismail, and Ishaq, with divining arrows placed in Ibrahim's hand with which to seek division. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: May Allah fight them; Ibrahim never sought division by arrows. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) called for saffron and smeared those images with it. Its chain is good."
"5146","4304 - Abu Yala said: Ishaq ibn Ismail narrated us, Ishaq ibn Sulayman narrated us, from Abu Sinan, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Abu al-Bakhtari, from al-Harith, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intended Makkah, he sent to some of his Companions that he intended Makkah, among them Hatib ibn Abi Baltaah. It spread among the people that he intended Hunayn. He said: Hatib wrote to the people of Makkah that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) intends you. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sent me with Abu Marthad... and he mentioned the hadith on the story of the letter with the woman at Rawdat Khakh, in it Habib ibn Abi Thabit said: She brought it out from her front."
"5147","4305 - Musaddad said: Hammad ibn Zayd narrated us, from Ayyub, from Abdullah ibn Abi Mulaykah, or from another of the people of Makkah, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded Bilal to call the adhan on the day of the Conquest atop the Kabah, while al-Harith ibn Hisham and Safwan ibn Umayyah sat, one beside the other, gesturing toward Bilal. One of them said: Look at this slave. The other said: If Allah dislikes him He will change him. And the hadith of Hatib, may Allah be pleased with him, has preceded in al-Mumtahanah."
"5148","4306 - Ishaq said: Wahb ibn Jarir ibn Hazim informed me, my father narrated me, I heard Muhammad ibn Ishaq narrating from Yahya ibn Abbad, from his father, from Asma bint Abi Bakr, who said: When the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) halted at Dhu Tuwa, Abu Quhafah said to his youngest daughter: Take me up the mountain - he was then blind - and she mentioned the story, in it: The girl had a silver necklace around her neck, and a man passed by her and snatched it. At its end is the saying of Abu Bakr: O little sister, reckon it for reward, for by Allah trustworthiness among people is scarce. Ahmad brought it at length except the last saying of Abu Bakr."
"5149","36 - Chapter on the campaign of Hunayn. 4307 - Abu Bakr said: al-Fadl ibn Dukayn narrated us, Yusuf ibn Suhayb narrated us, from Abdullah ibn Buraydah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: On the day of Hunayn the people were uncovered from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and only one man remained with him called Zayd, holding the bridle of his gray mule. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Woe to you, O Zayd, call the Muhajirun, for Allah the Exalted has a pledge upon their necks. Buraydah, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to me that he said: A thousand of them came, having cast down and broken the scabbards, then came to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until Allah granted them conquest."
"5150","4308 / 1 - Muhammad ibn al-Hasan narrated us, Ibrahim ibn Tahman narrated us, from Abu al-Zubayr, from Utbah, the freedman of Ibn Abbas, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came from Taif he alighted at al-Jiranah, distributed the spoils there, then performed umrah from it, that being with two nights remaining of Shawwal."
"5151","4308 / 2 - Abu Yala said: Abu Bakr narrated us with this."
"5152","4309 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated us, from Awf: Abd al-Rahman, the keeper of the water, narrated me: A man who was with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the day of Hunayn narrated me, who said: When we met, we and the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), they did not stand against us for a milking of a sheep before we uncovered them. While we drove them at their backs until we reached the owner of the white or gray mule, we met by it men white of face, who said: May the faces be disfigured, return. We fled because of their saying, and they rode upon our shoulders; it was that very rout."
"5153","4310 - Abd said: Musa ibn Masud narrated us, Said ibn al-Saib al-Taifi narrated us, my father al-Saib ibn Yasar narrated me, who said: I heard Yazid ibn Amir al-Suwai, who had witnessed Hunayn with the polytheists then embraced Islam. We asked him of the terror Allah the Exalted cast into the hearts of the polytheists on the day of Hunayn, how was it? He said: We would take a pebble and cast it into a basin with water in it and it would ring. He said: We felt in our insides the like of this."
"5154","4311 - And with it to Yazid ibn Amir, who said: He mentioned a rout in which the Muslims were uncovered, and the disbelievers followed them. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took a handful of earth, then turned with it upon the polytheists and cast it into their faces, saying: Return, may the faces be disfigured. He said: None met his brother except complaining of grit in his eyes."
"5155","4312 - Abu Yala said: Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr narrated us, Amr ibn Asim narrated us, Abu al-Awwam narrated us, from Mamar, from al-Zuhri, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: On the day of Hunayn the people fled from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) except al-Abbas ibn Abd al-Muttalib and Abu Sufyan ibn al-Harith. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) commanded al-Abbas to call: O companions of Surat al-Baqarah, O company of the Ansar. Then he urged the call among Banu al-Harith ibn al-Khazraj. When they heard the call they came, and by Allah I likened them only to camels yearning to their young. When they met, fighting joined, and he said: Now the oven has heated. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) took a handful of white pebbles and cast them, saying: They are defeated, by the Lord of the Kabah. Ali ibn Abi Talib that day was the fiercest of people in fighting before him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"5156","38 - Chapter on the campaign of Tabuk. 4313 - Ishaq said: Jarir informed us, from Yazid ibn Abi Ziyad, from Asim ibn Ubaydullah, he is Ibn Hafs ibn Asim ibn Umar, from his father, from his grandfather Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: We went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until, when we were at Ayn al-Rum, which is called the campaign of Tabuk, severe hunger befell us. I said: O Messenger of Allah, we shall meet the enemy tomorrow satiated while we are hungry. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed the people then said: Whoever has surplus food, let him bring it to us. A leather mat was spread, and some twenty-odd sas were brought. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) sat and invoked blessing, then called the people and said: Take. They took until a man tied the sleeve of his shirt and took in it. A remainder remained. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I bear witness there is no god but Allah and that I am the Messenger of Allah; no truthful man says them then enters the Fire."
"5157","4314 - Abu Bakr said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated us, Musa ibn Ubaydah narrated us, Abdullah ibn Abi Qatadah narrated me, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: When we came back from the campaign of Tabuk, he mentioned the hadith. In it: Whoever of you meets any of the laggards, let him not speak to him nor sit with him. And in it: This is Taybah; my Lord settled me in it. It expels the filth of its people as the bellows expels the filth of iron. Whoever of you meets any of the laggards, let him not speak to him nor sit with him."
"5158","4315 - Yazid ibn Harun narrated us, Dawud ibn Abi Hind informed us, from Abu al-Aliyah, from Fadalah al-Zahrani, from al-Mughirah ibn Shubah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: We were with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on a journey. While we traveled with him by night, the mount of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) turned aside with him, and I followed him. When he saw me he said: Where are the people? I said: I left them at such and such place. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) made his mount kneel then dismounted, then went on until hidden from me. He was delayed as long as a man takes to relieve himself... and he mentioned the hadith on wiping over the socks, saying at its end: Then he said: Your need. I said: I have no need. We rode until we caught the people. Its chain is sound."
"5159","4316 - Al-Harith said: Muawiyah ibn Amr narrated us, Abu Ishaq narrated us, from Humayd al-Tawil, from Bakr ibn Abdullah, he is al-Muzani, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Who will take this letter to Caesar, and Paradise is his? A man said: Even if he is not killed? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Even if he is not killed. The man went and brought him the letter, and he read it and said: Go to your Prophet and inform him that I follow him, but I do not wish to leave my kingship. He sent with him dinars to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He returned and informed him. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: He lied, and divided the dinars."
"5160","39 - Chapter on the dispatch of Khalid ibn al-Walid, may Allah be pleased with him, to Ukaydir of Dumah. 4317 - Abu Yala said: Jafar ibn Humayd narrated us, Ubaydullah ibn Iyad narrated us, from his father, from Qays ibn al-Numan, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Cavalry of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) went out, and Ukaydir of Dumat al-Jandal heard of it. He went to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and said: O Messenger of Allah, it reached me that your cavalry has gone out, and I fear for my land and wealth, so write for me a document that nothing of mine be touched, for I acknowledge the right due upon me. So the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wrote for him."
"5161","40 - Chapter on the delegation of Abyssinia. 4318 - Al-Harith said: Surayj ibn Yunus narrated us, Marwan, he is Ibn Muawiyah, narrated us, Khusayf narrated me, from Said ibn Jubayr, who said: The Negus sent to the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) a delegation of his companions. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) recited the Quran to them, and they affirmed and embraced Islam. Concerning them this verse was revealed: {You will surely find the most intense of people in enmity to the believers the Jews and those who associate} up to His saying: {The witnesses}. Then they returned to the Negus, and he embraced Islam. Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) learned of his death, and they prayed over him as prayed over the dead."
"5162","41 - Chapter on the death of our master the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 4319 - Ishaq said: Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Mamar informed us, from Ayyub, from Ikrimah, who said: al-Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I shall surely know how long the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) remains among us. He said: O Messenger of Allah, if you took something to sit upon that wards dust from you and keeps disputants from you. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: By Allah, I shall let them dispute with me for my cloak, tread upon my heels, and cover me with their dust, until Allah the Exalted Himself relieves me of them. He said: I knew his remaining among us was short. He said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died, Umar said: By Allah, I hope the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) lives until he cuts the hands and tongues of men of the hypocrites who say: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) has died. Al-Abbas said: O people, does any of you have a covenant or contract from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? They said: No. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not die until he severed ties and joined, fought and made peace, married women and divorced, and left you upon a clear open way and a straight path. If it is as Umar says, Allah the Exalted is not unable to uncover him and bring him out to us. Leave us with him that we may bury him, for he decomposes as people decompose. I say: al-Tabarani narrated it from the hadith of Ibn Uyaynah, from Ayyub, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, from al-Abbas, may Allah be pleased with him, likewise."
"5163","4320 - Al-Harith said: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar narrated us, Maysarah narrated us, from Abu Aishah, from Yazid ibn Umar, from Abu Salamah, from Abu Hurayrah and Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them, who both said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) addressed us with a sermon before his death, the last sermon he preached at Madinah before joining Allah the Exalted. He mentioned the hadith at length, in it: O people, my age has grown great, my bone has thinned, my body has worn, my soul has been announced to me, my term has drawn near, and I long for my Lord. This is the last covenant between me and you; while I am alive you see me, and when I die Allah is my successor over every Muslim. Peace, mercy, and blessings of Allah be upon you."
"5164","4321 - Abu Yala said: Kamil narrated us, Ibn Lahiah narrated us, Abu al-Aswad narrated us, from Urwah, from Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) died of pleurisy. This hadith is among the rejected reports of Ibn Lahiah."
"5165","4322 - Ubayd ibn Jannad narrated us, Ata ibn Muslim narrated us, from Jafar ibn Burqan, from Ata, from al-Fadl ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: I entered upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in his illness with a red band, or he said yellow, upon him. He said: Cousin, take this band and bind my head with it. So I bound his head with it. He said: Then he leaned upon me until we entered the mosque, and said: O people, I am but a mortal like you; my departure from among you may have drawn near. Whomever I have injured in honor, skin, hair, or wealth, here is the honor, hair, skin, and wealth of Muhammad; let him rise and retaliate. Let none of you say: I fear enmity and rancor from Muhammad; they are not of my nature nor of my character. He said: Then he left. The next day I came to him, and he said: My cousin, I do not think my standing yesterday sufficed for me. Take this band and bind my head with it. He said: I bound his head with it. He said: Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) leaned upon me until he entered the mosque, and said the like of his saying yesterday. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The dearest of you to us is one who retaliates. He said: A man rose and said: O Messenger of Allah, consider the day a beggar came to you asking, and you said: Who has anything to lend us? And I lent you three dirhams. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Fadl, give him. So I gave him. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Whoever is overcome, let him ask us and we shall supplicate for him. He said: A man rose and said: O Messenger of Allah, I am a cowardly man of much sleep. Al-Fadl said: I saw him the bravest of us and least sleeping. He said: Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to the house of Aishah and said to the women the like of what he said to the men."
"5166","4323 - Abu Bakr said: Khalid ibn Makhlad narrated us, from Musa ibn Yaqub al-Zamai: Abu Hazim narrated me, from Sahl ibn Sad, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: People shall console one another after me for consolation in me. People would say: What is this? When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was taken, people met one another consoling one another for the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). This is a good chain."
"5167","4324 - Al-Tayalisi said: Salih ibn Abi al-Akhdar narrated us, from Muhammad ibn al-Munkadir, from Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while he was dead and kissed his forehead."
"5168","4325 - Musaddad said: Abd al-Wahid narrated us, Mamar narrated us, from al-Zuhri, from Said ibn al-Musayyib, who said: Ali said: Four undertook the burial of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and his concealment from the people: Ali, al-Abbas, al-Fadl, and Salih, may Allah be pleased with them. A grave niche was dug for him, and bricks were set upright over him."
"5169","4326 / 1 - Ibn Abi Umar said: Muhammad ibn Jafar ibn Muhammad narrated us, who said: My father used to mention it from his father, from his grandfather, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Some men of Quraysh entered upon him, and he said: Shall I not narrate to you of Abu al-Qasim (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? They said: Yes. He said: Three days before the death of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Allah sent down Jibril, peace be upon him, to him, who said: O Ahmad, Allah has sent me to you honoring you, favoring you, and singling you out. I ask you of what He knows better than you. He says: How do you find yourself? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I find myself, O Jibril, distressed. Then he came to him the second day and mentioned the like exactly, then came to him the third day and mentioned the like exactly, adding: And I find myself, O Jibril, grieved. He said: With Jibril, peace be upon him, there descended an angel in the air called Ismail, over seventy thousand angels. Jibril, peace be upon him, said to him: O Ahmad, this is the Angel of Death seeking permission to enter upon you; he has not sought permission of any human before you, nor shall he seek permission of any human after you. He said: Grant him permission. Jibril, peace be upon him, granted him permission. The Angel of Death said to him: O Ahmad, Allah, mighty and majestic, has sent me to you and commanded me to obey you; if you command me to take your soul I shall take it, and if you dislike it I shall leave it. Jibril, peace be upon him, said: Allah the Exalted longs to meet you. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Angel of Death, proceed with what you were commanded. Jibril, peace be upon him, said: O Ahmad, peace be upon you; this is my last treading of the earth; you were my need of this world. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was taken and the condolence came, a comer came whose sound they heard but whose person they did not see, saying: Peace, mercy of Allah be upon you; in Allah is consolation from every calamity, succession from every perishing, and attainment of all missed. In Allah then trust, and Him then hope, for the deprived is one deprived of reward, and the afflicted is one deprived of reward. Peace be upon you. He said: Do you know who this is? This is al-Khidr, peace be upon him."
"5170","4326 / 2 - Al-Shafii narrated it in the reports which al-Tahawi heard, from al-Muzani, from him, who said: From al-Qasim ibn Abdullah ibn Umar ibn Hafs, from Jafar ibn Muhammad, from his father, who said: Some men of Quraysh entered upon his father Ali ibn al-Husayn, and he said: Shall I not narrate to you of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? They said: Yes. He narrated to us, saying: When he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) fell ill, Jibril came to him... and he mentioned the hadith at length, except he said: Called Ismail, over a hundred thousand angels, each angel of them a hundred thousand. He said in it after 'I shall leave it': He said: Would you do so, O Angel of Death? He said: Yes, thus I was commanded, and commanded to obey you. He said: He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked at Jibril, and Jibril said: O Muhammad... and he mentioned likewise. After his saying: Reward, Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Do you know who this is? This is al-Khidr, peace be upon him."
"5171","4327 - Abdullah ibn Ahmad said in the additions of al-Zuhd: Abu Kamil al-Jahdari narrated us, Abu Awanah narrated us, from Hilal ibn Khabbab, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: When {When Allah's help and victory come} was revealed until he sealed the surah, the soul of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was announced to him when it was revealed, and he took to the severest striving ever in the affair of the Hereafter."
"5172","42 - Chapter on the washing of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 4328 / 1 - Ishaq said: Yazid ibn Abi Hakim al-Adani informed us, al-Hakam ibn Aban narrated us, who said: I heard Ikrimah saying: They heard a voice at the death of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Al-Abbas hastened, and his foot struck the back of a woman of the Prophet's wives (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: O my mother, O my mother, O my mother, do not blame me for this. He found the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: The Highest Companion. Al-Abbas said: I knew he had been given choice. When death decreed upon His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Ali ibn Abi Talib and al-Fadl ibn al-Abbas washed him, while al-Abbas handed them water from behind the curtain. He said: Nothing prevents me from washing him except that we were boys carrying stones in the mosque... the hadith. In it is a break."
"5173","4328 / 2 - Ibrahim ibn al-Hakam ibn Aban informed us, my father narrated me likewise."
"5174","4329 / 1 - Ahmad ibn Mani said: I heard Salamah ibn Salih narrating from Abd al-Malik ibn Abd al-Rahman, from al-Ashath ibn Taliq, that he heard al-Hasan al-Urani narrating from Murrah, from Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him: Our Prophet and beloved (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) - my soul be ransom for him - announced his own soul to us a month before his death. When parting drew near, he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gathered us in the house of our mother Aishah, may Allah be pleased with her. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked at us and his eye shed tears. He testified then said: Welcome to you; may Allah greet you, have mercy on you, shelter you, guard you, aid you, benefit you, guide you, grant you success, keep you safe, accept you, provide for you, and raise you. I enjoin upon you God-consciousness, and I entrust you to Allah, and appoint Him over you. I bear witness to you that I am to you a clear warner, that you exalt not yourselves over Allah concerning His servants and lands, for Allah the Exalted said to me and to you: {That is the Home of the Hereafter} the verse, and He, mighty and majestic, said: {Is there not in Hell a lodging for the arrogant}. We said: When is the term? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The term has drawn near, and the return to Allah, to Sidrat al-Muntaha, to the Garden of Refuge, to the fullest Cup, to the Highest Companion, and to the most pleasant life. We said: Who will wash you? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Men of my household, nearest then nearest. We said: In what shall we shroud you? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: In these garments of mine, or garments of Egypt, or a Yamani suit. We said: Who will pray over you? He said: He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) wept and we wept. He said: Gently; may Allah forgive you and reward you well for your Prophet. When you have washed and shrouded me, place me upon my bed in this house of mine at the edge of this grave of mine, then leave me an hour. The first to pray over me shall be my friend and beloved Jibril, then Mikail, then Israfil, then the Angel of Death with all his hosts of angels, then enter upon me group by group and pray over me and salute with salutation. Do not harm me with commendation, shouting, or wailing. Let the men of my household and their women begin prayer over me, then you after, and whoever of my Companions is absent from me, convey my greeting to him, and whoever enters with you into my religion of my brothers, convey my greeting to him. I bear witness to you that I have greeted whoever follows me upon my religion from this day to the Day of Resurrection. We said: Who will lower you into your grave? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: My family, with many angels who see you whence you do not see them. I say: This was objected against al-Bayhaqi, where he said: Sallam al-Tawil alone narrated it from Abd al-Malik ibn Abd al-Rahman."
"5175","4329 / 2 - Al-Bazzar narrated it: From Muhammad ibn Ismail ibn Samurah, from Abd al-Rahman al-Muharibi, from Ibn al-Asbahani: That he informed him from Murrah, from Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Our beloved and Prophet - may my father be ransom for him, my soul for him - announced his own soul to us a year before his death. When parting drew near, he mentioned the hadith, saying at its end: Whoever enters with you into your religion after me, I bear witness to you that I convey greeting - I think he said - to him and to all who follow me upon my religion from this day of mine to the Day of Resurrection. Al-Bazzar said: This was narrated from Murrah by more than one chain, the chains from Murrah are close, and Abd al-Rahman al-Asbahani did not hear this from Murrah; he was only informed of it from him. We know none narrated it from Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, but Murrah."
"5176","43 - Chapter on the burial of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). 4330 - Ishaq said: Bishr ibn Umar al-Zahrani informed us, who said: I heard Sulayman ibn Bilal narrating, who said: I heard Yahya ibn Said narrating from al-Qasim ibn Muhammad, who said: People differed over the burial of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: No Prophet dies except he is buried where he is taken. So mark around the bed of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), then bury him where he was taken. Ahmad narrated it with a connected weak chain within a hadith, and also brought it with a mudal chain. This mursal way is sounder in origin, supporting that connected one and indicating it has a basis."
"5177","4331 - al-Fadl ibn Musa al-Sinani informed us, Muhammad ibn Amr narrated us, our shaykhs informed us, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was placed by the pulpit, and people prayed over him group by group."
"5178","4332 / 1 - Abu Bakr said: Abu Usamah narrated us, from Mujalid: Amir, he is al-Shaabi, informed us, who said: al-Mughirah ibn Shubah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I am the last of people in covenant with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). We dug for him and made a niche. When they buried him and went out, I cast the pickaxe into the grave, saying: The pickaxe, the pickaxe. I entered and took it, and wiped my hand over the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). I say: Mujalid is weak."
"5179","4332 / 2 - Ahmad ibn Mani said: Hushaym narrated us, Mujalid informed us, from al-Shaabi, from al-Mughirah ibn Shubah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: He used to narrate to us here at Kufah, saying: I am the last of people in covenant with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He said: When Ali ibn Abi Talib came out of the grave and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was buried, I cast my ring, saying: O Abu al-Hasan, my ring. He said: Go down and take your ring. I went down, took my ring, placed it upon the shroud, then went out."
"5180","4332 / 3 - Abu Yala said: Shuja ibn Makhlad narrated us, Hushaym narrated us, Mujalid narrated us with this."
"5181","43 - Book of Tribulations. 1 - Chapter clarifying the beginning of tribulation. 4333 - Ishaq said: al-Mutamir ibn Sulayman informed us, from Layth ibn Abi Sulaym, from Sadaqah, from al-Shaabi, from Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: He mentioned the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and praised him, then mentioned Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, and praised him, then mentioned Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and praised him, then said: After thirty, turn your face where you wish, for you will not turn it except to incapacity or wickedness. I say: In it is a break, along with the weakness of Layth."
"5182","4334 - Abu Bakr said: Ibn Numayr narrated us, from Muhammad ibn Ishaq... and he mentioned the hadith of Hudhayfah, may Allah be pleased with him, which preceded in the Duha prayer."
"5183","4335 - Ishaq said: al-Muqri informed us, Sharik informed us, from al-Mujalid, from Masruq, from Abdullah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to us: The mill of Islam shall turn after thirty-five years; if they reconcile among themselves without fighting, they shall enjoy the world seventy years. I say: Ahmad and Abu Dawud narrated it from the hadith of al-Bara ibn Najiyah, from Ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, with the wording: If they perish, then the path of those who perished, and if their religion stands for them it shall stand seventy years. It does not mention: If they reconcile among themselves without fighting."
"5184","4336 - Suwayd ibn Abd al-Aziz al-Dimashqi informed us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jabir informed us, from Yazid ibn Marthad, from Muadh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Verily the mill of faith turns, so turn with the Book wherever it turns... the hadith."
"5185","4337 - Abu Bakr said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated us, al-Ala ibn al-Minhal al-Atawi narrated us, Muhannad al-Qaysi narrated us - and he was trustworthy - Qays ibn Muslim narrated me, from Tariq ibn Shihab, from Hudhayfah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You are in prophethood and mercy; there shall be caliphate and mercy, and there shall be such and such, and there shall be biting kingship. They drink wine and wear silk, yet with that they shall be aided until the Hour comes."
"5186","4338 - Abu Bakr said: Yunus ibn Muhammad narrated us, Talhah ibn Amr narrated us, Asim ibn Kulayb narrated us, from Abu al-Juwayriyah al-Jarmi, from Zayd ibn Khalid al-Juhani, from Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I went out with the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) one night, heading toward the garden of so-and-so. I brought him purifying water, and when he came I placed it for him. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) began raising his gaze at me and lowering it. He said: Woe to you after me. I wept and said: O Messenger of Allah, shall I remain after you? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Yes. When you see building upon Mount Sala, join the Arabs, the land of Qudaah, for there shall come a day of a bow's length or two bows, or a spear or two spears."
"5187","4339 - Ishaq said: Yahya ibn Yahya informed us, Hushaym narrated us, from Abu Ishaq al-Kufi, from Abu Hariz, from Abdullah ibn Salam, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said: O Messenger of Allah, we find you standing before your Lord, your cheeks reddened, shy before your Lord because of what your nation innovated after you. This is a good chain."
"5188","2 - Chapter on the command to follow the community. Hadiths of it have preceded in faith. 4340 / 1 - Ishaq said: Jarir informed us, from al-Shaybani, from Ibn Yusayr ibn Amr, who said: I heard my father saying: Abu Masud al-Ansari, may Allah be pleased with him, went out to Madinah when Uthman was killed, while I was feverish. I rode and caught up with him at al-Salihin. He was in a garden, so I entered the garden, and some men sat at its far end. He had performed ablution with water running upon his beard. He said: I met him. He said: I praised Allah the Exalted and extolled Him, then said to him: You had two companions to whom recourse was had: Hudhayfah and Abu Musa. I adjure you by Allah the Exalted and adjure you by Islam: if you heard anything from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concerning this tribulation, narrate it to me, otherwise exert your opinion. He praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: Hold to the main body of the nation of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), for Allah, mighty and majestic, would not gather the nation of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon misguidance. Be patient until a righteous one rests or rest is had from a wicked one."
"5189","4340 / 2 - Yahya ibn Adam informed us, Sharik narrated us, from Qays ibn Yusayr ibn Amr, from his father, who said: When Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, was killed, I met Abu Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, in the house of a landowner at al-Salihin, and said to him: Narrate to me what you heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and conceal nothing from me. He said: We conceal nothing, O young man. Hold to the community, and beware division, for it is tribulation and misguidance. Allah the Exalted would not gather the nation of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) upon misguidance."
"5190","4341 / 1 - Abu Bakr said: Ahmad ibn Abdullah narrated us, from Abd al-Hamid ibn Bahram, who said: Shahr ibn Hawshab narrated me: Jundub ibn Sufyan, a man of Bajilah, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated me, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: After me there shall be tribulations like portions of dark night, striking a man as the foreheads of stud bulls strike. A man shall wake a Muslim and sleep a disbeliever, and sleep a believer and wake a disbeliever. A man said: How shall we do then, O Messenger of Allah? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Enter your houses and obscure your mention. A man said: What if one of us is entered upon in his house? He said: Let him hold his hand, and be Allah's slain servant, not Allah's killing servant. For a man shall be in the dome of Islam, then consume his brother's wealth, shed his blood, disobey his Lord, disbelieve in his Creator, and Hell becomes due for him. Its chain is good."
"5191","4341 / 2 - Abu Yala said: Muhammad ibn Bakkar narrated us, Abd al-Hamid ibn Bahram narrated us with it."
"5192","4342 - Abu Yala said: Uqbah ibn Mukram narrated us, Yunus narrated us, Ibn al-Mundhir narrated us, from Nafia ibn al-Harith, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Days and nights shall not pass until one rises saying: Who will sell us his religion for a handful of dirhams?"
"5193","4343 - Abu Bakr said: Ishaq ibn Sulayman al-Razi narrated us, from Abu Sinan, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Abu al-Bakhtari al-Tai, from Ibn Umm Maktum, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came out after the sun had risen, with people by the chambers, and said (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): O people of the chambers, the Fire has been stoked, and tribulations have come like portions of dark night. Had you known what I know you would weep much and laugh little."
"5194","3 - Chapter on leaving stipends fearing tribulation, and urging obedience to Allah, blessed and Exalted. 4344 - Ishaq said: Suwayd ibn Abd al-Aziz al-Dimashqi informed us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid ibn Jabir narrated us, from Yazid ibn Marthad, from Muadh ibn Jabal, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Take stipends while they remain stipends; when they become a bribe upon religion, do not take them. You shall not leave them; fear and poverty shall prevent you from that. Verily the mill of faith turns, so turn with the Book wherever it turns. Verily authority and the Book shall part, so do not part from the Book. There shall be rulers over you: if you obey them they mislead you, and if you disobey them they kill you. They said: How shall we do, O Messenger of Allah? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: As the companions of Isa son of Maryam, peace and prayer be upon him, did: carried upon wood and sawn with saws. Death in obedience to Allah, mighty and majestic, is better than life in disobedience to Him."
"5195","4 - Chapter clarifying that the cause of corruption and tribulations is appointing evil rulers. 4345 - Abu Bakr said: Uqbah ibn Mukram narrated us, Yunus ibn Bukayr narrated us, from Ziyad ibn al-Mundhir, from Nafia ibn al-Harith, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: After me there shall be leaders: if you obey them they make you disbelieve, and if you disobey them they kill you - leaders of disbelief and heads of misguidance."
"5196","4346 - Al-Harith said: Ismail ibn Abi Ismail narrated us, Ismail ibn Ayyash narrated us, Mubarak ibn Hassan al-Sulami narrated us, from al-Hasan al-Basri, from Abdullah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: Everything has a blight that corrupts it, and the blight of this religion is evil rulers."
"5197","4347 - Abu Bakr said: Khalid ibn Makhlad narrated us, from Sulayman ibn Bilal, from Sharik ibn Abdullah ibn Abi Namir, from Asha ibn Abd al-Rahman ibn Mukmil, from Azhar ibn Abdullah, who said: Ubadah, may Allah be pleased with him, came on pilgrimage from Sham and reached Madinah, and came to Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: Shall I not inform you of something I heard from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? He said: Yes. He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: There shall be rulers over you who command you with what you recognize and do what you deny; such have no obedience due from you."
"5198","4348 / 1 - Abu Yala said: Khalifah ibn Khayyat narrated us, Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated us, Hisham ibn Sad narrated us, from Muhammad ibn Uqbah, from Muawiyah ibn Abi Sufyan, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: There shall be rulers against whom none objects, rushing into the Fire, following one another."
"5199","4348 / 2 - Abu Yala said: I found in my book from Suwayd ibn Said, but I doubted it, and most of my thought is that I heard it from him, from Dimam ibn Ismail, from Abu Qabil, who said: Muawiyah, may Allah be pleased with him, addressed us on a Friday, saying: Wealth is but our wealth, and booty is but our booty; whom we wish we give, and whom we wish we withhold. None answered him. On the second Friday he said the like of his saying, and none answered him. On the third Friday he said the like of his saying, and a man who witnessed stood to him, saying: Nay; wealth is but our wealth, and booty is but our booty; whoever stands between us and it, we shall judge him with our swords. When he prayed he commanded the man brought to him, seated him with him upon the couch, then admitted the people to him, then said: O people, I spoke on the first Friday and none answered me, and on the second and none answered me. On the third this one revived me, may Allah revive him. I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: There shall come people who speak and none objects to them, plunging into the Fire like the plunging of monkeys. I feared Allah would make me of them. When this one answered me he revived me, may Allah revive him, and I hope Allah shall not make me of them."
"5200","5 - Chapter clarifying that none of the Companions shall remain past a hundred years after the Hijrah. 4349 / 1 - Abu Bakr said: Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated us, from Bashir ibn al-Muhajir, from Ibn Buraydah, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Toward a hundred years Allah shall send a cool pleasant wind in which He takes the soul of every believer."
"5201","4349 / 2 - Al-Ruyani said: al-Hasan ibn Ibrahim narrated us, Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated us, Bashir informed us with it. Its chain is good."
"5202","6 - Chapter on seclusion in tribulations. 4350 - Abu Yala said: Muhammad ibn Abbad al-Makki narrated us, Muhammad ibn Sulayman al-Masmuli narrated us, al-Qasim ibn Mukhawwal al-Bahzi narrated us, from his father, who said: Then the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) began narrating to us, saying: There shall come upon people a time when the best wealth therein is sheep between the two mosques, eating trees and coming to water; its owner eats of its produce, drinks of its milk, and wears of its wool - or he said its hair - while tribulations plunge among the hordes of the Arabs. By Allah, they care not. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said it three times."
"5203","4351 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated us, from Habib ibn Shihab: My father narrated me, who said: I heard Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, addressing the people at Tabuk: There is none among people like a man holding his horse's head, striving in the way of Allah the Exalted while avoiding people's evils, and like a desert man in blessing who honors his guest and gives his due."
"5204","7 - Chapter on the aid of the people of truth until Allah's command comes. 4352 / 1 - Ishaq said: Muadh ibn Hisham, the companion of al-Dastuwai, informed us: My father narrated me, from Qatadah, from Abu al-Aswad al-Dili, who said: I went with Zurah ibn Damrah and al-Ashari to Umar ibn al-Khattab, and I met Abdullah ibn Amr, who said: Soon none of the Arabs shall remain in the land of the non-Arabs except slain or captive whose blood is judged. Zurah said to him: Shall the polytheists prevail over the people of Islam? He said: Of whom are you? He said: Of Banu Amir ibn Sasah. He said: The Hour shall not come until the shoulders of the women of Banu Amir ibn Sasah crowd upon Dhu al-Khalasah - an idol of the idols of ignorance. He said: We mentioned to Umar the saying of Abdullah ibn Amr, and he said: Abdullah knows best what he says, three times. Then Umar addressed on Friday, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A party of my nation shall remain aided upon truth until Allah's command comes. He said: We mentioned to Abdullah ibn Amr the saying of Umar ibn al-Khattab, and Abdullah ibn Amr said: The Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) spoke truly; when Allah's command, mighty and majestic, comes, what I said shall be. I say: In it is a break between Qatadah and Abu al-Aswad, and its men are trustworthy."
"5205","4352 / 2 - Abu Yala said: Abu Khaythamah narrated us, Muadh ibn Hisham narrated us with it likewise."
"5206","4352 / 3 - Abu Said narrated us, Muadh narrated us with it."
"5207","4352 / 4 - Abu Dawud said: Hammam narrated us, from Qatadah, from Ibn Buraydah, from Sulayman ibn al-Rabi, from Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A party of my nation shall remain upon truth until the Hour comes."
"5208","4352 / 5 - Abu Yala said: Muhammad ibn Ismail ibn Abi Saminah al-Basri narrated us, Abu Dawud narrated us with it."
"5209","8 - Chapter on the command to abandon fighting in tribulation. 4353 - Ishaq said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Mahdi informed us, from Ikrimah ibn Ammar al-Yamami, from a man called Amr: My uncle narrated me, who said: I went out with Muslim ibn Uqbah. When we came abreast of a valley containing Muhammad ibn Maslamah, he sent me to him. I said: What if he does not come to you? He said: Bring me his head. I came to him and said: Answer the commander. He said: Who is the commander? I said: Muslim ibn Uqbah. He said: What does the commander want to do with me, when I pledged the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with this hand of mine, never breaking nor altering? I drew my sword and said: I shall bring him your head. He said: Then bring it. I said: What moves you to that? He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) enjoined upon me, saying: When you see people pledging two commanders, take your sword with which you strove alongside me and strike Uhud with it until it breaks, then sit in your house until a wrongful hand or a decreed death comes to you. I say: Ahmad narrated through him a hadith in the meaning other than this, not with this wording, nor in it: Until a hand comes to you... to its end. This is a soft chain containing one whose condition is unknown."
"5210","4354 - Abu Bakr said: Abd al-Rahman al-Muharibi narrated us, from Layth, from Awn ibn Abi Juhayfah, from Abd al-Rahman, from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Is one of you unable, when a man comes to kill him - meaning of the people of the qiblah - to be thus? He placed one of his hands upon the other, so he is as the better of Adam's two sons; he is in Paradise and his killer in the Fire."
"5211","9 - Chapter on the dislike of disagreement. 4355 - Ishaq said: Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Mamar narrated us, from al-Zuhri, from Sinan ibn Abi Sinan, that he heard al-Husayn ibn Ali, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrating that the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) concealed smoke for Ibn Sayyad, and asked him what he had concealed for him. He said to him: Dukh. He said: Be gone, you shall not exceed your measure. When he turned away, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: What did he say? Some said: Dukh, and some said: Dikh. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You have disagreed while I am among you, and after me you shall disagree more severely."
"5212","4356 - Abu Yala said: Muhammad ibn Marzuq narrated us, Muhammad ibn Bakr narrated us, from al-Salt ibn Bahram: al-Hasan narrated us: Jundub al-Bajali narrated us in this mosque, who said: Hudhayfah, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated him, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The most fearful of what I fear for you is a man who recited the Quran until, when you saw its splendor upon him and he was a support of Islam, he cast it off, threw it behind his back, strove against his neighbor with the sword, and accused him of polytheism. I said: O Prophet of Allah, which of them is more deserving of polytheism: the accuser or the accused? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Rather the accuser."
"5213","10 - Chapter on the prohibition of selling weapons in tribulation. 4357 - Ahmad ibn Mani said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated us, Bahr ibn Kunayz al-Saqqa informed us, from Abdullah al-Luqayti, from Abu Raja, from Imran ibn Husayn, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade selling weapons in tribulation."
"5214","11 - Chapter on the sign of the first tribulations. 4358 - al-Tayalisi said: Ibn Abi Dhib narrated us, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, who said: Two men disputed to Abu al-Darda, may Allah be pleased with him, over a span of land, and Abu al-Darda said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saying: When you are in a land and hear two men disputing over a span of land, leave it. He said: Abu al-Darda left and came to Sham."
"5215","12 - Chapter on the permissibility of monasticism in days of tribulations. 4359 - Abu Yala said: Abu Kurayb narrated us, Ibrahim ibn Ayyash narrated us, Rawwad narrated us, from Sufyan, from Mansur, from Ribi, from Hudhayfah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The best of you at the turn of two hundred years is the light-backed. It was said: O Messenger of Allah, what is lightness of back? He said: One with neither family nor wealth."
"5216","4360 - Al-Harith said: Abd al-Rahim ibn Waqid narrated us, Masadah ibn Sadaqah narrated us, Sufyan al-Thawri narrated us, from his father, from al-Rabi ibn Khuthaym, from Abdullah ibn Masud, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There shall come upon people a time when bachelorhood becomes lawful, and none with religion keeps his religion except one who flees with his religion from peak to peak and from hole to hole, like a bird fleeing with its chicks and like a fox with its cubs. He establishes prayer, gives zakah, and withdraws from people except for good. A hundred dusky sheep I herd at Sala is dearer to me than the kingship of Banu al-Nadir, and that when such and such occurs."
"5217","13 - Chapter on the number of tribulations. 4361 - Ishaq said: I said to Abu Usamah: Did al-Amash narrate to you, from Mundhir al-Thawri, from Asim ibn Damrah, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Allah, mighty and majestic, placed in this nation five tribulations: a private tribulation, then a public tribulation, then a private tribulation, then a public tribulation, then comes a black dark tribulation in which people become as cattle. Abu Usamah affirmed it and said: Yes. There occurs between this section and the coming section in all copies what follows:"
"5218","14 - Chapter on the beginning of tribulations and the story of the appointment of Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him. It has preceded in the virtues of Umar the hadith of his making the affair consultation among six. 4362 - Al-Harith said: Abu al-Nadr Hashim ibn al-Qasim narrated us, al-Layth ibn Sad narrated us, from Ubaydullah ibn Umar, from Ibn Shihab, that he narrated him, from al-Miswar ibn Makhramah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: On the night whose morning the men whom Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with them, appointed would finish with the caliphate, I prayed Isha then returned to my house and slept. The voice of my uncle Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, may Allah have mercy on him, woke me from sleep: O Miswar. He said: I came out wrapped in my garment. He said: Did you sleep? I said: Yes, I slept. He said: Take your garment then join me at the mosque. I did. When I reached him he said to me: Call al-Zubayr and Sad for me, or one of them. I went and called him. When I brought him to him he said: Step back from us as far as not to hear our speech. He said: I did, and they conversed a little. Then he said: Call the other for me. When I brought him to him he said: Step back from us as far as not to hear our speech. He said: They conversed a little. Then he called: O Miswar, go and call Ali for me - that when the darkness of Isha had gone. He said: I brought Ali. He said: Step back from us as far as not to hear our speech. He said: They kept speaking from Isha until dawn, except I did not hear from their mouths what makes me think they had turned toward him. At dawn he called me, with Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, by him, saying: Go and call Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, for me. He said: I did. They conversed privately, and the caller called for dawn. He said: They dispersed for ablution, while people knew it was the morning of the caliphate. They gathered for dawn as they gather for Friday. Abd al-Rahman, may Allah be pleased with him, commanded the men to sit before the pulpit. When people saw one another and the sun rose, Abd al-Rahman, may Allah be pleased with him, stood beside the pulpit, praised Allah the Exalted and extolled Him as He deserves, then said: O people, you know what occurred of the death of the Commander of the Believers and his appointing us, O men; my companions agreed it is for me, and I shall choose a man of them, and here they are before you. Then he faced them one by one, saying: O so-and-so, upon you is Allah's covenant and pact: shall you hear and obey whom I appoint, accept and submit? He said: Yes, raising his voice so people heard, until he finished with them one by one: Uthman, Ali, al-Zubayr, and Sad, may Allah be pleased with them. He said: As for Talhah, I guarantee his consent. Then he said: I have kept asking you about these men for three days, then asked them of themselves, and found you, O people, and them agreed upon Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him. Rise, O Uthman. No man of the Muhajirun, Ansar, Arab delegations, or righteous people said: You did not consult us nor seek our command. They accepted and submitted. They remained six years blaming nothing. He said: Some of them preferred him over Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: Justice is as Umar, and gentleness gentler than Umar. Then what happened happened."
"5219","4363 - And with it al-Layth said: From Usamah ibn Zayd, from a man of them, meaning Abd al-Rahman, may Allah be pleased with him: Whenever he called one of them that night he mentioned their virtues, then said: You are worthy of it; if it misses you, whom? He said: If it misses me, then Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him."
"5220","Book of Conquests. 15 - Mention of the conquests of Iraq. 4364 - Abu Dawud said: Wuhayb narrated us, from Dawud, from Abu Nadrah, from Abu Said, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Zayd ibn Thabit, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked toward Iraq and said: O Allah, turn their hearts."
"5221","Ibn Abi Umar said: Bishr ibn al-Sari narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, from Abu Imran al-Jawni, from Alqamah ibn Abdullah al-Muzani, from Ma'qil ibn Yasar, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Umar ibn al-Khattab consulted al-Hurmuzan about Isbahan, Fars and Adharbayjan, with which of them to begin. He said: O Commander of the Believers, Isbahan is the head, and Fars and Adharbayjan are the two wings; if you cut off one of the wings the head will take refuge with the other wing, and if you cut off the head the two wings will fall, so begin with Isbahan. He said: And Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, entered the mosque, and there was al-Nu'man ibn Muqarrin praying, so he waited for him until he finished his prayer, then said: I am appointing you. He said: As a tax-collector, no, but as a warrior. He said: Then you are a warrior. He said: So he dispatched him and wrote to the people of Kufah to join him, and among them were al-Zubayr ibn al-Awwam, Hudhayfah ibn al-Yaman, Abdullah ibn Amr, al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah, al-Ash'ath ibn Qays, and Amr ibn Ma'di Karib, may Allah be pleased with them. He said: Al-Nu'man, may Allah be pleased with him, came to them with a river between him and them, so he sent al-Mughirah ibn Shu'bah to them, and their king was Dhu al-Janahayn. He consulted his companions and said: What do you think, shall I sit for them in the array of war, or sit for them in the array of kingship and its splendor? They said: No, rather sit for them in the array of kingship and its splendor."
"5222","Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Hammad ibn Salamah, from Asim ibn Bahdalah, from Abu Wa'il who said: Khalid ibn al-Walid, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote to Mihran and Rustum and the land of Fars: From Khalid ibn al-Walid to Mihran and Rustum: Peace be upon whoever follows guidance. As for what follows: I offer you Islam; if you affirm Islam, for you is what belongs to Islam and upon you is what is upon Islam, and if you refuse, I offer you the jizyah, and if you refuse, then with me are men who love fighting as Fars loves wine."
"5223","Abu Yala said: Abu al-Harith, Surayj ibn Yunus, narrated to us, Yahya ibn Zakariyya ibn Abi Za'idah narrated to us, Mujalid narrated, from Amir, meaning al-Sha'bi, who said: Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, wrote to Khalid, may Allah be pleased with him, on the day of al-Yamamah and the killing of the people of apostasy, to march to al-Hirah then proceed to al-Sham. When he alighted at al-Hirah he wrote to the people of Fars, then said: I love not to depart until I frighten them, so he raided them until he reached Sura, killing and taking captives, then raided Ayn al-Tamr, killing and taking captives, then proceeded to al-Sham. Amir, meaning al-Sha'bi, said: Ibn Buqaylah, meaning Abd al-Masih al-Himyari, brought out to me the letter of Khalid ibn al-Walid, may Allah be pleased with him, to them: In the name of Allah, the Most Merciful, the Most Compassionate; from Khalid ibn al-Walid to the marzbans of Fars: Peace be upon whoever follows guidance. As for what follows: I praise Allah, besides whom there is no god, with the praise He deserves, who has severed your sanctities, scattered your unity, weakened your might and stripped your kingdom. When this letter of mine reaches you, accept protection from me, pay the jizyah, and send hostages to me; otherwise, by the One besides whom there is no god, I shall surely fight you with a people who love death as you love life. And peace be upon whoever follows guidance. And the hadith of Nadlah ibn Amr about the conquest of Hulwan will come, if Allah Almighty wills, in the tribulations."
"5224","Chapter: What occurred in the caliphate of Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, of conquests. Musaddad said: Abdullah ibn Dawud narrated to us, from Bishr ibn Abi Ishaq, from Abu al-Safar who said: Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, when he dispatched to al-Sham, pledged them upon piercing and plague."
"5225","Abu Yala said: Zuhayr narrated to us, Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated, Umar ibn Sa'id ibn Abi Husayn narrated, Ibn Abi Mulaykah narrated, from Dhakwan, the freedman of A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: A small box was brought to Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and most of his companions looked at it but did not know its value. He said: Do you permit me to send it to A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, because of the love of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) for her? They said: Yes. It was brought to A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and she opened it, and it was said: Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, sent this to you. She, may Allah be pleased with her, said: What has Ibn al-Khattab opened upon me after the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? O Allah, do not keep me alive for next year's gift."
"5226","Chapter: The conquest of Alexandria. Abu Yala said: Wahb ibn Baqiyyah narrated to us, Khalid, he is al-Tahhan, narrated, from Muhammad ibn Amr, from his father, he is Amr ibn Alqamah ibn Waqqas, from his grandfather who said: Amr ibn al-As, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: An army of Muslims set out, I being their commander, until we alighted at Alexandria. A great one of their great ones said: Bring out to me a man whom I may address and who may address me. I said: None but I shall go out to him. So I went out with an interpreter with me, and with him his interpreter, until two pulpits were placed for us. He said: Who are you? We said: We are Arabs, people of thorn and qaraz (fodder); we are the people of the House of Allah, Mighty and Majestic. We were the narrowest of people in land and the worst in livelihood; we ate carrion and blood, and raided one another; we lived the worst life lived by people, until there emerged among us a man who was on that day neither the greatest of us in nobility nor the richest of us in wealth. He said: I am the Messenger of Allah to you; he commands us with what we do not know, and forbids us what we were upon and our fathers were upon. So we reviled him, disbelieved him, and rejected his word, until a people other than us went out to him and said: We believe you, believe in you, follow you, and fight whoever fights you. So he went out to them, and we went out against him, and he fought us and we fought him, and he killed us and prevailed over us and overcame us, and took on the Arabs next to him, fighting them until he prevailed over them. If those of the Arabs behind me knew the life you are in, none would remain but would come to you to share with you the life you are in. He laughed, then said: Your messenger has spoken truthfully; our messengers had come to us with the like of what your messenger brought, and we were upon it, until kings appeared among us who began to act among us according to their desires and abandon the command of the prophets. If you hold to the command of your Prophet, none will fight you but you will overcome him, and none will face you but you will prevail over him; but if you do the like of what we did, abandoning the command of your Prophet and acting as they acted according to their desires, the matter will be left between us and you, and you will not be more numerous than us nor stronger than us. Amr ibn al-As, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I never spoke to a more cunning man than him."
"5227","Al-Harith said: Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Muqri narrated to us, Musa ibn Ulayy ibn Rabah narrated, who said: I heard my father say: When the people of Alexandria resisted Amr ibn al-As, may Allah be pleased with him, he set up the mangonel against it."
"5228","Chapter: The murder of Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him. Ishaq said: al-Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman informed us, my father informed me, Abu Nadrah informed me, from Abu Sa'id, the freedman of Abu Usayd al-Ansari, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, heard that the delegation of the people of Egypt had come, so he went out to meet them, and he, may Allah be pleased with him, was in a village outside Madinah, or as he said. When they heard of him they came toward him to the place he was in. They said: He disliked that they come to him in Madinah, or the like of that. They came to him and said to him: Call for the mushaf. He said: So he called for the mushaf. They said to him: Open the seventh, and they used to call Surah Yunus the seventh. He recited until he came to this verse: Say: Have you considered what Allah has sent down to you of provision, of which you made some unlawful and some lawful; say: Did Allah permit you, or do you forge against Allah? They said to him: Stop; as for what has been reserved of Allah Almighty's reserve, did Allah permit you, or do you forge against Allah? He said: Carry on; it was revealed concerning such-and-such, and as for the reserve, Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, had reserved the reserve before me for the camels of charity, and when I took charge I reserved for the camels of charity. Carry on. They kept holding him to the verse, and he kept saying: Carry on; it was revealed concerning such-and-such. He said: The one who used to attend upon Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, was of your age. He said: Abu Nadrah used to say that to me, Abu Sa'id, I being of your age, my father said: and my face had not yet grown hair then, I do not know, perhaps he said another time: I being then thirty years old. He said: Then they took hold of him over matters for which he had no escape, so he acknowledged them, and said: I seek Allah's forgiveness and repent to Him. Then he said to them: What do you want? They said: So they took his covenant and he wrote a condition upon them, then he took upon them that they not split the staff nor leave the community, so long as he fulfilled their condition for them, or as they took upon him. Then he said to them: What do you want? They said: We want that the people of Madinah not take a stipend, for this wealth belongs only to whoever fought for it, and to these elders among the companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). So they were satisfied and came with him to Madinah satisfied. He said: So he, may Allah be pleased with him, stood and addressed them, saying: By Allah, I have never seen a delegation on earth better than this delegation of the people of Egypt. Whoever has crops, let him join his crops, and whoever has a milking animal, let him milk. Indeed there is no wealth for you with us. He said: So the people became angry and said: This is the plotting of Banu Umayyah. Then the Egyptian delegation returned satisfied, but while they were on the road, a rider appeared to them, approaching them then leaving them, then returning to them, then leaving them, and reviling them. They said to him: What is with you? You must have some matter; what is your affair? He said: I am the messenger of the Commander of the Believers to his governor in Egypt. They searched him, and there was the letter with him in the words of Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, bearing his seal, to his governor in Egypt, to kill them or crucify them, or cut off their hands and feet from opposite sides. So they came until they arrived at Madinah, and came to Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: Do you not see the enemy of Allah writing concerning us such-and-such? Allah has made his blood lawful. Stand with us against him. He said: By Allah, I shall not stand with you against him. They said: Then why did you write to us? He said: By Allah, I never wrote a letter to you at all. He said: Some of them looked at others. They said: Is it for this that you fight, or for this that you are angry? Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, set out leaving Madinah for a village. They set out until they entered upon Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, saying to him: You wrote concerning us such-and-such, and Allah has made your blood lawful. He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: There are two matters: either you establish against me two men of the Muslims, or my oath by Allah besides whom there is no god that I did not write, nor dictate, nor know; and you know that a letter may be written in a man's handwriting, and a seal may be engraved upon a seal. They said: By Allah, Allah has made your blood lawful for breaking the covenant and pact. He said: So they besieged him, may Allah be pleased with him. One day, while besieged, he looked down upon them and said: Peace be upon you. Abu Sa'id, may Allah be pleased with him, said: By Allah, I did not hear anyone of the people return the greeting except a man returning it to himself. He said: I adjure you by Allah besides whom there is no god, do you know? He said: Then he mentioned something about his affair, and also mentioned, I think, his writing of the Mufassal in detail, and the prohibition spread, and people began to say: Gently with the Commander of the Believers. The prohibition spread, and al-Ashtar stood and said, I do not know, whether that day or another day. He said: Perhaps he and you have been plotted against. He said: The people trampled him until he met such-and-such. Then he, may Allah be pleased with him, looked down upon them another time, exhorting and reminding them, but the exhortation did not take effect in them; people used to be affected by exhortation the first time they heard it, but when it was repeated to them it did not affect them. He said: Then he, may Allah be pleased with him, opened the door and placed the mushaf before him, and that was because he had seen the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said to him: O Uthman, break your fast with us tonight. My father said: al-Hasan narrated to me that Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, entered upon him and took hold of his beard, and he, may Allah be pleased with him, said: You have taken from me a hold, or sat from me in a seat, that your father would not have sat, or he said: would not have taken. So he went out and left him. And a man called al-Mawt al-Aswad entered upon him, may Allah be pleased with him, and strangled him, then strangled him, then went out saying: By Allah, I strangled him, and I never saw anything softer than his throat, until I saw his breath moving in his body like the breath of a jinn. He said: So he went out and left him. He said: And in the hadith of Abu Sa'id, may Allah be pleased with him: A man entered upon him and said: Between me and you is the Book of Allah, then went out and left him. Then another entered upon him and said: Between me and you is the Book of Allah Almighty, with the mushaf before him, and he struck with the sword, and Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, shielded himself with his hand, so he cut it; I do not know whether he severed it or cut it without severing. Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, said: By Allah, it is the first hand to write the Mufassal. He said: And he said in other than the hadith of Abu Sa'id, may Allah be pleased with him: al-Tujibi entered upon him and pierced him with an arrowhead, and blood splashed upon this verse: So Allah will suffice you against them, and He is the Hearing, the Knowing. He said: It is in the mushaf, not yet effaced. He said: And Bint al-Farafisah, may Allah be pleased with her, took her jewelry in the hadith of Abu Sa'id, may Allah be pleased with him, and placed it in her lap, and that was before he was killed, may Allah be pleased with him. When he was pierced, or killed, she spread herself over him, and one of them said: May Allah kill her, how great her buttocks are! Abu Sa'id, may Allah be pleased with him, said: So I knew that the enemies of Allah wanted nothing but this world. I said: Its men are trustworthy, each hearing from the other."
"5229","Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Ma'mar informed us, from al-Zuhri, from Kathir ibn Aflah, the freedman of Abu Ayyub al-Ansari, from his father who said: Abdullah ibn Salam, may Allah be pleased with him, before the people of Egypt came, used to enter upon the chiefs of Quraysh saying to them: Do not kill this man, meaning Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him. They would say: By Allah, we do not intend to kill him. Then he would go out leaning on my hand, saying: By Allah, they will surely kill him. Then he said to them: Do not kill him, for by Allah he will die within forty days. They refused. Then he went out to them after some days saying to them: Do not kill him, for by Allah he will die within fifteen nights. This is a fair chain."
"5230","Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Ma'mar informed us, from Ayyub, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Ibn Salam, may Allah be pleased with him, that he said to them: The angels have not ceased surrounding this city of yours since the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to it until today. By Allah, if you kill him they will surely depart and never return to you. And the sword has not ceased sheathed among you; by Allah, if you kill him Allah will surely unsheathe it against you, then it will never be sheathed from you again, or he said: until the Day of Resurrection. No prophet was killed but seventy thousand were killed for him, and no caliph was killed but thirty-five thousand were killed for him. And he mentioned that seventy thousand were killed for the blood of Yahya ibn Zakariyya."
"5231","Mu'adh ibn Hisham informed us, my father narrated to me, from Ayyub al-Ahmar, he is Ayyub ibn A'idh, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Abdullah ibn Mughaffal who said: Abdullah ibn Salam, may Allah be pleased with him, used to come on a she-ass from a land of his on Friday. He mentioned the hadith like the hadith of Ma'mar."
"5232","Al-Nadr ibn Shumayl narrated to us, Sulayman, he is Ibn al-Mughirah, narrated, Humayd, he is Ibn Hilal al-Adawi, narrated, Abdullah ibn Mughaffal narrated, who said: Abdullah ibn Salam, may Allah be pleased with him, used to come from a land of his on a she-ass or donkey on Friday, arriving early; when he finished the prayer he went to his land. When the people rose against Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, Abdullah ibn Salam, may Allah be pleased with him, said to them: Do not kill him, and seek his redress. By the One in whose Hand is my soul, no community killed its prophet but Allah set right their relations only after seventy thousand of their blood was shed, and no community killed a caliph but Allah set right their relations only after forty thousand of their blood was shed. And no community ever perished until they raised the Qur'an against authority. Then he said to them: Do not kill him, and seek his redress. He said: They paid no heed to what he said, and killed him. He said: Then he sat on the road of Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, until Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, came to him. He said to him: Where are you heading? He said: Iraq. He said: Do not go to Iraq; keep to the pulpit of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), stay with it, and I do not know whether it will save you. By Allah, if you leave it you will never see it again. Those around him said: Let us kill him. Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Abdullah ibn Salam is a righteous man from us. Ibn Mughaffal said: I had asked permission of Ibn Salam regarding a land next to his land to buy it, and he said to me after that: This is the beginning of forty years, and after it there will be peace, so buy it. Sulayman said: I said to Humayd: How do they raise the Qur'an against authority? He said: Have you not seen the Khawarij, how they interpret the Qur'an against authority."
"5233","Musaddad said: Khalid ibn Ziyad al-Zayyat narrated to us, Zur'ah ibn Amr narrated to me, from his father who said: My father was one of four who buried Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, on the day of the House, after the last night prayer, in al-Baqi'."
"5234","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Shababab ibn Sawwar narrated to us, Yahya ibn Abi Rashid, the freedman of Amr ibn Hurayth, narrated, from Uqbah ibn Usayd and another man whom he named, both from al-Nu'man ibn Bashir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Na'ilah bint al-Farafisah al-Kalbiyyah, the wife of Uthman, may Allah be pleased with them both, narrated to me, saying: When Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, was besieged, he spent his day fasting. At the time of breaking the fast he asked them for sweet water. They said: Before you is this well, and it was a well into which filth was thrown. He spent that night in that state, not tasting. At dawn I went to a neighboring woman over connected rooftops, meaning adjoining roofs, and asked them for sweet water, and brought him a jug of water. When I came down, he, may Allah be pleased with him, was asleep at the bottom of the stairs, snoring. I woke him and said: Here is sweet water I have brought you. He, may Allah be pleased with him, raised his head and looked at the dawn, saying: I am fasting; I have woken fasting. I said: From where, when I have seen no one bring you food or drink? He said: The Messenger of Allah looked down upon me from this ceiling, with a bucket of water with him, saying: Drink, O Uthman. So I drank and was quenched, then he said: More. So I drank until I was full. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The people will deny you; if you leave them you will break your fast with us. She said: They entered upon him that day and killed him, may Allah be pleased with him."
"5235","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Abd al-Malik ibn Abi Sulayman narrated, from Abu Layla al-Kindi who said: Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, looked down upon us on the day of the House, saying: O people, do not kill me, for if you kill me you will be like this, and he interlaced his fingers, may Allah be pleased with him."
"5236","Al-Harith said: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us, Abu al-Miqdam Hisham ibn Ziyad narrated, from Hisham ibn Urwah, from his father, from A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, sought permission from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and he permitted him. He entered with his waist-wrapper untied. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Draw near to me, O Uthman. So he drew near to him. Then he said: Draw near to me, O Uthman. So he drew near to him until his knee, may Allah be pleased with him, touched the knee of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) buttoned him up with his hand. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: O Uthman, you will come on the Day of Resurrection with your jugular veins gushing blood. I shall say: Who did this to you? Then you will name and complain, amid a commander, a plotter and a deserter. While you are thus, you will hear a caller calling from heaven: Indeed Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, in the judgment of his enemies, is a close ally. How will you be then, O Uthman? He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: There is no might nor power except through Allah, three times."
"5237","Al-Hasan ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, Husayn the teacher narrated, from Nafi' who said: Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, wore armor on the day of the House, the house of Uthman, may Allah be pleased with them both, twice. He entered upon him and said: I accompanied the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I used to recognize for him the right of prophethood and the right of authority. I accompanied Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, and I used to recognize for him the right of authority. Then I accompanied Umar ibn al-Khattab, may Allah be pleased with him, and I used to recognize for him the right of authority and the right of fatherhood. And I recognize for you the like of that. He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: May Allah reward you with good, O family of Umar; sit in your house until my command reaches you."
"5238","Khalid ibn al-Qasim narrated to us, Abu Ma'shar narrated, who said: I heard Abu Sa'id al-Maqburi narrating from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I was besieged in the house with Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him. They shot a man of ours and killed him. I said: O Commander of the Believers, fighting has become good; they have killed a man of ours. He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I adjure you, O Abu Hurayrah, to throw down your sword, for only my soul is sought, and I shall shield the Muslims with my soul. Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: So I threw down my sword, and I do not know where it is until this hour."
"5239","Al-Hasan ibn Qutaybah narrated to us, Ibn Fadalah narrated, from Muhajir ibn Habib and Ibrahim ibn Masqalah who said: Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, sent to Abdullah ibn Salam, may Allah be pleased with him, while besieged. He entered upon him and said to him: Raise your head; do you see this skylight? The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) looked down from it last night and said: O Uthman, have they besieged you? I said: Yes. He let down to me a bucket from which I drank, and I still feel its coolness upon my liver. Then he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: If you wish, I shall supplicate Allah Almighty and He will aid you against them, and if you wish, you will break your fast with us. Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I said to him: Which did you choose? He said: Breaking fast with him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, left for his house. When the day rose he said to his son: Go out and see what Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, has done, for he ought not to be alive at this hour. He went to him and said: The man has been killed."
"5240","Abu Yala said: Ubaydullah ibn Umar narrated to us, Affan narrated, Wuhayb narrated, from Musa ibn Uqbah, from Abu Alqamah, the freedman of Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, from Kathir ibn al-Salt who said: Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, slept on the day he was killed, which was Friday. When he awoke he said: Were it not that people would say: Uthman wished a wish, I would narrate to you a hadith. He said: We said: Narrate to us, may Allah set you right, for we shall not say as people say. He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in this dream of mine, and he said: You will attend Friday with us."
"5241","Al-Bazzar said: Muhammad ibn al-Muthanna narrated to us, al-Mughirah ibn Maslamah narrated, Wuhayb narrated, from Musa; Abu Alqamah narrated to us with it. And al-Hakim brought it."
"5242","Abu Yala also said: Ishaq ibn Ismail narrated, Ishaq ibn Sulayman al-Razi narrated, who said: I heard Abu Ja'far al-Razi mentioning from Ayyub al-Sakhtiyani, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, woke in the morning narrating to the people, saying: I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in a dream, and he said: O Uthman, break your fast with us. He, may Allah be pleased with him, woke fasting, and was killed that day, may Allah be pleased with him."
"5243","Al-Bazzar narrated it from Ibrahim ibn Ziyad, from Ishaq ibn Sulayman with it. And al-Hakim brought it from the path of Ishaq ibn Sulayman also, and authenticated it."
"5244","Abu Yala said: Ibrahim ibn Muhammad ibn Ar'arah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Abbad al-Huna'i narrated, Ibn Abi Fadalah narrated, al-Hadrami informed me, from Abu Maryam ibn Radi' al-Jarud who said: I was in Kufah, and al-Hasan ibn Ali, may Allah be pleased with them both, stood as orator and said: O people, last night I saw an amazing thing in my dream. I saw the Lord, Blessed and Exalted, above His Throne. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came until he stood at one of the legs of the Throne. Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, came and placed his hand upon the shoulder of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Then Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, came and placed his hand upon the shoulder of Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him. Then Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, came and was apart. He said: O Lord, ask Your servants why they killed me. He said: Then two spouts of blood gushed from heaven onto the earth. He said: It was said to Ali, may Allah be pleased with him: Do you not see what al-Hasan, may Allah be pleased with him, narrates? He, may Allah's pleasure be upon him, said: He narrates what he saw."
"5245","Sufyan ibn Waki' narrated to us, Jumay' ibn Umayr al-Ijli narrated, from Mujahid or Mujalid, from Tuhrub al-Ijli, from al-Hasan ibn Ali, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: I shall not fight after a vision I saw. I saw the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) placing his hand upon the Throne, and I saw Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, placing his hand upon the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I saw Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, placing his hand upon Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, and I saw Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, placing his hand upon Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, and I saw blood between them. I said: What is this blood? It was said: The blood of Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him; Allah Almighty seeks retribution for it."
"5246","Musaddad said: Abu Abdullah ibn al-A'rabi narrated to us, Sa'id ibn Muslim narrated, from Ibn Awn who said: I heard al-Qasim ibn Muhammad say: O Allah, forgive my father his sin concerning Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him."
"5247","Ibn Abi Umar said: Abd al-Wahhab narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah who said: A man of Quraysh called Thumamah was over Sana'. When Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, was killed, he gave a sermon and wept severely. When he recovered he said: Today the caliphate of prophethood has been stripped from the ummah of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and it has become kingship and tyranny; whoever overcomes something devours it."
"5248","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Yazid, he is Ibn Harun, narrated to us, Abu al-Ashhab informed us, from al-Hasan that he heard him say: I saw those who spoke about Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, and disputed in the mosque, until I could hardly see the surface of the sky. A man from the chambers of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) pointed with a mushaf and said: Do you not know that Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is free of whoever leaves his religion and becomes sects: Indeed those who divided their religion and became sects, you are not of them in anything, to the end of the verse."
"5249","Ibn Ulayyah narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Ibn Abi Mulaykah, from Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr who said: I said to Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him: With you in the house is a victorious band; Allah Almighty aids with even less than it, so permit me to fight. He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I adjure by Allah any man who shed blood for me, or he said: shed blood concerning me."
"5250","Ishaq said: Abu Amir al-Aqadi narrated, from Muhammad, he is Ibn Talhah ibn Musarrif; Kinanah, the freedman of Safiyyah bint Huyayy, narrated to me that he witnessed the murder of Uthman, saying: I was then fourteen years old. He said: Safiyyah ordered us to saddle a mule with a howdah, so we saddled it, then we walked around it to the door. There was al-Ashtar with people with him. Al-Ashtar said to her: Return to your house, but she refused. He raised a spear with him, or a lance, and struck the mule's rump; the mule bolted and the howdah tilted until it almost fell. When she saw that she said: Take me back, take me back. He said: Four men of Quraysh came out of the house, beaten and carried, who had been defending Uthman. He mentioned al-Hasan ibn Ali, Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, Ibn Khatib and Marwan ibn al-Hakam. I said: Was Muhammad ibn Abi Bakr stained with any of his blood? He said: God forbid; he entered upon him, and Uthman said to him: You are not his man, and spoke to him with words, so he went out unstained with any of his blood. I said: Who killed him? He said: A man of the people of Egypt called Jabalah, who kept saying: I am the killer of Na'thal. I said: Where was Uthman then? He said: In the house."
"5251","Chapter: The innocence of Ali of the murder of Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him. Musaddad said: Abdullah narrated to us, from Rubayh, from Abu Musa, from Abdullah ibn Abi Sufyan who said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Banu Umayyah fight me, claiming I killed Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him. They have lied; they only want kingship. If I knew it would remove what is in their hearts, that I swear to them at the Maqam: By Allah, I did not kill Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, nor order his killing, I would do it. But they only want kingship. And I hope that I and Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, are among those of whom Allah Almighty said: And We removed whatever rancor was in their breasts, to the end of the verse."
"5252","Chapter: Fighting the people of rebellion. Ishaq said: Abdah ibn Sulayman informed us, Salim al-Muradi Abu al-Ala narrated, who said: I heard al-Hasan, may Allah be pleased with him, say: When Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, came to Basra concerning Talhah and his companions, may Allah be pleased with them, Abdullah ibn al-Kawwa' and Ibn Ubad stood and said: O Commander of the Believers, tell us about this march of yours: is it a bequest the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) bequeathed to you, a covenant he entrusted to you, or an opinion you formed when the ummah split and its word differed? He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I would not be the first to lie upon him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). By Allah, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) did not die suddenly, nor was he killed. He remained in his illness, and each time the caller came informing him of prayer, he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would say: Order Abu Bakr to lead the people in prayer. He left me while he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw my place, and had he entrusted anything to me I would have upheld it, until a woman of his wives (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) objected, saying: Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, is a soft man; when he stands in your place he will not make the people hear. If you ordered Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, to lead the people in prayer. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You are the companions of Yusuf. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) passed away, the Muslims looked into their affair, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) had put Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, over their religion, so they put him over their worldly affair. The Muslims pledged to him, and I pledged with them. I raided when he sent me raiding, took when he gave me, and was a whip before him, may Allah be pleased with him, in establishing penalties. Had there been favoritism at his death, may Allah be pleased with him, he would have placed it in his son; he disliked choosing a man from Quraysh to appoint over the ummah, lest any wrong after him attach to Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, in his grave. So he chose six of us, I among them, for us to choose a man for the ummah. When we gathered, Abd al-Rahman ibn Awf, may Allah be pleased with him, jumped and gave us his share of it, provided we give him covenants that he choose from the five a man to appoint over the ummah. We gave him our covenants. He took the hand of Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, and pledged to him. Something occurred in myself at that, but when I looked into my affair, my covenant had preceded my pledge, so I pledged and submitted. I raided when he sent me raiding, took when he gave me, and was a whip before him, may Allah be pleased with him, in establishing penalties. When Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, was killed, I looked into my affair, and the covenant upon my neck to Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, had been discharged, and the covenant to Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, I had fulfilled. I am a man of the Muslims with no claim against me from anyone, nor any demand. Then one unlike me jumped into it, meaning Mu'awiyah, may Allah be pleased with him; neither his kinship like my kinship, nor his knowledge like my knowledge, nor his precedence like my precedence, and I was more deserving of it than him. They said: You have spoken truthfully. Tell us about your fighting these two men, meaning Talhah and al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with them both, your two companions in the emigration, your two companions in the pledge of Ridwan, and your two companions in counsel. He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: They pledged to me in Madinah and opposed me in Basra. Had a man who pledged to Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, deposed him, we would have fought him, and had a man who pledged to Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, deposed him, we would have fought him."
"5253","Muhammad ibn Ubayd al-Tanafisi informed us, from Salim al-Muradi, from al-Hasan, may Allah be pleased with him, the like of it exactly. I said: Abu Dawud and al-Nasa'i narrated part of it from the hadith of al-Hasan, from Qays ibn Abbad."
"5254","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Abd al-Malik ibn Abd al-Aziz al-Nasa'i narrated to us, from Kawthar ibn Hakim, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do you know how the judgment of Allah, Mighty and Majestic, is concerning whoever rebels of this ummah? He said: We said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He said: Their wounded shall not be finished off, their captive shall not be killed, and their fleeing one shall not be pursued."
"5255","Al-Harith said: Abd al-Mun'im ibn Idris ibn Sinan narrated to us, from al-Kawthar ibn Hakim, then he mentioned it with the wording: He said to Ibn Umm Abd: Do you know, to its end, and added: And their booty shall not be divided. This is Allah's judgment concerning whoever rebels of this ummah, and in our view they are the Khawarij."
"5256","Abu Yala said: Isma'il ibn Musa narrated to us, al-Rabi' ibn Sahl narrated, Sa'id ibn Ubayd narrated to me, from Ali ibn Rabi'ah who said: I heard Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, say upon the pulpit, when a man came to him and said: O Commander of the Believers, why do I see you driving people as a man drives his camels? Is it by a covenant from the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) or something you opined? He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: By Allah, I have not lied, nor was I lied to, nor gone astray, nor been led astray; rather it is a covenant from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and whoever forges has failed."
"5257","And with it: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) covenanted to me to fight the oath-breakers, the unjust and the renegades. Al-Bazzar narrated it: Abbad ibn Ya'qub narrated to us, al-Rabi' narrated, and said: We do not know it from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, except with this chain."
"5258","Abu Yala said: al-Salt ibn Mas'ud al-Jahdari narrated to us, Ja'far ibn Sulayman narrated, al-Khalil ibn Murrah narrated, from al-Qasim ibn Sulayman, from his father, from his grandfather who said: I heard Ammar ibn Yasir, may Allah be pleased with him, say: I was commanded to kill the oath-breakers, the unjust and the renegades."
"5259","Chapter: The Battle of the Camel. Abu Bakr said: Waki' narrated to us, from Isam ibn Qudamah al-Bajali, from Ikrimah, from Ibn Abbas, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Which of you is the owner of the well-fed camel, around whom many slain will be killed; she will escape after nearly perishing."
"5260","Ishaq said: Abdullah ibn Idris informed us, who said: I heard Husayn narrating from Amr ibn Ja'wan, from al-Ahnaf ibn Qays who said: We set out as pilgrims and came to Madinah. While we were in our lodgings laying down our saddles, a comer came to us saying: The people have panicked and gathered in the mosque. We set out to the mosque, then he mentioned the hadith of Uthman's, may Allah be pleased with him, adjuring the Companions and their affirming, may Allah be pleased with them, his virtues. Al-Ahnaf ibn Qays said: I met Talhah and al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with them both, and said: I see this one as surely killed; whom do you command me to pledge? They said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him. I said: Do you command me that and approve it for me? They said: Yes. I went out until I came to Makkah. While thus, it was said: Uthman ibn Affan, may Allah be pleased with him, has been killed, and A'ishah, Mother of the Believers, may Allah be pleased with her, was there. I came to her and said to her: I adjure you by Allah, whom do you command me to pledge? She, may Allah be pleased with her, said: Ali. I said: Do you command me that and approve it for me? She said: Yes. He said: I returned and came to Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, in Madinah and pledged to him, then returned to my family in Basra, seeing the affair as settled. While thus, a comer came to us saying: Here is A'ishah, Mother of the Believers, with Talhah and al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with them, alighted at the side of al-Khuraybah. I said: What brought them? They said: They sent to you seeking aid for the blood of Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, killed wrongfully. There came to me the most dreadful matter ever to come to me. I said: My forsaking a people with them the Mother of the Believers and the disciple of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) is severe, and my fighting a man, the cousin of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), whom you commanded me to pledge, by Allah I shall not fight you while the Mother of the Believers is with you, nor fight the cousin of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). Choose from me one of three: either open for me the bridge gate so I join such-and-such land, meaning the land of the non-Arabs, until Allah decrees in His affair what He decrees, or I join Makkah, or I withdraw to be near you, neither with you nor against you. They said: We shall consult, then send to you. He said: They consulted and said: As for opening the bridge gate for him to join the land of the non-Arabs, the dissenter and deserter will come to him; and as for joining Makkah, he will stir Quraysh against you and inform them of your news; that is no good for you. But make him near here, where you tread upon his ear. So he withdrew at al-Jalha', two farsakhs from Basra, and with him withdrew some six thousand people. Then the people met, and the first slain was Talhah ibn Ubaydullah, may Allah be pleased with him. He said: Ka'b ibn Sur used to recite the mushaf, reminding these and those, until Ka'b was killed, and those killed of them were killed. Al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with him, reached Safawan of Basra, at the distance of al-Qadisiyyah from you. He said: al-Na'r, a man of Banu Mujashi', met him and said: Where are you going, O disciple of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? Come to me, you are under my protection, none shall reach you. He came with him. He said: A man came to al-Ahnaf ibn Qays, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: Here is al-Zubayr arrived at Safawan. He said: What security has he? He gathered the Muslims until some struck the eyebrows of others with the sword, then joined his sons and family. He said: Uwaymir ibn Jurmuz, Fadalab ibn Habis and Nufay' heard him, so they rode seeking him and met him with al-Na'r."
"5261","And al-Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman informed us, who said: I heard my father narrating from Husayn: Umar ibn Ja'wan, a man of Banu Tamim, narrated, and that was when I said to him: What was the withdrawal of al-Ahnaf ibn Qays, may Allah be pleased with him? He said: I heard al-Ahnaf ibn Qays, may Allah be pleased with him, say: I came to Madinah as a pilgrim, then he mentioned the hadith like what preceded. He said: Some misguided people heard it, among them Ibn Jurmuz, Fadalab and Nufay', so they set out seeking him and met him coming with al-Na'r. Umayr ibn Jurmuz came to him from behind and stabbed him a weak stab while he was upon a weak horse of his. Al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with him, charged at him upon a horse of his called Dhu al-Khimar. When Ibn Jurmuz thought al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with him, would kill him, he called Fadalab and Nufay', and they charged upon al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with him, and killed him."
"5262","Al-Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman informed us, a man of the freedmen of Banu Tamim called Abu Sufyan narrated to me: Atikah, the wife of Umar, said: May it be paralyzed."
"5263","Yahya ibn Adam informed us, Abu Isra'il informed me, from al-Hakam who said: On the day of the Camel, when they lined up, Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, called al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with him, and he came to him. He said: I adjure you by Allah Almighty, do you not remember that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You will surely fight him while wronging him? He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: O Allah, yes, but I did not remember it before this stand of mine. So he went back. When his companion saw him he followed him, meaning Talhah, may Allah be pleased with him, but Marwan shot him with an arrow and fastened his thigh to the iron of the saddle."
"5264","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Abu Ahmad narrated to us, Abu Isra'il narrated, with it."
"5265","Ishaq said: Ya'la ibn Ubayd informed us, Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid narrated, from Abd al-Salam, a man of Hayyah, who said: Ali was alone with al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with them both, on the day of the Camel and said: I adjure you by Allah Almighty, how did you hear the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say, while you were twisting my hand in the portico of Banu so-and-so: You will surely fight him while wronging him, then he will surely be aided against you? He said: I heard; no doubt, I shall not fight you."
"5266","Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Shu'bah, Abu Awn narrated to me, from Abu al-Duha who said: Sulayman ibn Surad said to al-Hasan ibn Ali, may Allah be pleased with them both: Excuse me before the Commander of the Believers. Al-Hasan said: I saw him on the day of the Camel clinging to me, saying: I wish I had died so-and-so years before this. Shu'bah said: I met Mansur and he said: That one-eyed one does not know, meaning Abu Awn."
"5267","Al-Harith said: Qurad Abu Nuh narrated to us, from Shu'bah, from Muhammad ibn Ubaydullah Abu Awn al-Thaqafi, from Abu al-Duha, from Sulayman ibn Surad who said: I came to al-Hasan, may Allah be pleased with him, and said: Excuse me before the Commander of the Believers for not attending the battle. Al-Hasan, may Allah be pleased with him, said: What will you do with this? I saw him clinging to me, saying: O Hasan, would that I had died twenty years before this."
"5268","Abu Yala said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Salih narrated to us, Abu Bakr ibn Ayyash narrated, from Sadaqah ibn Sa'id, from Jumay' ibn Umayr who said: His mother and maternal aunt entered upon A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, then he mentioned the hadith. They said: Tell us about Ali, may Allah be pleased with him? She said: What are you asking me about a man who placed his hand at the place of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) from which his soul flowed into his hand, and he wiped his face with it? They said: Why did you go out against him? She, may Allah be pleased with her, said: A decreed matter; I wish I could ransom it with all upon earth."
"5269","Abu Bakr said: al-Fadl ibn Dukayn narrated to us, from Abd al-Jabbar ibn al-Abbas, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from Umar ibn al-Hajanna', from Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: It was said to him: What prevented you from fighting with your aid on the day of the Camel? He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: There will emerge a perished people who will not prosper, their leader a woman, their leader in Paradise."
"5270","Al-Bazzar said: Muhammad ibn Umar and Ahmad ibn Mansur narrated to us, saying: Abu Nu'aym al-Fadl narrated with it, and said: Abd al-Jabbar is alone in it. Another said: From Ata, from Bilal ibn Buqtur, from Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him."
"5271","Abu Bakr said: Yazid narrated to us, Sharik narrated, from al-Aswad ibn Qays; one who saw al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with him, making the horses prance, meaning on the day of the Camel, narrated to me. Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, called out to him: O Abu Abdullah! He came until the necks of their mounts met. Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said to him: I adjure you by Allah, Mighty and Majestic, do you remember a day the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) came to us while I was confiding with you, and he said: Are you confiding with him? By Allah, he will surely fight you while wronging you. He said: Al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with him, struck his mount's face and turned away."
"5272","Abu Yala said: Ya'qub ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Abu Asim narrated, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Abd al-Malik ibn Muslim, from his grandfather, from Abu Jarw al-Mazini who said: I witnessed Ali and al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with them both, when they met. Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said to him: O Zubayr, I adjure you, did you hear the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: You will fight Ali while wronging him? He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Yes, and I did not recall that except in this stand of mine. Then he turned away, may Allah be pleased with him."
"5273","Chapter: The murder of Ammar, may Allah be pleased with him, at Siffin, and his saying (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): The rebellious party will kill Ammar. Musaddad said: Abd al-Warith narrated to us, from Abu al-Tayyah who said: Ibn Abi al-Hudhayl narrated to me, saying: Ammar ibn Yasir, may Allah be pleased with him, was a strong man, carrying two stones at a time. That reached the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), so the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) met him and pushed his chest. He stood shaking dust from his head, saying: Woe to you, O Ibn Sumayyah; the rebellious party will kill you."
"5274","Al-Harith said: al-Abbas ibn al-Fadl narrated to us, Abd al-Warith narrated with this."
"5275","Abu Yala said: Ja'far ibn Mihran narrated to us, Abd al-Warith narrated, and he mentioned it in a hadith."
"5276","Al-Harith said: Ubaydullah ibn Muhammad narrated to us, Hammad narrated, from Abu al-Tayyah, from Abdullah ibn Abi al-Hudhayl, from Ammar ibn Yasir, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The rebellious party will kill you."
"5277","Abu Yala said: Abu Sa'id narrated to us, Ali ibn Mushir narrated, from Muslim, from Habbah who said: Ibn Mas'ud said to Hudhayfah, may Allah be pleased with them both: Fitnah has occurred, so narrate to me what you heard the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say. He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I heard him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say to Ibn Sumayyah, may Allah be pleased with him: Woe to Ibn Sumayyah; the rebellious party will kill him."
"5278","Musaddad said: Mu'tamir ibn Sulayman narrated to us, from Layth ibn Abi Sulaym, from Mujahid, from Abdullah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them both, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: The rebellious party will kill Ammar, may Allah be pleased with him."
"5279","And with it to Abdullah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: Two men disputing over the blood of Ammar and his spoils came to Amr ibn al-As, may Allah be pleased with him. Amr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Leave him alone; I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Quraysh has become obsessed with Ammar; the killer of Ammar and his despoiler, may Allah be pleased with him, are in the Fire."
"5280","Abu Bakr said: Abu Mu'awiyah narrated to us, from al-A'mash, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Ziyad, from Abdullah ibn al-Harith, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I was traveling with Mu'awiyah, may Allah be pleased with him, on his return from Siffin, between him and Amr ibn al-As, may Allah be pleased with him, when Abdullah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: O father, did you not hear the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Woe to you, O Ibn Sumayyah; the rebellious party will kill you."
"5281","Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, from al-Awwam ibn Hawshab who said: al-Aswad ibn Mas'ud narrated to me, from Hanzalah ibn Khuwaylid who said: I was sitting with Mu'awiyah, may Allah be pleased with him, when two men entered disputing over the head of Ammar, may Allah be pleased with him, each saying: I killed him. Abdullah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Let one of you make the other content with it, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The rebellious party will kill him. Mu'awiyah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Will you not rest us of your madman, O Amr? What is he doing with us? He said: I am with you but I do not fight; my father complained of me, and the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Obey your father so long as he lives, and do not disobey him. So I am with you but I do not fight."
"5282","Waki' narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Yahya ibn Hani', from a man, from Abdullah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: I did not stab with a spear, strike with a sword, nor shoot an arrow. He said: It was said to him: Why? He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Obey your father, so I obeyed him."
"5283","Abu Yala said: Amr ibn Malik narrated to us, Yusuf ibn Atiyyah narrated, Kulthum ibn Jabr narrated, who said: I heard Abu al-Ghadiyah al-Juhani say: I charged upon Ammar ibn Yasir, may Allah be pleased with him, on the day of Siffin and pushed him off his horse. A man of the people of Sham beat me to him and cut off his head. We disputed to Mu'awiyah, may Allah be pleased with him, over the head, placing it before him, each claiming his killing, each seeking the prize for his head. With him was Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As, may Allah be pleased with them. Abdullah ibn Amr ibn al-As, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say to Ammar, may Allah be pleased with him: The rebellious party will kill you; give glad tidings of the Fire to the killer of Ammar. So I let go of it, saying: I did not kill him, and my companion let go, saying: I did not kill him. When Mu'awiyah, may Allah be pleased with him, saw that, he turned to Abdullah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: What prompts you to this? He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say a word, and I loved to say it."
"5284","Abu Khaythamah narrated to us, Jarir narrated, from al-A'mash, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Ziyad, from Abdullah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: On the day of Siffin, when they turned away, Abdullah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The rebellious party will kill Ammar. Amr said to Mu'awiyah, may Allah be pleased with them both: Do you not hear what your nephew says? He said: I seek refuge with Allah from doubt; are you in doubt? Did we kill him? Only whoever brought him killed him."
"5285","Isma'il ibn Musa narrated to us, Asbat narrated, from al-A'mash, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Ziyad, from Abdullah ibn al-Harith ibn Nawfal who said: I returned with Mu'awiyah, may Allah be pleased with him, from Siffin. Mu'awiyah and Abu al-A'war al-Sulami were traveling on one side, and Amr and his son on another side. I was between them, none other. Sometimes I hastened to these, sometimes to those. I heard Abdullah ibn Amr, may Allah be pleased with them both, say to his father: O father, did you not hear the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say to Ammar, may Allah be pleased with him, while building the mosque: You are keen upon reward? He said: Yes, may Allah be pleased with him. And he (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You are of the people of Paradise, and the rebellious party will kill you. He said: Yes, I heard it. He said: Then why did you kill him? He said: He turned to Mu'awiyah, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, do you not hear what this one says? He said: He mentioned it. He said: Woe to you, you keep slipping in your speech; did we kill him? Only whoever brought him killed him."
"5286","Abu Bakr said: Waki' narrated to us, from Sufyan, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit, from Abu al-Bakhtari who said: On the day of Siffin, when the heat intensified, Ammar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Bring me a drink to drink. Then he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The last drink you drink of this world is a drink of milk. Then he advanced and was killed."
"5287","Abu Yala said: al-Qawariri narrated to us, Ibn Mahdi narrated, from Sufyan, the like of it."
"5288","Abu Yala said: al-Fadl ibn Sukayn narrated to us, Ahmad ibn Muhammad al-Ramli narrated, Yahya ibn Isa al-Ramli narrated, al-A'mash narrated, from Zayd ibn Wahb who said: Ammar ibn Yasir, may Allah be pleased with him, was fond of Quraysh and they fond of him. They came upon him in the morning and beat him. Uthman, may Allah be pleased with him, came out angry, ascended the pulpit, praised Allah and extolled Him, then said: O people, what is with me and Quraysh? May Allah do to Quraysh and do. They came upon a man in the morning and beat him. I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say to Ammar, may Allah be pleased with him: The rebellious party will kill you."
"5289","Wahb ibn Baqiyyah narrated to us, Khalid narrated, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from Maysarah and Abu al-Bakhtari who said: Ammar, may Allah be pleased with him, on the day of Siffin kept fighting without being killed. He would come to Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: O Commander of the Believers, is this day not such-and-such? He would say: Go away from you. He said that repeatedly. Then milk was brought to him, may Allah be pleased with him, and he drank it. Ammar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: This is the last drink I drink in this world. Then he advanced and fought until he was killed, may Allah be pleased with him."
"5290","Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, saying: I heard Jarir say: I heard an elder narrating to Mughirah, from the daughter of Hisham ibn al-Walid ibn al-Mughirah, who used to nurse Ammar, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Mu'awiyah came to Ammar ibn Yasir, may Allah be pleased with them both, visiting him in illness. When he left him he said: O Allah, do not make his death at our hands, for I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The rebellious party will kill Ammar."
"5291","Al-Harith said: Yazid ibn Harun narrated to us, Abd al-Malik ibn Qudamah informed me, Umar ibn Shu'ayb, the brother of Amr ibn Shu'ayb, in Sham, informed me, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: Umm Abdullah bint Munabbih ibn al-Hajjaj used to be kind to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He came to her one day and said: How are you, O Umm Abdullah? She said: Well. How are you, may my father and mother be ransom for you, O Messenger of Allah? He said: Well. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: And how is Abdullah? She said: Well; and Abdullah is a man who has abandoned this world. His father said to him on the day of Siffin: Go out and fight. He said: O father, how do you command me to fight when there was in the covenant of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) what you heard? He said: I adjure you by Allah, do you know that the last thing in the covenant of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was that he took your hand and placed it in my hand, saying: Obey Amr ibn al-As? He said: Yes. He said: I command you to fight. So he went out and fought. When war broke out, Amr, may Allah be pleased with him, began to say: Ath-thabaj, and Amr, may Allah be pleased with him, also said: The forelocks have turned; pledge."
"5292","Abu Yala said: Salih ibn Hatim ibn Wardan narrated to us, my father narrated, Ali ibn Zayd narrated to me, a man of Banu Sa'd narrated, who said: I was standing at Siffin beside al-Ahnaf, may Allah be pleased with him, and al-Ahnaf beside Ammar, may Allah be pleased with him. I heard Ammar, may Allah be pleased with him, say: My close friend (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) covenanted to me that my last provision of this world is a sip of milk. He said: While thus, dust rose and they said: The people of Sham have come, and the water-bearers stood giving people drink. A slave-girl came with a cup of milk and handed it to Ammar, may Allah be pleased with him, and he drank it. Then Ammar, may Allah be pleased with him, handed his remainder to al-Ahnaf ibn Qays, may Allah be pleased with him, then al-Ahnaf handed to me. I said: If your companion is truthful, he is likely to be killed now. He said: The people overwhelmed us, and Ammar, may Allah be pleased with him, advanced and I heard him say; then that was the last acquaintance with him, may Allah be pleased with him."
"5293","Chapter. Al-Harith said: Sa'id ibn Amir narrated to us, Hisham ibn Hassan narrated, who said: A group of the companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) gathered, among them Ibn Mas'ud, Hudhayfah, Sa'd, Ibn Umar and Ammar, may Allah be pleased with them. Hudhayfah, may Allah be pleased with him, mentioned a fitnah, saying: As for me, if I reach it I shall know the way out of it. Ibn Mas'ud, may Allah be pleased with him, said: And I, if I reach it, shall know the way out of it. Sa'd, may Allah be pleased with him, said: As for me, if I reach it and find a sword saying: This is a believer, leave him, and this is a disbeliever, kill him, I shall fight, otherwise I shall not fight. Ibn Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both, said: And I am with you. Ammar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: As for me, if I reach it I shall take my sword and place it upon my shoulder, then head toward its greatest crowd and strike until it disperses. This is disconnected."
"5294","Ishaq said: Yahya ibn Adam informed us, Sharik narrated, from Muhammad ibn Abdullah al-Muradi, from Amr ibn Murrah, from Abdullah ibn Salamah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: There was a poet on the night of Siffin reciting lampoon of Mu'awiyah and Amr ibn al-As, while Ammar ibn Yasir, may Allah be pleased with him, was saying: Stick to the two debauched ones. A man said: This while you are the companions of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)? Ammar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: If you wish to sit, sit, and if you wish to go, go."
"5295","Chapter: The indication to pardon whoever fought among the Companions, may Allah be pleased with them, in these battles. Abu Yala said: Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Abu Dawud al-Hafari narrated, Ibn Abi Za'idah narrated, from Sa'd ibn Tariq, from Abu Hazim, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: This ummah is a merciful ummah; there is no punishment upon it except what it inflicts upon itself. She said: I said: And how does it punish itself? He said: Was not the day of the Camel a punishment? Was not the day of Siffin a punishment? Was not the day of al-Nahr a punishment?"
"5296","Wahb ibn Baqiyyah narrated to us, Khalid narrated, from Yunus, from Humayd ibn Hilal, from Abu Burdah, from a man of the Emigrants, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The punishment of this ummah is by the sword."
"5297","Ishaq said: Abd al-Razzaq informed us, from Ma'mar, from al-Zuhri, who said: Khuzaymah ibn Thabit, may Allah be pleased with him, who is called Dhu al-Shahadatayn, fought on the day of Siffin with Ali, may Allah be pleased with him."
"5298","Chapter. Ibn Abi Shaybah said: Muhammad ibn Bishr narrated to us, Mujalid narrated, from Abu al-Waddak, from Abu Sa'id, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: When you see so-and-so orating upon my pulpit, kill him."
"5299","Chapter: Reports of the Khawarij. The hadith of Ali and Ammar, may Allah be pleased with them both, in the chapter of fighting the people of rebellion has preceded. Abu Yala said: Muhammad ibn Bakkar narrated to us, Abu Ma'shar narrated, Aflah ibn Abdullah ibn al-Mughirah narrated, from al-Zuhri, from Ubaydullah ibn Abdullah ibn Utbah, from Abu Sa'id al-Khudri, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was present on the day of Hunayn distributing booty, the hadith, concerning the man's saying: This is a division by which Allah's Face was not intended. He said: I was present after that with Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, when he killed them at al-Nahrawan. Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, sought, meaning al-Mukhdaj. He said: He did not find him, then found him after that under a wall upon this description. Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Which of you knows this man? A man of the people said: We know him; this is Hurqus, and his mother is here. He said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, sent to his mother, saying to her: From whom is he? She said: I do not know, O Commander of the Believers, except that in Jahiliyyah I was herding sheep of mine at al-Rabadhah, and something like darkness covered me, so I conceived from it and gave birth to him."
"5300","Al-Humaydi said: Abd al-Malik ibn Ibrahim narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Muslim narrated, Abu Kathir narrated to me, who said: I was with my master, meaning Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, when he killed the people of al-Nahrawan, and the people seemed to feel distress in themselves about his killing. Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: O people, the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) narrated to me: People will exit from religion as the arrow exits from the game, then never return to it. The sign of that is that among them is a black man with a defective arm, one of his hands like a woman's breast, with a nipple like a woman's nipple. He said: And I think he said: Around it seven hairs. Seek him, for I see him only among them. They found him at the river edge under the slain. He said: Allah and His Messenger spoke truthfully. Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, was wearing an Arab bow of his, prodding his outlet with it. He said: The people rejoiced when they saw him and were glad, and what they had felt left them."
"5301","Ibn Abi Umar said: Abd al-Rahman ibn Khalid narrated, from Isma'il ibn Muslim; Abu Kathir, the freedman of the Ansar, narrated to me, who said: I was with my master Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, and he mentioned it."
"5302","Abu Yala said: Ubaydullah ibn Umar al-Qawariri narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn al-Uryan al-Harithi narrated, al-Azraq ibn Qays narrated, from a man of Abd al-Qays who said: I witnessed the day the people of al-Nahrawan were killed, then he mentioned the hadith, and in it: Had a man's soul exited from joy, the soul of Ali ibn Abi Talib, may Allah be pleased with him, would have exited that day. He said: Allah and His Messenger spoke truthfully; whoever narrates to me that he saw him among the people before this death-spot of his, I am a liar."
"5303","Chapter: The merit of whoever killed the Haruriyyah. Ishaq said: al-Mughirah ibn Salamah al-Makhzumi informed us, Abd al-Wahid ibn Ziyad narrated, from Asim ibn Kulayb, who said: My father narrated to me, saying: People's gatherings were the mosques, until they returned from Siffin and disavowed the arbitration. People took matters lightly and sat in the streets seeking news. While we were sitting with Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, a man stood saying: Permit me to speak, but he was distracted with what he was engaged in. We said to him: What did you want to ask the Commander of the Believers about? He said: I was in Umrah, and I entered upon A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, and she said: Who are these who fell before you, called Harura? She said: Did you witness their destruction? Ali ibn Abi Talib, if he wished, could narrate to you their hadith. When Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, finished what he was engaged in, he said: Where is the man? He related to him, and Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said the tahlil and takbir, then said: I entered upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) with none but A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her, present. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: How will you be, O son of Abu Talib, with such-and-such people? I said: Allah and His Messenger know best. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A people exiting from the east, reciting the Qur'an not passing their throats, passing through religion as the arrow passes through the game; among them a man with a defective arm, as if his hand is the breast of an Abyssinian woman. He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I adjure you by Allah, I told you that among them, and you said: Not among them, then you brought him to me dragging him? They said: O Allah, yes. He, may Allah be pleased with him, said the tahlil and takbir."
"5304","Abu Bakr said: Muhammad ibn Fudayl ibn Ghazwan narrated to us, from Asim ibn Kulayb, from his father who said: I was sitting with Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, while he was engaged in some affair of the people, when a man in travel clothes came saying: O Commander of the Believers. Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, was distracted with what he was engaged in of people's affairs. My father said: I said to him: What is your affair? He said: I was performing Hajj, or Umrah, and I passed by A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her. She said to me, asking me about those people who went out among you, called al-Haruriyyah. I said: They went out in a place called Harura', so they were named al-Haruriyyah for it. She, may Allah be pleased with her, said: Blessed is whoever witnessed their destruction. She said: By Allah, had Ibn Abi Talib wished, he could have informed you of their account. From there I came to ask him about that. He said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, finished and said: Where is the questioner? He stood to him and related to him. Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said the tahlil and takbir twice or thrice, and mentioned the like of it. He said at its end: Dragging him as I described to you. He said: Then he, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Allah and His Messenger spoke truthfully, three times. I said: The origin of the Mukhdaj story is in the Sahih and others, but they did not bring it with this context, nor from the hadith of A'ishah, may Allah be pleased with her."
"5305","Abu Yala narrated it from Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah with it."
"5306","And from Abu Hisham, from Ibn Fudayl, the like of it."
"5307","Al-Bazzar narrated it the like of it."
"5308","Ishaq said: Yahya ibn Adam informed us, Yazid ibn Abd al-Aziz ibn Siyah narrated, from his father, from Habib ibn Abi Thabit who said: I came to Abu Wa'il while he was in his quarter's mosque, and we withdrew to a corner of the mosque. I said: Will you not inform me about those people whom Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, killed: why did they leave him, why did they respond to him when he called them, and when they left him, why did he deem fighting them lawful? He said: When we were at Siffin, killing intensified among the people of Sham, then he mentioned a story. He said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, returned to Kufah, and the Khawarij said about him what they said, and alighted at Harura', some ten-odd thousand. Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, sent to them, adjuring them by Allah Almighty: Return to your caliph; for what do you blame him, in distribution or judgment? They said: We fear entering fitnah. He said: Do not hasten this year's misguidance fearing next year's fitnah. They returned, saying: He shall be on our side; if he accepts the arbitration we shall fight him as we fought the people of Sham at Siffin for it, and if he breaks it we shall fight with him. They marched until they crossed Nahrawan, and a party of them split off killing people. Their companions said: Not upon this did we leave Ali. When their deed reached Ali, may Allah be pleased with them, he stood saying: Do you march to your enemy or return to these who stayed behind in your lands? They said: Rather we return to them. He said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A party will exit from the east at a dissension of people, deeming your striving with your striving nothing, your prayer with their prayer nothing, and your fasting with their fasting nothing, passing through religion as the arrow passes through the game; their mark a man whose upper arm is like a woman's breast; the nearer of the two parties to truth will kill them. Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, marched to them and they fought him fiercely. Ali's cavalry, may Allah be pleased with him, began to stand against them. He said: O people, if you fight them only for me, by Allah I have nothing to inform you with, and if you fight for Allah Almighty, this shall not be your fighting. They turned upon them and killed them all. He said: Seek him. They sought but did not find. Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, mounted his animal and came to a hollow of land with slain, some upon others. He was extracted from beneath them and dragged by his leg while people watched. Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I shall not raid this year. He returned to Kufah and was killed. The people made al-Hasan ibn Ali, may Allah be pleased with them both, successor. Al-Hasan sent the pledge to Mu'awiyah, may Allah be pleased with him, and al-Hasan wrote that to Qays ibn Sa'd, may Allah be pleased with them both. Qays ibn Sa'd stood among his companions saying: O people, two matters have come to you, you must have one of them: entering fitnah, or killing without an imam. The people said: What is this? He said: al-Hasan ibn Ali has given the pledge to Mu'awiyah. The people returned and pledged to Mu'awiyah, may Allah be pleased with him. Mu'awiyah had no concern but those at al-Nahrawan; they kept falling upon him pledging to him, until three hundred and odd remained of them, the companions of al-Nukhaylah. I said: This chain is authentic."
"5309","Abu Bakr said: Abdullah ibn Numayr narrated to us, Abd al-Aziz ibn Siyah narrated, with it."
"5310","Abu Yala said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us with it. The origin of the raised report is in Sahih Muslim and others; I cited this only because it contains additions over the paths brought by the authors of the books, and Ahmad also."
"5311","Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from al-Taymi, from Abu Mijlaz, I think from Qays ibn Abbad who said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, refrained from fighting the people of al-Nahr until they innovated. They set out and came upon Abdullah ibn Khabbab, in a village of his, withdrawn from fitnah. They seized him. He said: They saw a date fallen from a palm top; a man of them took it and put it in his mouth. They said: A date of the covenanted people's dates, taken without price. He said: So he spat it out. He said: They came upon a pig, and one of them ripped it with his sword and killed it. They said: A pig of the covenanted people's pigs, you killed it. Abdullah ibn Khabbab, may Allah be pleased with him, said to them: Shall I not inform you of one greater in right over you than this date and this pig? They said: Who? He said: I. I think he said: I have not left a prayer since puberty, nor fasting Ramadan, and he counted things. They brought him near and killed him. That reached Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, so he ordered his companions to march to them. He said: Give us retaliation for Abdullah ibn Khabbab. They said: How shall we give you retaliation for him when all of us killed him? He said: Allah is Greatest. He said to his companions: Fall upon them; by Allah, not ten of you will be killed, nor ten of them flee. So it was. Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Seek a man of such-and-such description. They sought but did not find, then sought and found him. Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Who knows this one? He was not known. A man said: I saw this one at al-Najaf. He said: I want this garrison town, and in it is none of lineage nor acquaintance. Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: You spoke truthfully; he is a man of the jinn. The hadith of Jabir, may Allah be pleased with him, in the chapter of warning against the Khawarij creed from the book of decree has preceded."
"5312","Abu Bakr said: Zayd ibn al-Hubab narrated to us, Musa ibn Ubaydah narrated, Hud ibn Ata al-Yamami narrated, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Among us was a youth of worship, asceticism and striving. He said: We named him to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) but he did not know him, and we described him by his description but he did not know him. While thus, he came. We said to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Here he is. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: I see upon his face a touch of Satan. He came and greeted. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Have you made in yourself that none among the people is better than you? He said: O Allah, yes. Then he turned and entered the mosque. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Who will kill the man? Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I. He entered and there he was standing praying. He said: Shall I kill a man praying, when the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) forbade us striking those who pray. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Who will kill the man? Umar, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I, O Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). He entered the mosque and there he was prostrating. He said the like of Abu Bakr, may Allah be pleased with them both, adding: I shall return, for one better than me has returned. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Quiet, O Umar, and he mentioned to him. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Who will kill the man? Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: You will kill him if you find him. He entered the mosque and found he had gone out. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: By Allah, had you killed him, he would have been their first and last, and no two in my ummah would have differed."
"5313","Abu Yala said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us with it."
"5314","And Amr ibn al-Dahhak ibn Makhlad narrated to us, my father narrated, Musa narrated with it."
"5315","And Muhammad ibn al-Faraj Abu Ja'far in Baghdad narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Zibriqan narrated, Musa ibn Ubaydah narrated to me with it."
"5316","Abu Khaythamah narrated to us, Umar ibn Yunus narrated, Ikrimah, he is Ibn Ammar, narrated, from Yazid al-Raqashi; Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to me, who said: There was a man in the time of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) raiding with us; when he returned and unloaded his mount, he headed to the mosque and prayed in it at length, until some companions of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) deemed him superior to them. One day he passed while the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) was sitting among his companions. One of his companions said to him (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): There he is; either he sent to him or he came of himself. When the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw him coming he said: By the One in whose Hand is my soul, between his eyes is a touch of Satan. When he stood at the gathering, the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to him: Did you say in yourself when you stood: None among the people is better than me? He said: Yes. Then he left. He came to a side of the mosque, drew a line with his foot, aligned his heels, then stood praying, then he mentioned the hadith. It has preceded in the book of decree from the hadith of Zayd ibn Aslam from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him."
"5317","Al-Bazzar said: Ibrahim ibn Abdullah ibn Muhammad al-Kufi narrated to us, Abd al-Rahman ibn Sharik narrated, my father narrated, from al-A'mash, from Abu Sufyan, from Anas ibn Malik who said: We were with the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) when a man of good silence came, and he mentioned the like of it. He said: We do not know it narrated from Anas except from this angle. I said: I knew it has another angle besides that."
"5318","Chapter: The murder of Ali, may Allah be pleased with him. Al-Humaydi said: Sufyan narrated to us, Abd al-Malik ibn A'yan narrated, who said: I heard Abu Harb ibn Abi al-Aswad narrating from his father, who said: I heard Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, say: Abdullah ibn Salam, may Allah be pleased with him, came to me with my foot in the stirrup. He said to me: Where are you heading? I said: Iraq. He said: If you go to it, the edge of the sword will surely strike you there. Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: By Allah, I had heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say it before him. I heard my father say: I marveled at him, saying: A warrior narrating about himself such as this."
"5319","Ibn Abi Umar said: Sufyan narrated to us with this."
"5320","Abu Yala said: Ishaq ibn Abi Isra'il narrated to us."
"5321","Al-Bazzar said: Ahmad ibn Aban narrated to us, both saying: Sufyan informed us with it, and said: We know none narrating it but Abd al-Malik from Abu Harb, nor from him but Ibn Uyaynah. Ibn Hibban and al-Hakim authenticated it."
"5322","Abd said: Muhammad ibn Bishr narrated to us, Ibn Abi al-Zinad narrated, from Zayd ibn Aslam, from Abu Sinan al-Du'ali Yazid ibn Umayyah who said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, fell ill with an illness we feared for him, then he recovered and was well. We said: Praise be to Allah who healed you, O Commander of the Believers; we had feared for you from this illness of yours. He, may Allah be pleased with him, said: But I did not fear for myself. The truthful, believed one narrated to me, saying: You will not die until this of yours is struck, and the most wretched kills you, as the she-camel of Allah Almighty was hamstrung by the most wretched of Banu so-and-so, specifying him to his lower thigh besides Thamud."
"5323","Abu Yala said: Suwayd ibn Sa'id narrated to us, Rishdin ibn Sa'd narrated, from Yazid ibn Abdullah ibn al-Had, from Uthman ibn Suhayb, from his father, that Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to me: Who is the most wretched of the former? I said: The hamstringer of the she-camel. He said: You spoke truthfully. Who is the most wretched of the latter? I said: I have no knowledge. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The one who strikes you upon this, pointing to his crown. He used to say: Would that the most wretched of you had risen and dyed this from this, meaning his beard from his head's blood."
"5324","Isma'il ibn Musa narrated to us, Sharik narrated, from Ammar, from Abu Salih, from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in my dream and complained to him of my ummah's disbelief and harm. I wept, and the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Do not weep, O Ali. He turned, and I turned, and there were two men ascending, with boulders crushing their heads until exposed, then returning. I went early to Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, as I used to go early to him every day, until in al-Harariq I met people who said: He has been killed."
"5325","Al-Humaydi said: Sufyan narrated to us, from Ibrahim ibn Maysarah, from one who informed him, from al-Hasan or al-Husayn, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said: My Beloved (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), meaning in a dream, the Prophet of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), met me. He said: I complained to him of the people of Iraq after him, and he promised me rest from them. He, may Allah be pleased with him, remained only three days."
"5326","Abu Yala said: Ibrahim ibn al-Hajjaj al-Sami narrated to us, Sukayn ibn Abd al-Aziz narrated, Ja'far ibn Muhammad narrated, from his father, from his grandfather, who said: When Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, was killed, al-Hasan ibn Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, stood as orator, praised Allah Almighty and extolled Him, then said: As for what follows: By Allah, you killed tonight a man on a night in which the Qur'an descended, in which Isa son of Maryam was raised, and in which Yusha' ibn Nun, the youth of Musa, upon them be peace, was killed."
"5327","Ibrahim narrated to us, Sukayn narrated, my father narrated to me, from Khalid ibn Jabir, from his father, from al-Hasan, may Allah be pleased with him, the like of it, adding: And in it repentance was accepted for Banu Isra'il. And adding: None before him preceded him, nor will any after him catch him. The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) would send him in expeditions with Jibril, upon him be peace, on his right and Mika'il, upon him be peace, on his left. By Allah, he, may Allah be pleased with him, left neither yellow nor white except eight hundred or seven hundred dirhams, set aside to buy a servant."
"5328","Al-Bazzar said: Amr ibn Ali narrated to us, Abu Asim narrated, Sukayn narrated, with it, and he mentioned it, saying: We know none narrating this but al-Hasan, nor narrating it from Ja'far but Sukayn."
"5329","Abu Bakr and al-Harith said: al-Hasan ibn Musa narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Rashid narrated, from Abdullah ibn Muhammad ibn Aqil, from Fadlalah who said: I went out with my father to Yanbu' visiting Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, who was ill there. My father said to him: What keeps you in this dwelling? Had you perished in it, none but the bedouins of Juhaynah would have taken charge of you. Move to Madinah; if your term befalls you, your companions will take charge of you and pray over you. Abu Fadlalah, may Allah be pleased with him, was of the people of Badr. Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, said to him: I shall not die of this pain of mine. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) covenanted to me that I not die until I am appointed, then this, meaning his beard, is dyed from the blood of this, meaning his crown. Abu Fadlalah, may Allah be pleased with him, was killed at Siffin. Al-Bazzar narrated it from Muhammad ibn Abd al-Rahim, from al-Hasan, may Allah be pleased with him, saying: We know Fadlalah, may Allah be pleased with him, narrating from Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, but this."
"5330","Chapter: The murder of al-Husayn ibn Ali, may Allah be pleased with them both. Ishaq said: al-Mughirah ibn Salamah al-Makhzumi informed us, Mahdi ibn Maymun narrated, from Wasil, the freedman of Abu Uyaynah, from Yahya ibn Aqil, from Abu Yahya, from a man of Banu Dabbah who said: I witnessed Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, when he alighted at Karbala'. He went and stood aside, gesturing with his hand, saying: Their mounts' resting place is before him, and their baggage place to his left. He, may Allah be pleased with him, struck the earth with his hand, took a handful of earth and smelled it, saying: Ah, how lovely the bloods shed in it. Then al-Husayn, may Allah be pleased with him, came and alighted at Karbala'. Al-Dabbi said: I was in the cavalry Ibn Ziyad sent to al-Husayn, may Allah be pleased with him. When I came, it was as if I looked at the stand of Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, gesturing with his hand. I turned my horse, then went to al-Husayn ibn Ali, may Allah be pleased with them both, greeted him and said to him: Your father, may Allah be pleased with him, was the most knowledgeable of people; I witnessed him at such-and-such time saying such-and-such, and by Allah you are surely to be killed now. He said: What do you intend to do, join us or join your family? I said: By Allah, I have debt upon me and a family, and I think I shall join my family. He said: In that case, take from this wealth your need, and it was wealth placed before him, before it becomes forbidden to you, then escape. By Allah, none hears the caller, none sees the lightning, none harms us but will be cursed upon the tongue of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). I said: By Allah, I shall not combine two matters today: taking your wealth and forsaking you. He left and abandoned him. The hadith of Zaynab bint Jahsh, may Allah be pleased with her, of his (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) foretelling the killing of al-Husayn, may Allah be pleased with him, passed in the book of purification, in the chapter of removing impurity, and part of it preceded in his merit in the virtues."
"5331","Abu Yala said: Muhammad ibn Uqbah narrated to us, Ali ibn Muhammad Abu Muhammad al-Qurashi narrated, Abu Abd al-Rahman al-Ghanawi narrated, from Abd al-Malik ibn Umayr who said: I saw the head of al-Husayn ibn Ali, may Allah be pleased with them both, brought to Ubaydullah ibn Ziyad; I saw the head of Ubaydullah ibn Ziyad brought to al-Mukhtar ibn Abi Ubayd; I saw the head of al-Mukhtar ibn Abi Ubayd brought to Mus'ab ibn al-Zubayr; and I saw the head of Mus'ab ibn al-Zubayr brought to Abd al-Malik ibn Marwan."
"5332","Chapter: The appointment by Mu'awiyah, may Allah be pleased with him, of his son Yazid as successor. Abu Yala said: al-Hasan ibn Umar ibn Shaqiq narrated to us, Ja'far narrated, from Hisham, from Muhammad ibn Sirin who said: When Mu'awiyah, may Allah be pleased with him, wanted to appoint Yazid as successor, he sent to the governor of Madinah to send to him whomever he wished. He said: Amr ibn Hazm al-Ansari came to him seeking permission, and the chamberlain of Mu'awiyah, may Allah be pleased with him, came seeking permission, saying: Here is Amr come seeking permission. He said: What brought them to me? He said: O Commander of the Believers, he seeks your favor. Mu'awiyah, may Allah be pleased with him, said: If he is truthful, let him write to me; I shall give him what he asks without seeing him. He said: The chamberlain went out to him saying: What is your need? Write whatever you wish. He said: Glory be to Allah, I come to the door of the Commander of the Believers and am veiled from him; I wish to meet him and speak to him. Mu'awiyah, may Allah be pleased with him, said to the chamberlain: Promise him such-and-such day; when he prays the morning prayer, let him come. He said: When Mu'awiyah, may Allah be pleased with him, prayed the morning prayer, he ordered his couch placed in the hall, then people were removed from him; none was with him but a chair placed for Amr. Amr, may Allah be pleased with him, came, sought permission, was permitted, greeted him, then sat upon the chair. Mu'awiyah, may Allah be pleased with him, said to him: Your need? He said: He praised Allah Almighty and extolled Him, then said: By my life, Yazid ibn Mu'awiyah, may Allah be pleased with him, has become the middle of lineage in Quraysh, rich beyond wealth, rich except of every good. I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Allah Almighty entrusts no servant with subjects but He will question him about them on the Day of Resurrection: How did he act? I remind you of Allah, O Mu'awiyah, concerning the ummah of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him): Whom will you appoint over it? He said: Mu'awiyah, may Allah be pleased with him, was seized with panting and breath on a cold morning until he sweated, wiping sweat from his face for a while, then recovered, praised Allah Almighty and extolled Him, then said: As for what follows, you are a sincere man; you spoke your opinion, however far it went. None remains but my son and their sons; my son is more deserving than their sons. Your need? He said: I have no need. He said: Stand. His brother said to him: We came from Madinah wearing out its livers for some words. He said: You came only for the words. He ordered their prizes for them, and ordered for Amr double thereof."
"5333","Chapter: The cursing by the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) of al-Hakam ibn Abi al-As, his sons, and Banu Umayyah. Abu Yala said: Muhammad ibn Uqbah narrated to us, Ja'far ibn Sulayman narrated, Shu'bah narrated, from Ali ibn al-Hakam, from Abu al-Hasan al-Jazari, from Amr ibn Murrah who said: al-Hakam ibn Abi al-As sought permission upon the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and he recognized his speech, saying: Admit him; may Allah curse him and all that exits from his loins except their believers, and few are they; extravagant in this world, abased in the Hereafter, people of plotting and deceit, given in this world with no share for them in the Hereafter."
"5334","Ishaq said: Sulayman ibn Harb informed us, from Hammad ibn Salamah, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from Abu Yahya who said: I was between al-Hasan and al-Husayn, may Allah be pleased with them both, while Marwan was reviling al-Husayn and al-Hasan was restraining al-Husayn, may Allah be pleased with them both, when Marwan became angry and said: A cursed household. Al-Hasan, may Allah be pleased with him, became angry and said: Did you say: A cursed household? By Allah, Allah cursed you while you were in your father's loins."
"5335","Abu Yala said: Ibrahim ibn al-Hajjaj narrated to us, Hammad narrated, with it."
"5336","Ishaq said: Jarir informed us, from Ata ibn al-Sa'ib, from Abu Yahya who said: One day I was with al-Hasan and al-Husayn, may Allah be pleased with them both, and Marwan reviled them with foul reviling, until he said: By Allah, you are a cursed household. Al-Hasan and al-Husayn, may Allah be pleased with them both, or one of them, said: By Allah, by Allah, then by Allah, Allah Almighty cursed you upon the tongue of His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while you were in the loins of al-Hakam. Marwan fell silent."
"5337","Abu Yala said: Abu Ma'mar narrated to us, Jarir narrated, with it."
"5338","Ishaq said: Isma'il ibn Ibrahim informed us, from Ibn Awn, from Umayr ibn Ishaq who said: Marwan was governor over us for years, reviling Ali, may Allah be pleased with him, every Friday upon the pulpit. Then Marwan was dismissed and Sa'id ibn al-As was appointed for years, and he would not revile him. Then Sa'id was dismissed and Marwan reappointed, and he reviled him. It was said to al-Hasan ibn Ali, may Allah be pleased with them both: Do you not hear what Marwan says, yet you return nothing? He would come on Friday, enter the chamber of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and stay in it; when the sermon ended he would go out to the mosque and pray in it, then return to his family. Marwan was not satisfied with that until he sent to him in his house. While we were sitting with him, it was said to him: So-and-so at the door. He permitted him, and he entered saying: I came to you from a ruler, and I came to you with an oath. He said: Speak. He said: Marwan sent with Ali and with Ali, and with you and with you, and I found no likeness for you but the mule: it is said to her: Who is your father? She says: My mother is the mare. He said: Return to him and say to him: By Allah, I shall not efface from you anything of what you said by reviling you; my appointment and yours is with Allah. If you are truthful, may Allah reward you for your truth, and if you are lying, Allah is severer in retribution. Allah Almighty honored my grandfather that my likeness be the mule's. Then he went out and met al-Husayn, may Allah be pleased with him, in the chamber, who asked him. He said: I was sent with a message and delivered it. He said: By Allah, you shall inform me of it, or I shall order you beaten until none knows when the beating of you ends. When al-Hasan, may Allah be pleased with him, saw him he said: Release him. He said: I cannot. He said: Why? He said: I swore. He said: Marwan sent with Ali and with Ali, and with you and with you, and I found no likeness for you but the mule: it is said to her: Who is your father? She says: My mother is the mare. Al-Husayn, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Bite your mother's vulva if you do not convey to him from me what I say to him. Say to him: With you, your father and your people, and the sign between me and you, that you hold your shoulders from the curse of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him)."
"5339","Al-Nadr ibn Shumayl informed us, Ibn Awn informed me, from Umayr ibn Ishaq, and he mentioned the like of it. He said in his hadith: Allah Almighty honored my grandfather that his likeness be the mule's. He said: The messenger went out and was met by al-Husayn, may Allah be pleased with him, who does not swerve from anything he wants, saying: Al-Husayn, may Allah be pleased with him, said: I have sworn. Al-Hasan, may Allah be pleased with him, said: Inform him, for when he persists in something he persists. He said: His word, may Allah be pleased with him, weighed heavily upon Marwan, meaning his saying: That you hold your shoulders, to its end."
"5340","Abd al-Razzaq informed us, Sufyan ibn Uyaynah narrated, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid and Mujalid, from al-Sha'bi who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) cursed al-Hakam and whoever exits from his loins."
"5341","Ahmad narrated it from Abd al-Razzaq, from Ibn Uyaynah, from Isma'il ibn Abi Khalid and Mujalid, from al-Sha'bi who said: I heard Ibn al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with him, leaning upon the Ka'bah, saying: By the Lord of this Ka'bah, then he mentioned it."
"5342","Ishaq said: Jarir informed us, from al-A'mash, from Salim ibn Abi al-Ja'd, from Ali ibn Alqamah, from Abdullah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: Everything has a blight, and the blight of this religion is Banu Umayyah."
"5343","Abu Bakr said: Hawdhah ibn Khalifah narrated to us, from Awf, from Abu Mijlaz, from Abu al-Aliyah who said: When Yazid ibn Abi Sufyan was governor in Sham, the Muslims raided, were safe and gained booty. Among their booty was a precious slave-girl, falling to a man of the Muslims. Yazid sent to him and seized her from him, while Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, was then in Sham. The man sought Abu Dharr's, may Allah be pleased with him, aid against Yazid. He went with him, saying to Yazid: Return his slave-girl to him. He hesitated three times. Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, said: By Allah, if you do, I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The first to alter my Sunnah is a man of Banu Umayyah. Then he turned from him. Yazid caught up with him, saying: I adjure you by Allah Almighty, am I he? He said: O Allah, no. And he returned the slave-girl to the man."
"5344","Abu Yala said: Muhammad ibn Isma'il ibn Abi Suminah narrated to us, Abd al-Wahhab narrated, from Awf, from al-Muhajir Abu Makhlad, from Abu al-Aliyah; Abu Muslim narrated, who said: Abu Dharr, may Allah be pleased with him, was in Sham in the time of Yazid ibn Abi Sufyan. The Muslims raided, gained booty and took a precious slave-girl, falling to a man of the Muslims in a share, then he mentioned the like of it."
"5345","Mus'ab ibn Abdullah narrated to us, Ibn Abi Hazim narrated, from al-Ala, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) saw in a dream as if Banu al-Hakam were leaping upon his pulpit. He woke as if angry, saying: Why do I see Banu al-Hakam leaping upon my pulpit like monkeys leap? He was never seen laughing after that until he died."
"5346","Yahya ibn Ayyub narrated to us, Isma'il, he is Ibn Ja'far, narrated, from al-Ala, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: When Banu Abi al-As reach thirty, Allah's religion will be deceit, Allah's wealth apportioned, and Allah's servants enslaved."
"5347","Ahmad ibn Mani' said: al-Haytham ibn Kharijah narrated. Abu Yala said: al-Hakam ibn Musa narrated, both saying: Yahya ibn Hamzah narrated, from Hisham ibn al-Ghaz, from Makhul, from Abu Ubaydah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: This affair will remain upright with justice until a man of Banu Umayyah breaches it. Its men are trustworthy except that it is disconnected."
"5348","Al-Harith and Abu Yala both said: al-Hakam ibn Musa narrated to us, al-Walid narrated, from al-Awza'i, from Makhul, from Abu Ubaydah, the like of it, adding: Called Yazid."
"5349","Chapter: The indication to al-Hajjaj, al-Mukhtar and others. Abu Yala said: Uthman ibn Abi Shaybah narrated to us, Muhammad ibn al-Hasan al-Asadi narrated, Sharik narrated, from Abu Ishaq, from Abdullah ibn al-Zubayr, may Allah be pleased with them both, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Hour will not rise until thirty liars emerge, among them Musaylimah, al-Ansi and al-Mukhtar; the worst Arab tribes are Banu Umayyah, Banu Hanifah and Thaqif."
"5350","Ahmad ibn Umar al-Waki'i narrated to us, Waki' narrated, Umm Ghurab narrated to us, from a woman called Aqilah, from Salamah bint al-Hurr, may Allah be pleased with her, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: In Thaqif is a destroyer."
"5351","Al-Harith said: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us, Hammad ibn Salamah narrated, from Ali ibn Zayd; one who heard Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to me, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A tyrant of the tyrants of Banu Umayyah will surely bleed upon this pulpit of mine. He said: One who saw Amr ibn Sa'id ibn al-As told me: He bled upon the pulpit of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) until blood flowed upon the pulpit steps."
"5352","Abu Yala said: Abu Bakr narrated to us, Ishaq ibn Sulayman narrated, from Hariz ibn Uthman, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Abi Awf, from al-Hasan ibn Ali, may Allah be pleased with them both, that he said to Abu al-A'war al-Sulami: Woe to you, did not the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) curse Ri'l, Dhakwan and Amr ibn Sufyan."
"5353","Chapter: The appearance of corruption in the end of time and the merit of enjoining good at that time. Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Yazid, he is Ibn Harun, narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ubaydullah al-Fazari narrated, Ubaydullah ibn Zahr narrated, from Ali ibn Yazid, from al-Qasim, from Abu Umamah, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Everything has advance and decline, and this religion has advance and decline. Of the advance of this religion is what Allah sent me with, until the tribe learns from its last to its first, leaving only one or two sinners, subdued, suppressed, humbled; if they speak or utter, they are suppressed, subdued and oppressed. Then he mentioned of the decline of this religion: that the whole tribe turns away from its last to its first, leaving in it only one or two jurists, subdued, suppressed, humbled; if they speak or utter, they are suppressed, subdued and oppressed, and it is said to them: Do you tyrannize over us? Until wine is drunk in their clubs of evil, gatherings and markets, wine given another name, until the last of this ummah curses its first, except curse befalls him. They say: No harm in this drink; a man of them drinks of it what he wishes, then refrains from it, until a woman passes and he rises to her, lifts her tail and copulates with her while they watch, as one lifts a ewe's tail. He lifted a garment upon him of these Sahuliyyah cloths, and the speaker among them says: Had you kept her from the road. That one among them is like Abu Bakr and Umar, may Allah be pleased with them both. Whoever reaches that time and enjoins good and forbids evil has the reward of fifty of those who accompanied me, believed in me and deemed me truthful. This is a weak hadith, with four in sequence."
"5354","Abu Yala said: Ghassan ibn al-Rabi' narrated to us, from Musa, from his father, from Anas, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said to us: Allah Almighty will establish you in the land, working in it what Allah wills you to work. When you work in it with sins, your enemy will be given power over you and return you to the land of the Arabs. He said: I said then: O Messenger of Allah, how will the land of the Arabs carry us, when you told us of the multitude of Muslims? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Allah will send down for you in it provision, as He sent down for Banu Isra'il when they wandered."
"5355","Al-Harith said: Muhammad ibn Ja'far al-Warkani narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated, from Aban ibn Abi Ayyash who said: Abu al-Jald narrated to me, from Ma'qil ibn Yasar al-Muzani, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Nights and days will not pass until the Qur'an wears out in the breasts of peoples of this ummah as garments wear out, and other than it becomes more pleasing to them. Their affair will be all greed, unmixed with fear. If one falls short of Allah Almighty's right, his soul tempts him with vain hopes; if he transgresses Allah Almighty's prohibition, he says: I hope Allah will overlook me. They wear sheep skins over wolves' hearts. The best of them in their own view is the flatterer. It was said: What is the flatterer? He said: One who neither enjoins nor forbids."
"5356","Chapter: The remaining of Islam until Allah's command comes. Abu Yala said: Dawud ibn Rushayd narrated to us, al-Walid ibn Muslim narrated, from Muhammad ibn Muhajir, from al-Walid ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Jurashi, from Jubayr ibn Nufayr, from al-Nawwas ibn Sam'an, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: A conquest was granted to the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and I came to him saying: O Messenger of Allah, horses have been let loose, weapons laid down, war has laid its burdens, and they said: No fighting. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Now fighting has come. Allah, Mighty and Majestic, will keep turning the hearts of peoples whom you fight; Allah provides from them until Allah's command comes upon that. The stronghold of the believers' abode is in Sham."
"5357","Abu Talib Abd al-Jabbar ibn Asim narrated to us, Isma'il ibn Ayyash al-Himsi Abu Utbah narrated, from al-Walid ibn Abbad, from Amir al-Ahwal, from Abu Salih al-Khawlani, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: A band of my ummah will keep fighting at the gates of Damascus and around it, and at the gates of Bayt al-Maqdis and around it; those who forsake them will not harm them. Ibn Hamdan added: The forsaking of whoever forsakes them will not harm them, victorious upon truth until the Hour rises."
"5358","Tammam said in his Fawa'id: Ahmad ibn Sulayman ibn Ayyub ibn Khadhlam narrated to us, my father narrated, Sulayman ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated, Ibn Ayyash narrated, al-Walid ibn Abbad narrated, with it. Ibn Adi brought it in al-Kamil from Ja'far ibn Abi Asim, from Hisham ibn Ammar, from Isma'il ibn Ayyash, saying: None narrates it with this wording but Isma'il ibn Ayyash from al-Walid. I said: Abu Ali Abd al-Jabbar narrated it in the history of Darayya from Ahmad ibn Sulayman ibn Ayyub ibn Khadhlam, except he inverted its chain, making it from al-Walid ibn Abbad, from Asim al-Ahwal, from Abu Muslim al-Khawlani; the correct is: Amir al-Ahwal from Abu Salih. Ahmad brought it from the path of al-Qa'qa', from Abu Salih Dhakwan, from Abu Hurayrah, may Allah be pleased with him, abridged, with wording: A band of this affair will remain upon truth; the opposition of whoever forsakes them will not harm them, until Allah Almighty's command comes."
"5359","Musaddad said: Khalid narrated to us, Ata ibn al-Sa'ib narrated, who said: I heard Abd al-Rahman al-Hadrami in the days of Ibn al-Ash'ath orating, saying: O people of Sham, rejoice, for so-and-so informed me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There will be a people of the last of my ummah given of reward like what its first is given, fighting the people of fitnah, denouncing evil, and you are they. Abu al-Bakhtari said to him: Your backside has missed the pit."
"5360","Chapter: The prohibition of fighting the Turks, for fear of their overpowering Islamic lands. Abu Yala said: Muhammad ibn Yahya al-Basri narrated to us, Muhammad ibn Ya'qub narrated, who said: Ahmad ibn Ibrahim narrated to me, who said: Ishaq ibn Ibrahim ibn al-Ghimr, the freedman of Sumuk, narrated to me, who said: My father narrated to me, from my grandfather, who said: I heard Mu'awiyah ibn Hudayj say: I was with Mu'awiyah ibn Abi Sufyan, may Allah be pleased with him, when his governor's letter reached him informing him that he had fallen upon the Turks, routed them, many killed of them and much booty. Mu'awiyah, may Allah be pleased with him, became angry at that, then ordered written to him: I understood what you mentioned of killing and booty; I shall surely know if you return to any of that or fight them until my command reaches you. I said to him: Why, O Commander of the Believers? He said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The Turks will expel the Arabs until they join them to the growths of wormwood and qaysum. I dislike fighting them for that."
"5361","Chapter: The permissibility of leaving forbidding evil for whoever cannot bear it. Al-Harith said: al-Khalil ibn Zakariyya narrated to us, Habib ibn al-Shahid narrated, al-Hasan ibn Abi al-Hasan narrated, who said: A man stood to him saying: O Abu Sa'id, al-Hajjaj has delayed prayer on Friday until we neared Asr. Stand to him and command him to fear Allah Almighty. Al-Hasan said: Then he will kill me. The man said to him: Did not Allah, Mighty and Majestic, say: They did not forbid one another the evil they did, to the end of the verse? Al-Hasan said: Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, narrated to me that the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The believer may not humiliate himself. They said: How does he humiliate himself, O Messenger of Allah? He said: He undertakes of tribulation what he cannot bear. Its narrators are trustworthy except al-Khalil. It is with Abu Yala in a long story from the path of al-Mu'alla ibn Ziyad al-Qardusi, from al-Hasan al-Basri, that he narrated two hadiths: one from Abu Sa'id, may Allah be pleased with him, and the second he said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said, and he mentioned the text here."
"5362","Abu Yala said: Qatan ibn Nusayr narrated to us, Ja'far ibn Sulayman narrated, al-Mu'alla ibn Ziyad narrated, who said: When Yazid ibn al-Muhallab routed the people of Basra, I feared sitting in al-Hasan's circle, being found and recognized there. I came to al-Hasan in his house and entered upon him, saying: O Abu Sa'id, what of this verse? He said: Which verse? He said: I said: His saying, Mighty and Majestic: They did not forbid one another the evil they did, to the end of the verse. He said: O servant of Allah, the people presented the sword, and the sword barred speech. I said: O Abu Sa'id, do you know any merit for a speaker? He said: No. Al-Mu'alla said: Then he narrated two hadiths. He said: Abu Sa'id narrated to us, from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) who said: Let not fear of people prevent one of you from speaking truth when he sees it, for it neither brings near a term nor distances provision. He said: Then al-Hasan narrated another hadith, saying: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The believer may not humiliate himself. It was said: What is his humiliating himself? He said: He exposes himself to tribulation he cannot bear. It was said: O Abu Sa'id, what of Yazid al-Dabbi in his speech about prayer? He said: He did not leave prison until he regretted. Al-Mu'alla said: I rose from al-Hasan's gathering and came to Yazid al-Dabbi, saying: O Abu Mawdud, while I and al-Hasan were conferring, you set your affair plainly. He said: Hush, O Abu al-Hasan. I said: I did. He said: What did he say? He said: He did not leave prison until he regretted his statement. Yazid said: I did not regret my statement. By Allah, I stood a stand risking myself in it. Yazid said: I came to al-Hasan and said: O Abu Sa'id, are we overcome in everything, overcome even in our prayer? He said: O servant of Allah, you expose yourself to them, then you accomplish nothing. He said: Then I came to him and he said to me the like of his statement. I stood on Friday in the mosque while al-Hakam ibn Ayyub was orating, saying: Prayer, may Allah have mercy on you. When I said that, men surrounded me, seizing my beard, head and collar, striking my belly with their sword hilts, and took me toward the enclosure. I entered and stood between al-Hakam, he silent, saying: Are you mad? Were we not in prayer? I said: May Allah set right the commander, is any speech better than the Book of Allah, Mighty and Majestic? He said: No. I said: What if a man spread a mushaf reciting it morning to night, would that suffice him for his prayer? He said: By Allah, I think you mad. He said: Anas ibn Malik was sitting beneath his pulpit silent. I said: O Anas, O Abu Hamzah, I adjure you by Allah Almighty, having served the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) and accompanied him: did I speak good or evil? Did I speak truth or falsehood? He said: By Allah, he did not answer me a word. Al-Hakam ibn Ayyub said to him: O Anas. He said: At your service, may Allah set you right. He said: Had the prayer time gone? He said: Rather a remnant remained. Al-Hakam said: Imprison him. Yazid said: I swear to you, O Abu al-Hasan, what I met from my companions was harder upon me than what I met from al-Hakam. Some said: A show-off; some said: Mad. He said: Al-Hakam wrote to al-Hajjaj that a man of Banu Dabbah stood to me on Friday while I was orating, saying: Prayer, and the just witnesses testified with me that he is mad. Al-Hajjaj wrote to him: If the just witnesses testify he is mad, release him; otherwise cut his hands and feet, blind his eyes and crucify him. He said: They testified with al-Hakam that I am mad, so he released me. Al-Mu'alla said: From Yazid al-Dabbi, then a brother of ours died and we followed his bier, prayed over him, and when buried I withdrew with a band, remembering Allah Almighty, Mighty and Majestic, and remembering our return. While thus, we saw horses' forelocks and spears. When my companions saw him they scattered, leaving me alone. Al-Hakam came until he stood over me, saying: What were you doing? I said: May Allah set right the commander, a companion of ours died; we prayed over him, buried him and sat remembering our Lord, our return and what he came to. He said: What prevented you fleeing as they fled? I said: May Allah set you right, I am innocent of that; shall I flee from the commander? Al-Hakam fell silent. Abd al-Malik ibn al-Muhallab, over his police, said to him: Do you know who this is? He said: No. He said: This is the speaker of Friday. Al-Hakam became angry, saying: You are bold indeed. Seize him. I was seized and he struck me four hundred lashes; I knew not when he left me from the severity of his beating. He said: He sent me to Wasit, and I was in al-Hajjaj's prison until al-Hajjaj died. Al-Tirmidhi and others brought it from the path of Ali ibn Zayd, from al-Hasan al-Basri, from Jundub ibn Abdullah al-Bajali, from Hudhayfah, may Allah be pleased with him, like the hadith of al-Hasan from Abu Bakrah, may Allah be pleased with him, and it is likelier correct; Ali ibn Zayd is better in condition than al-Khalil ibn Zakariyya, and Allah knows best."
"5363","Abu Yala said: Ahmad ibn al-Miqdam narrated to us, Ubayd ibn al-Qasim narrated, al-Ala ibn Tha'labah narrated, from Abu al-Malih al-Hudhali, from Wathilah ibn al-Asqa', may Allah be pleased with him, who said: I saw the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) at the Khayf mosque, and his companions said to me: To you, O Wathilah, meaning: move from before him. He said: Leave him, for he came only to ask. I drew near and said: May my father and mother be ransom for you, O Messenger of Allah, give us a ruling on a matter we take from you after you. He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Let your soul give you ruling. I said: How can I do that? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Leave what doubts you for what does not doubt you, even if muftis rule for you. I said: How shall I know that? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Place your hand upon your heart, for the heart settles to the lawful and does not settle to the unlawful. The scrupulousness of a Muslim is to leave the small fearing falling into the major. I said: Who is the greedy? He said: One who seeks earning in unlawful ways. I said: Who is the scrupulous? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: One who stops at doubt. I said: Who is the believer? He said: One whom people trust over their wealth and blood. I said: Who is the Muslim? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: One from whose tongue and hand Muslims are safe. I said: Which jihad is best? He (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: A word of truth before an unjust ruler. Al-Ala ibn Tha'labah, Abu Hatim said: Unknown. Its end has a witness from the hadith of Abu Abdah, may Allah be pleased with him, brought by al-Bazzar."
"5364","Chapter: The indication to the non-Arabs' dominance over Islamic kingdoms and the departure of worldly adornment with the humiliation of the Arabs. Abu Yala said: Abu Kurayb narrated to us, Ibn Abi Fudayk narrated, from Abd al-Malik ibn Zayd, from Mus'ab ibn Mus'ab, from Ibn Shihab, from Abu Salamah ibn Abd al-Rahman, from his father, may Allah be pleased with him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The adornment of this world will be lifted in the year one hundred twenty-five."
"5365","Kamil ibn Talhah narrated to us, Ibn Lahi'ah narrated, from Yazid ibn Abi Habib, from Hudayj ibn Abi Amr who said: I heard al-Mustawrid ibn Shaddad, may Allah be pleased with him, say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: Every ummah has a term, and my ummah's term is one hundred years. When one hundred years pass over my ummah, what Allah Almighty promised it will come to it."
"5366","Abu Bakr said: Humayd ibn Abd al-Rahman narrated to us, from Bashir ibn al-Muhajir, from Abdullah ibn Buraydah, from his father (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Within a hundred years Allah the Exalted will send a cool pleasant wind in which He will take the soul of every believer."
"5367","Abu Ya'la said: Abu Bakr narrated to us, with this same report."
"5368","Sufyan ibn Waki' narrated to us: my father narrated to us: Bashir ibn al-Muhajir narrated to us, similar to it."
"5369","Al-Ruyan said: al-Hasan ibn Ibrahim narrated to us: Ubaydullah ibn Musa narrated to us: Bashir narrated to us, then he mentioned it. Its chain is hasan."
"5370","Chapter 38: On the Mahdi and other just caliphs. 4484 - Musaddad said: Yahya narrated to us, from Abu Yunus: Abu Bahr narrated to us that Abu al-Jald told him, swearing to it: this ummah will not perish until there are twelve caliphs among them, all acting upon guidance and the religion of truth; among them two men from the household of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), one of whom will live forty years and the other thirty years; and there will be caliphs after them who are not of them."
"5371","4485/1 - Al-Harith said: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar ibn Qahdham narrated to us: my father narrated to me, from his father Qahdham ibn Sulayman, from Mu'awiyah ibn Qurrah, from his father (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The earth will be filled with wrongdoing and injustice, and when it is filled with wrongdoing and injustice Allah the Exalted will send a man from me, his name my name, or the name of a prophet; he will fill it with equity and justice; the sky will withhold nothing of its rain nor the earth of its vegetation; he will remain among them seven or eight, and if more then nine, meaning years."
"5372","4485/2 - Al-Bazzar said: Isma'il ibn Abi al-Harith and Ahmad ibn Yahya al-Susi narrated to us, both saying: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us, and he mentioned it; and he said: Ma'mar narrated it from Abu Harun, from Mu'awiyah ibn Qurrah, from Abu al-Siddiq, from Abu Sa'id (may Allah be pleased with him)."
"5373","4486 - Abu Ya'la said: Abu Bakr ibn Abi al-Nadr narrated to us: Abu al-Nadr narrated to us: al-Murajja ibn Raja' al-Yashkuri narrated to me, from Isa ibn Hilal, from Bashir ibn Nahik, who said: I heard Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) say: my dear friend Abu al-Qasim (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) told me, he said: The Hour will not come until a man from my household comes forth against them and strikes them until they return to the truth. He said: I said: how long will he rule? He said: five and two. I said: what is five and two? He said: I do not know."
"5374","Chapter 39: The signs before the Hour, like the Beast and the rising of the sun from its setting place. 4487/1 - Al-Tayalisi said: Talhah ibn Amr and Jarir ibn Hazim narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Ubayd ibn Umayr; Talhah said: Abdullah ibn Ubaydullah told me that Abu al-Tufayl told him, from Hudhayfah ibn Asid al-Ghifari Abu Surayhah; as for Jarir, he said: from Abdullah ibn Ubayd, from a man of the family of Ibn Mas'ud; and the hadith of Talhah is the most complete and best. He said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) mentioned the Beast and said: It will emerge three times in history: it will emerge in the far desert and its mention will not enter the town, meaning Makkah; then it will hide for a long time; then it will emerge another emergence less than that, and its mention will spread in the desert and enter the town, meaning Makkah. The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Then while people are in the greatest mosque in sanctity before Allah, the best and most honored, the Sacred Mosque, nothing will startle them but it bellowing between the Corner and the Station, shaking dust from its head; people will scatter from it separately and together, and a group of believers will stand firm, knowing they cannot escape Allah the Exalted. It will begin with them and brighten their faces until they become like brilliant stars; it will roam the earth, no seeker overtaking it and no fleer escaping it, until a man will seek refuge from it in prayer, and it will come to him from behind and say: now, O so-and-so, you pray; he will turn to it and it will mark him on his face, then go on. People will share in wealth and mix in the cities, the believer distinguished from the disbeliever, until the believer says: O disbeliever, pay me my right, and the disbeliever says: O believer, pay me my right. 4487/2 - Al-Hakim brought it through al-Abqari from Talhah alone in full. Talhah is weak."
"5375","4488 - Abu Ya'la said: Wasil ibn Abd al-A'la narrated to us: Ibn Fudayl narrated to us, from Layth, that is Ibn Abi Sulaym, from Sa'id ibn Amir, from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with them both), that he said: Shall I show you the place from which the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said the Beast of the earth will emerge? Then he struck with his staff the cleft in al-Safa and said: It has feathers and down; a third of it will emerge with the speed of a swift horse for three days and three nights; it will pass by them and they will flee from it to the mosques, and it will say to them: do you think the mosques will save you from me? Then it will muzzle them, and they will be driven in the markets, saying: O believer! O disbeliever!"
"5376","4489 - Al-Harith said: Isma'il ibn Abi Isma'il narrated to us: Isma'il ibn Ayyash narrated to us, from al-Kalbi, from Abu Salih, from Ibn Abbas (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: The Hour will not come until two old men meet and one says to his companion: when were you born? He will say: on the day the sun rose from its setting place."
"5377","4490 - Abu Ya'la said: Abdullah ibn Umar ibn Aban narrated to us: Ibn Fudayl narrated to us, from Sulayman ibn Abi Idam, who said: I heard Ibn Abi Awfa (may Allah be pleased with him) say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: There will come the like of three nights of these nights of yours; when the night worshippers recognize it, a man will stand and recite his portion, then sleep, then stand and recite his portion, then sleep, then stand and recite his portion; while they are so, people will stir among one another saying: what is this? They will rush to the mosques, and behold the sun has risen from here, from its setting place; it will come until when it reaches the middle of the sky it will return; that is: No soul will benefit from its faith, the verse."
"5378","4491 - Abu Bakr said: Mu'awiyah ibn Hisham narrated to us: Sharik narrated to us, from Uthman ibn Abi Zur'ah, from Abu Sadiq, from Abd al-Rahman ibn Yazid, from Abdullah ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Paradise has eight gates, seven closed and one gate open until the sun rises from its setting place."
"5379","Chapter 40: The first of the nations to perish. The hadith of Umar (may Allah be pleased with him) in the chapter of hunting and slaughtered animals has preceded: the first thing to perish of these nations is locusts. 4492 - Musaddad said: Awn ibn Musa narrated to us, from Hilal ibn Khabbab, who said: I asked Sa'id (may Allah be pleased with him): what is the sign of people's ruin? He said: when their scholars perish."
"5380","Chapter 41: Portents and signs of the Hour. 4493/1 - Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn Abdullah ibn Ammar narrated to us: al-Mu'afa ibn Imran narrated to us: Ibn Lahi'ah narrated to us: Humayd ibn Hani' narrated to me, from Shufayy, from Abdullah ibn Amr (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There will come upon people a time when their hearts are the hearts of non-Arabs. It was said: what are the hearts of non-Arabs? He said: love of this world; their way the way of bedouins; whatever provision Allah gives them they put into livestock; they consider jihad a burden and charity a fine."
"5381","4493/2 - Al-Harith said: al-Muqri' narrated to me: Sa'id ibn Abi Ayyub narrated to us: Ibn Hani' narrated to me: Shufayy narrated to me, from Abdullah ibn Amr (may Allah be pleased with them both), his saying, with this. I said: and this is more correct."
"5382","4494 - Al-Tayalisi said: Musa ibn Mutayr narrated to us, from his father, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Hour will not come until people of my ummah return to idols they used to worship besides Allah the Mighty and Majestic."
"5383","4495 - Al-Harith said: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from al-Kalbi, from Salamah ibn al-Sa'ib, from Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Hour will not come until the Arabs worship what their fathers used to worship, for a hundred and fifty years."
"5384","4496 - Abu Dawud said: Isma'il ibn Ja'far narrated to us, from Amr ibn Abi Amr, from al-Muttalib, from Hudhayfah (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Hour will not come until you fight your leader and clash with your swords, and the worst of you inherit your world."
"5385","4497/1 - Ishaq said: Abdullah ibn Idris told us, saying: I heard Husayn ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Sulami narrate from Abd al-A'la ibn al-Hakam, from Kharijah ibn al-Salt al-Burjumi, who said: We went out with Abdullah (may Allah be pleased with him) from his house while the imam was bowing; we bowed, then walked until we joined the row; a man passed and said: peace be upon you, O Abu Abd al-Rahman; he said: Allah is Most Great, Allah and His Messenger spoke the truth. When we finished the prayer we said: O Abu Abd al-Rahman, it seemed the man's greeting startled you. He said: yes; it used to be said: among the portents of the Hour are that mosques be taken as pathways, that a man greet a man only from acquaintance, that a woman and a man trade together, and that women and horses both become dear, then become cheap and never become dear again."
"5386","4497/2 - Al-Nadr told us: Shu'bah told us, from Husayn, with this chain, the like of it."
"5387","4497/3 - Al-Tayalisi said: Shu'bah narrated to us, from Husayn, similar to it."
"5388","4497/4 - Ahmad ibn Mani' said: Hushaym narrated to us: Husayn narrated to us, similar to it."
"5389","4497/5 - Al-Harith said: Dawud narrated to us: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Abu Hamzah, from Ibrahim, from Alqamah, who said: Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) was between me and Masruq; a bedouin passed and said: peace be upon you, O son of Umm Abd; Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him) laughed and was asked, and he mentioned it similar to it."
"5390","4497/6 - Abu Ya'la said: Muhammad ibn al-Khattab narrated to us: Mu'ammal narrated to us: Sufyan narrated to us, from Husayn, similar to it. Ahmad narrated it from the hadith of al-Aswad ibn Yazid, from Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him), with its meaning in brief, and by way of Tariq, from Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him), with its meaning as well."
"5391","4498 - Ishaq said: al-Nadr ibn Shumayl told us: Ibn Awn narrated to us, from Ibn Sirin, from Ibn Mas'ud (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: All that Allah and His Messenger promised us we have seen except four: the rising of the sun from its setting place, the Beast of the earth, the Dajjal, and the emergence of Gog and Magog. In it there is a break."
"5392","4499 - Ishaq said: Isa ibn Yunus told us: Musa ibn Ubaydah al-Rabadhi narrated to us, from Iyas ibn Salamah ibn al-Akwa', from his father (may Allah be pleased with him), from the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), who said: The stars are a safety for the people of the sky, and my household are a safety for my ummah. This is a weak chain."
"5393","4500 - Al-Mulla'i told us: al-Walid, that is Ibn Jumay', narrated to us: Abu Bakr ibn Abi al-Jahm al-Qurashi narrated to me, saying: I came forth, I and Zayd ibn al-Hasan and Ibn Rummanah the freedman of Abd al-Aziz ibn Marwan, having held out our hands to him while he leaned on us, and we entered the mosque of the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) in which was Ibn Niyar, a man of the Companions of the Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him); he sent to Abu Bakr (may Allah be pleased with him): come to me; he came to him and said: I saw you two, with Ibn Rummanah between you leaning on you and on Zayd ibn al-Hasan; I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: The Hour will not come until this world is in the hands of Luka' son of Luka'."
"5394","4501 - Al-Humaydi said: Sufyan narrated to us: Muhammad ibn Ishaq told us that he heard Muhammad ibn Ibrahim al-Taymi narrate from Buqayrah, wife of al-Qa'qa' ibn Abi Hadrad al-Aslami (may Allah be pleased with her), who said: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) on the pulpit say: O people! When you hear of an army near by swallowed by the earth, the Hour has cast its shadow over you."
"5395","4502 - Al-Harith said: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Aban ibn Abi Ayyash, from Ja'far ibn Zayd, from Abu Zayd al-Ansari (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There will come upon this ummah a day when they enter evening asking: who was swallowed tonight? Just as they ask: who remains of the family of so-and-so, and who remains of the family of so-and-so?"
"5396","4503 - He said: Dawud ibn al-Muhabbar narrated to us: Hammad ibn Salamah narrated to us, from Yahya ibn Sa'id, from a shaykh who told him, who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: There will be a swallowing in the east. It was said: a swallowing in a land with Muslims in it? He said: yes, when most of their deeds are wrongdoing."
"5397","4504 - Abu Ya'la said: Ibrahim ibn al-Hajjaj narrated to us: Abd al-Wahid narrated to us: Uthman ibn Hakim narrated to us, from Abu Umamah ibn Sahl, from Abdullah ibn Amr (may Allah be pleased with them both), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: The Hour will not come until people copulate in the roads like asses copulate."
"5398","4505 - Ahmad ibn Isa narrated to us: Ibn Wahb narrated to us, from Abu Sakhr, saying: Sa'id al-Maqburi told him that he heard Abu Hurayrah (may Allah be pleased with him) say: I heard the Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) say: By the One in whose hand is the soul of Abu al-Qasim (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), Jesus son of Mary will descend as a just judge, break the cross, kill the swine, abolish the jizyah, and wealth will overflow until no one accepts it, and a single prostration will be better than the world and what is in it."
"5399","4506 - Ibrahim ibn Sa'id narrated to us: Rayhan ibn Sa'id narrated to us: Abbad ibn Mansur narrated to us, from Ayyub, from Abu Qilabah, from Anas (may Allah be pleased with him), who said: The Messenger of Allah (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) said: Men of my ummah will surely meet Jesus son of Mary (peace and prayer be upon him) and believe in him and accompany him."
"5400","4507/1 - Mu'adh ibn al-Muthanna, narrator of the Musnad of Musaddad, in what he added to it, said: al-Hasan ibn Abi Shu'ayb narrated to us: Uthman ibn Abd al-Rahman al-Harrani narrated to us: Muntasir ibn Dinar narrated to us, from Abdullah ibn Abi al-Hudhayl, who said: Sa'd ibn Abi Waqqas (may Allah be pleased with him) sent Nadlah ibn Amr al-Ansari (may Allah be pleased with him) with three hundred of the Emigrants and Helpers; they raided Hulwan and conquered it, taking much booty and many captives; they came driving what they had, between two mountains, until the afternoon pressed them; Nadlah (may Allah be pleased with him) said to them: turn to the foot of the mountain; they did; then Nadlah stood and called the adhan: Allah is Most Great, Allah is Most Great; a voice from the mountain answered him, with no form seen: you have magnified greatly, O Nadlah; he said: I bear witness there is no god but Allah; it said: you have been sincere, O Nadlah, in sincerity; he said: I bear witness Muhammad is the Messenger of Allah; it said: a prophet sent, no prophet after him; he said: come to prayer; it said: an ordained duty; he said: come to success; it said: he succeeds who comes to it and keeps to it; he said: the prayer has begun; it said: endurance belongs to the ummah of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him), and the Hour will come upon their leaders. When they prayed, Nadlah stood and said: O fine good beautiful speech, we have heard fine speech; are you one of Allah's angels, or a visitor, or a dweller? Come out to us and speak to us, for we are the delegation of Allah the Mighty and Majestic and the delegation of His Prophet (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him). An old man came out to him from a mountain pass, white of head and beard, with a head like a millstone, long-bearded, in two white woolen garments, and said: peace and Allah's mercy be upon you; they returned the greeting; Nadlah said to him: who are you, may Allah have mercy on you? He said: I am Zarnab ibn Tharmla, the legatee of the righteous servant Jesus son of Mary; he prayed for me to remain until his descent from heaven, so my abode is in this mountain; convey greetings to Umar ibn al-Khattab, Commander of the Believers (may Allah be pleased with him), and say to him: be firm, right and moderate, for the matter has drawn near; and beware, O Umar, if traits appear in the ummah of Muhammad (peace and blessings of Allah be upon him) while you are among them, then flight, flight. Nadlah said: O Zarnab, may Allah have mercy on you, tell us of these traits by which we may know the passing of our world and the coming of our hereafter. He said: when your men suffice themselves with your men, and your women with your women, and your food increases yet your prices only rise, and your rule is in your children, and the preachers of your pulpits are your slaves, and your jurists lean to your rulers, permitting them the unlawful and forbidding them the lawful, and giving them rulings as they desire, and they take the Qur'an as tunes and pipes with their voices, and you decorate your mosques and lengthen your minarets, and gild your copies of the Qur'an with gold and silver, and your women ride saddles, and your commander's counselor is your eunuchs, and the innocent is killed to admonish the public, and rain remains as drought-heat, and the child as vexation, and you are denied stipends while slaves and lowly take them, and charity lessens until a pauper goes round from year to year and is not given ten dirhams; when it is so, disgrace and trial will descend on you. Then the form went away and was not seen; they called but were not answered. When Nadlah came to Sa'd (may Allah be pleased with him) he told him of what Allah had granted him and of Zarnab's affair; Sa'd wrote to Umar ibn al-Khattab (may Allah be pleased with him) informing him; Umar wrote: May your father be devoted to Allah, O Sa'd! Ride yourself to the mountain. Sa'd rode until he came to the mountain and called forty mornings but was not answered; he wrote to Umar, and they left. This is a stopped report, strange by this way; I have not seen it at length except by this chain. 4507/2 - Abd al-Rahman ibn Ibrahim al-Rassi narrated it from Malik, from Nafi', from Ibn Umar (may Allah be pleased with him), and mentioned it, but not at length, naming the commander Nadlah ibn Mu'awiyah; al-Daraqutni brought it in the Gharib narrations of Malik by his way. It came to us by another chain naming Ja'wanah ibn Nadlah, and Allah knows best; I explained his affair in the biography of Ja'wanah under the letter jim of my book on the Companions (may Allah be pleased with them)."
